__d(function(e,t,o,a,s,n,i){s.exports={topic:{id:"business",name:"Business",emoji:"\ud83d\ude80",color:"#7A4A1E"},books:[{id:"the-lean-startup",title:"The Lean Startup",author:"Eric Ries",kind:"book",year:2011,description:"How to build companies under extreme uncertainty by learning fast from real customers.",ideas:[["Build, measure, learn","Most failed startups don't fail because they couldn't build their product; they fail because they built something nobody wanted. Eric Ries suggests treating every product idea as an experiment. Build the smallest version that tests your assumption, measure how real people actually behave and learn whether to continue or change direction. Then repeat, faster each time. The goal is to shorten the loop from idea to evidence. Today: write down one assumption about your project, such as \"people will pay for this\", and think of the cheapest way to test it this week."],["The minimum viable product","A minimum viable product isn't a cheap version of your dream; it's the quickest way to start learning. Sometimes it's not even a real product. Dropbox famously tested demand with a simple video showing how the service would work, before building most of it. Others have run a service by hand behind a website to see if customers would use it. Today: imagine the simplest possible version of your idea that would tell you whether people want it, and sketch how you could build it in a week."],["Pivot or persevere","At regular points, Ries recommends an honest meeting with a hard question: is this working, or should we change a fundamental part of the strategy? A pivot keeps what you've learned but changes direction, such as a new customer, feature or business model. Many famous companies, from YouTube to Slack, pivoted from very different starting ideas. Persevering blindly wastes years; pivoting too often wastes focus. Today: set a date to review a project you're working on, and decide in advance what evidence would make you change course."],["Vanity metrics","Total sign-ups, page views and downloads feel great on a dashboard, but they can rise while a business quietly fails. Ries calls them vanity metrics. More useful are numbers that reflect real behaviour: how many users come back next week, how many pay, how many recommend you. Good metrics help you make decisions; vanity metrics just make you feel good. Today: look at the numbers you track for a project or goal, and ask which ones actually change what you'd do next."],["Validated learning","In a startup, progress isn't measured by lines of code written or features shipped. It's measured by what you've learned about customers, backed by evidence. Ries calls this validated learning. A week that proves an idea wrong is a productive week, because it saves months of building in the wrong direction. Today: at the end of your week, write down one thing you've genuinely learned about your customers or audience, not just what you've done."]]},{id:"zero-to-one",title:"Zero to One",author:"Peter Thiel & Blake Masters",kind:"book",year:2014,description:"Notes on startups and how to build companies that create something genuinely new.",ideas:[["Zero to one versus one to n","Copying what already works, opening another caf\xe9 or launching another similar app, takes the world from one to n: more of the same. Creating something genuinely new takes it from zero to one. Peter Thiel argues that the biggest value comes from the latter, because new things can't be competed away easily. Not every project needs to be revolutionary, but it's worth asking whether you're adding something new or just more. Today: think about your work and ask what you're doing that no one else is."],["The contrarian question","Thiel likes to ask job candidates a hard question: what important truth do very few people agree with you on? It's difficult because it requires both original thinking and the courage to say something unpopular. Great businesses are often built on such secrets, like insights others have missed or dismissed. Today: spend ten minutes trying to answer Thiel's question for yourself. Even if you don't find an answer, the search sharpens your thinking."],["Start small and monopolize","Starting by competing in a huge, crowded market is a fast way to make no profit. Thiel recommends dominating a small, specific market first, then expanding. Amazon began with books; Facebook began at a single university. Owning a niche gives you a base of loyal customers, cash and lessons before you take on bigger challenges. Today: if you're building something, define the smallest group of people you could serve brilliantly, and focus on them."],["Competition erodes profit","In perfect competition, where many similar firms sell the same thing, profits get competed down to almost nothing. Thiel argues that lasting businesses have something unique that protects them, such as proprietary technology, strong network effects, economies of scale or a powerful brand. Competition feels virtuous, but it often traps companies in a race to the bottom. Today: ask what makes your work hard to copy, and how you could make that advantage stronger."]]},{id:"good-to-great",title:"Good to Great",author:"Jim Collins",kind:"book",year:2001,description:"A research study into why some companies make the leap to lasting greatness and others don't.",ideas:[["First who, then what","Jim Collins studied companies that went from good to truly great and found that their leaders first got the right people on the bus, and the wrong people off, before deciding where to drive it. With the right people, the direction becomes easier to choose and to change. With the wrong people, even a brilliant strategy fails. Today: think about a team you're part of and ask who you'd want with you on any journey, regardless of the destination."],["Level 5 leadership","The leaders behind the great companies in Collins' study surprised him. They weren't flashy, celebrity CEOs. They combined deep personal humility with intense professional will. They gave credit to others when things went well and took responsibility when things went badly. Collins called this Level 5 leadership. Today: notice how you talk about successes and failures. Try crediting others for one win and owning one setback yourself."],["The hedgehog concept","Collins describes the hedgehog concept: the intersection of three circles. What can you be the best in the world at? What drives your economic engine? What are you deeply passionate about? Great companies focused relentlessly on where those circles overlapped, ignoring tempting distractions. Individuals can use the same lens for careers. Today: draw three circles and fill them in for yourself. Where do they overlap?"],["The flywheel","Great companies rarely transform in one dramatic moment. Collins compares their progress to pushing a giant, heavy flywheel. At first it barely moves, but consistent pushes in the same direction build momentum until it spins on its own. From the outside it looks like a sudden breakthrough; from the inside it was years of steady effort. Today: identify one consistent push you can make toward a goal every day this week, and keep pushing even if nothing seems to move."],["Confront the brutal facts","The great companies in Collins' study shared a paradox: they kept unwavering faith that they would succeed in the end, while honestly confronting the harshest facts of their current reality. He named this the Stockdale Paradox after a prisoner of war who survived years of captivity. Optimism without honesty leads to disappointment; honesty without faith leads to despair. Today: face one uncomfortable fact about a goal of yours, and remind yourself why you'll still get there."]]},{id:"the-innovators-dilemma",title:"The Innovator's Dilemma",author:"Clayton M. Christensen",kind:"book",year:1997,description:"Why well-run companies can do everything right and still be overtaken by new technologies.",ideas:[["Disruptive innovation","Clayton Christensen noticed a strange pattern: well-run companies, doing everything right, were often overtaken by newcomers with simpler, cheaper products. These disruptors start with customers the big players ignore, people who don't need the best and most expensive option, then steadily improve until they challenge the main market. Digital cameras, cheap steel and budget airlines followed this path. Today: think about your industry and ask what simpler, cheaper alternative might be quietly improving at the edges."],["Listening to customers can mislead","Big companies listen carefully to their best customers, who ask for more features and higher performance. That sounds smart, but Christensen showed it can be a trap. It pushes companies upmarket, leaving the low end open to newcomers with \"good enough\" products. By the time the threat is obvious, it's too late. Today: ask whether you're only listening to your most demanding customers, and what you might be missing from those who want something simpler."],["Small markets don't excite big companies","A new market worth a few million pounds is exciting for a startup but meaningless to a giant company with billions in revenue. So big firms often ignore emerging opportunities until they're too large to catch. Christensen called this one of the core reasons incumbents fail. Today: look at a small, unglamorous opportunity you've been dismissing, and ask what it might become in ten years."],["Create a separate unit","Christensen found that incumbents who successfully handled disruption often did it by creating a separate unit, with its own goals, cost structure and culture, free from the pressures of the main business. Inside the main company, the new idea would be judged by the wrong standards and starved of resources. Today: if you're trying something new within an established structure, ask whether it needs its own space and rules to grow."]]},{id:"the-hard-thing",title:"The Hard Thing About Hard Things",author:"Ben Horowitz",kind:"book",year:2014,description:"A frank guide to the problems no business book prepares you for, from layoffs to near-bankruptcy.",ideas:[["There's no recipe","Management books often describe how to avoid problems. Ben Horowitz, a venture capitalist and former CEO, writes about the hard things: situations with no clean answer, like laying off friends, firing an executive or deciding whether to sell the company. In those moments, there's no recipe; you have to choose the least bad option and live with it. Today: if you're facing a hard decision, accept that there may be no perfect answer, and choose the option you could explain honestly to everyone affected."],["The struggle","Every founder, Horowitz writes, goes through the struggle: periods when nothing works, the money is running out and quitting looks like the sensible choice. He describes it as feeling alone and doubting everything. But going through the struggle is part of building anything worthwhile, not a sign of failure. Many great companies nearly died several times. Today: if you're in a tough stretch, remind yourself it's a normal part of the path, and focus on the next small step."],["Take care of people, products and profits, in that order","Horowitz insists on an order of priorities: take care of the people, then the products, then the profits. Without good people who feel valued, you can't build great products; without great products, profits won't last. It sounds simple, but under pressure many leaders reverse the order. Today: think of one way you could make the people you work with feel more supported, and act on it."],["Tell it like it is","When things go badly, the temptation is to hide the bad news to avoid panic. Horowitz argues the opposite: tell it like it is. Being honest with your team about problems builds trust and lets everyone help solve them. Hiding bad news makes the hard thing harder, because people sense something is wrong and fill the silence with worse stories. Today: share one piece of honest, difficult news with the people it affects, along with your plan to deal with it."]]},{id:"rework",title:"Rework",author:"Jason Fried & David Heinemeier Hansson",kind:"book",year:2010,description:"Short essays challenging conventional business wisdom about growth, planning and work.",ideas:[["Planning is guessing","Long-term business plans pretend you can predict the future, but markets, customers and technology change constantly. Jason Fried and David Heinemeier Hansson call planning a form of guessing. Instead of writing a five-year plan, decide what you'll do this week, do it, then adjust based on what you learn. It's less stressful and more realistic. Today: replace one long-range plan with a clear decision about what you'll do in the next seven days."],["Small is fine","Not every company needs to become huge. The founders of Basecamp argue that staying small and profitable can bring more freedom, less stress and better products than chasing growth at any cost. Small teams make decisions faster, waste less and stay close to customers. Growth should be a choice, not an obligation. Today: think about one area of your life or work where \"bigger\" isn't actually better, and enjoy keeping it small."],["Build half a product, not a half-assed product","When time or money is short, the authors advise cutting features ruthlessly and doing what's left really well. Half a product that works beautifully is better than a full product that works badly. This forces you to decide what truly matters to your customers. Today: look at a project you're working on and identify one feature or task you could drop to make the rest better."],["Meetings are toxic","A one-hour meeting with ten people isn't one hour; it's ten hours of work time gone. Fried and Heinemeier Hansson call most meetings toxic: they interrupt focus, drift off topic and rarely produce clear decisions. When you must meet, keep it small, short, with a clear agenda and a clear end. Today: cancel or shorten one meeting this week, or replace it with a short written update."],["Say no by default","Every yes adds complexity: another feature to maintain, another commitment to keep. The authors recommend saying no by default, even to good ideas, so the product stays simple and focused. The truly important requests will come back again and again. Today: practise saying no to one request that doesn't fit your priorities, politely and without long explanations."]]},{id:"shape-up",title:"Shape Up",author:"Ryan Singer",kind:"book",year:2019,description:"How Basecamp plans and builds software in six-week cycles without endless backlogs.",free:{url:"https://basecamp.com/shapeup",note:"Free to read on the publisher's site. All rights reserved, so it's read there, not copied into the app."},ideas:[["Fixed time, variable scope","Most teams estimate how long a feature will take and then watch the estimate slip. Ryan Singer describes a different approach used at Basecamp: decide how much time a problem is worth, its appetite, and shape the solution to fit that time. A two-week appetite produces a different solution than a six-week one. Fixed time, variable scope keeps projects from dragging on forever. Today: for your next task, decide in advance how much time it deserves, and design your approach to fit."],["Shape before you build","Before handing work to a team, senior people at Basecamp shape it: they define the problem, sketch a rough solution and identify risks, at just the right level of detail. Too vague, and the team flounders; too detailed, and there's no room for their creativity. Shaping is like giving someone a map with landmarks, not turn-by-turn directions. Today: when you delegate a task, describe the problem, the boundaries and the rough shape of a solution, then let them fill in the details."],["Bets, not backlogs","Instead of an ever-growing backlog of ideas, leaders review a few shaped pitches each cycle and bet on the ones worth doing. Unchosen ideas aren't kept in a guilt-inducing list; if they're important, they'll come up again. This keeps teams focused and decisions clear. Today: look at your own to-do list or backlog and delete or archive anything you haven't touched in months. If it matters, it will return."],["Six-week cycles and cool-down","Basecamp teams work uninterrupted for six weeks, long enough to build something meaningful, short enough to keep urgency. Then they take two weeks of cool-down to fix bugs, explore ideas and breathe. The rhythm protects focus and prevents burnout. Today: try your own version: pick a focused period for a project, followed by a short break to reflect before starting the next one."],["Hill charts","Work, Singer explains, has an uphill phase where you're figuring things out and solving unknowns, and a downhill phase where you're simply executing. Basecamp teams use hill charts to show where each piece of work sits, which reveals real progress far better than counting completed tasks. A task stuck uphill for too long is a warning sign. Today: look at a project you're working on and ask which parts are still uphill, full of unknowns, and tackle one of those first."]]},{id:"getting-real",title:"Getting Real",author:"37signals",kind:"book",year:2006,description:"The small, fast, less-is-more approach to building web software that 37signals used to make Basecamp.",free:{url:"https://basecamp.com/gettingreal",note:"Free to read on the publisher's site. All rights reserved, so it's read there, not copied into the app."},ideas:[["Build less","Basecamp's founders built their product by doing less than their competitors on purpose. Fewer features meant less to maintain, a simpler product and a clearer message. Many customers actually preferred the simpler option. Building less isn't laziness; it's discipline about what really matters. Today: look at something you're making and ask what you could remove to make it better."],["Embrace constraints","Limited time, money and people force focus and creativity. Instead of complaining about constraints, the authors argue, embrace them. A small budget makes you find clever solutions; a tight deadline forces you to cut the unnecessary. Some of the best products were created by tiny teams with very little. Today: think of one constraint you're facing and ask how it could push you toward a simpler, better solution."],["Start with the interface","Many software projects start with databases and code, then bolt on the screens at the end. Getting Real suggests the opposite: design the interface people will actually use first. It's much cheaper to change a sketch than a finished system, and it keeps the focus on the user's experience. Today: before starting a project, sketch what the end result will look like for the person using it."],["Launch, then iterate","Real usage teaches far more than plans and predictions. The authors recommend getting a simple version out into the world, listening carefully to customers and improving in small steps. Waiting for perfection delays learning. Many successful products launched rough and improved quickly. Today: take something you've been polishing and share an early version with a few real users this week."],["Say no by default","Every feature request sounds reasonable on its own. Say yes to all of them, and your product becomes a cluttered mess. The authors suggest saying no by default and listening for the requests that keep coming back. Those are the ones worth considering. Saying no keeps the product coherent and the team focused. Today: when someone asks for something extra on a project, practise a polite \"not now\", and note it down to see whether it comes up again."]]}]}},886,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,n,i,r){n.exports={topic:{id:"career",name:"Career",emoji:"\ud83d\udcbc",color:"#3F4A5C"},books:[{id:"so-good-they-cant-ignore-you",title:"So Good They Can't Ignore You",author:"Cal Newport",kind:"book",year:2012,description:"Why \"follow your passion\" is bad career advice, and what works instead.",ideas:[["Passion follows mastery","\"Follow your passion\" sounds inspiring, but Cal Newport found that most people who love their work didn't start with a fully formed passion. They became passionate about work they'd become very good at, as skill brought respect, autonomy and meaning. Passion is often the result of mastery, not the starting point. Today: instead of asking \"What's my passion?\", ask \"What could I get really good at, starting now?\""],["Career capital","Newport describes career capital: rare and valuable skills that you can trade for the things people want from work, like interesting projects, flexibility and impact. The more capital you build, the more control you gain over your career. Jumping to a dream job without capital rarely works. Today: identify one skill that's both rare and valuable in your field, and plan a small step to strengthen it this week."],["The craftsman mindset","The craftsman mindset, Newport says, focuses on the value you can offer rather than what the job offers you. Instead of constantly asking whether your work is fulfilling enough, you ask how you can get better at it. Paradoxically, this mindset often leads to more fulfilment, because skill brings opportunities. Today: in your next task, focus entirely on doing it a little better than last time, and notice how that changes your experience."],["Deliberate practice","Getting better at anything, Newport explains, requires deliberate practice: stretching beyond your comfort zone, with clear feedback. It's uncomfortable, like a musician practising the hardest passage over and over, rather than playing pieces they already know. Coasting at what you already do well builds no new capital. Today: choose one skill and spend twenty minutes practising the hardest part of it, with a way to measure progress."]]},{id:"range",title:"Range",author:"David Epstein",kind:"book",year:2019,description:"Why generalists often triumph in a world that celebrates early specialization.",ideas:[["Kind and wicked learning environments","Chess and golf have clear rules, quick feedback and repeated patterns. David Epstein calls them kind learning environments, where early specialisation works well. But most of life is wicked: rules are unclear, feedback is delayed and patterns change. In wicked environments, broad experience and flexible thinking often beat narrow expertise. Today: think about whether your work is more like chess or more like real life, and what that means for how you should learn."],["The sampling period","Many top performers, Epstein found, tried many activities before settling on one. Roger Federer played several sports as a child before focusing on tennis. This sampling period helped them find a good match for their abilities and interests, which mattered more than starting early. Today: if you feel behind because you've changed paths, remember that exploring is often how people find the right fit."],["Analogical thinking","People with broad experience can borrow solutions from unrelated fields. Epstein tells stories of outsiders who solved problems that insiders were stuck on, by drawing analogies from different domains: a chemist solving a biology puzzle, an engineer helping doctors. Breadth gives you more tools to think with. Today: take a problem you're facing and ask how someone from a completely different field might approach it."],["Late starters aren't behind","Switching careers or starting late can feel like wasted time. Epstein argues the opposite: what you learned elsewhere often becomes an unusual advantage, a unique combination of skills and perspectives. Many successful people found their true path in their thirties, forties or later. Today: list three skills from past jobs or hobbies that could help you in your current goals."]]},{id:"designing-your-life",title:"Designing Your Life",author:"Bill Burnett & Dave Evans",kind:"book",year:2016,description:"Two Stanford designers apply design thinking to building a career and life you love.",ideas:[["Prototype your career","Before making a big life change, Bill Burnett and Dave Evans, who teach design at Stanford, suggest building prototypes: small, cheap experiments that test the idea. Talk to people who already do the job, shadow someone for a day or take on a small project. You learn far more from trying than from imagining. Today: pick one career idea you're curious about and arrange a conversation with someone who already lives it."],["Odyssey plans","The authors suggest an exercise called odyssey plans. Sketch three different versions of your next five years: one based on your current path, one for what you'd do if that path disappeared, and one for what you'd do if money and other people's opinions didn't matter. It loosens the feeling that there's only one right life. Today: spend twenty minutes sketching your three odyssey plans, and notice which one makes you feel most alive."],["Gravity problems","Some problems, the authors say, are like gravity: you can't solve them, so struggling against them wastes energy. If a field pays poorly and you can't change that, it's a gravity problem. The useful move is to accept it and design around it: change your goals, find a niche or combine roles. Today: identify one gravity problem you've been fighting, and brainstorm ways to work with it instead."],["Track your energy","For a few weeks, the authors suggest, keep a simple log of your daily activities and note how much energy and engagement each one gave or drained. Patterns emerge: maybe you light up in workshops but drain in long meetings. This good time journal reveals what kind of work fits you, often more accurately than personality tests. Today: start a simple log tonight, listing your activities and marking each with a plus or minus for energy."]]},{id:"the-7-habits",title:"The 7 Habits of Highly Effective People",author:"Stephen R. Covey",kind:"book",year:1989,description:"A principle-centred approach to personal and professional effectiveness.",ideas:[["Be proactive","Between what happens to us and how we respond, Stephen Covey wrote, there is a space, and in that space lies our freedom. Proactive people focus on what they can influence: their choices, attitudes and actions. Reactive people focus on what they can't control and blame circumstances. Being proactive doesn't mean being pushy; it means taking responsibility for your response. Today: when something frustrating happens, pause and ask, \"What's within my control here?\" Then act on that."],["Begin with the end in mind","Covey suggests imagining your own funeral and hearing what family, friends and colleagues say about you. What would you want them to say? That picture reveals what truly matters to you. Beginning with the end in mind means letting that vision guide everyday choices, so you don't climb a ladder only to discover it's leaning against the wrong wall. Today: write a short description of the person you want to be remembered as, and choose one action that moves you toward it."],["Put first things first","Covey sorted activities by urgency and importance. Urgent but unimportant tasks, like many emails and interruptions, eat our days. Important but not urgent ones, like planning, exercise, relationships and learning, are where real effectiveness lies, yet we often neglect them because nothing forces us to act. Today: schedule one important but not urgent activity this week and protect that time like an appointment."],["Seek first to understand","Most people listen with the intent to reply, already preparing their answer while the other person is speaking. Covey encourages listening to understand first: trying to see the situation from the other person's perspective before sharing your own. People who feel understood are far more open to hearing you. Today: in your next conversation, try summarising the other person's view before giving yours."],["Sharpen the saw","Imagine someone exhausted from sawing a tree who refuses to stop and sharpen the saw because they're too busy sawing. Covey's final habit is regular renewal of body, mind, relationships and spirit. Without maintenance, effectiveness wears down. Rest, exercise, learning and connection aren't luxuries; they're what make sustained effort possible. Today: choose one way to sharpen your saw, like a walk, a good book or a call with a friend, and do it."]]},{id:"grit",title:"Grit",author:"Angela Duckworth",kind:"book",year:2016,description:"Research showing that passion and perseverance often matter more than talent.",ideas:[["Effort counts twice","Angela Duckworth's research suggests a simple but powerful formula: talent times effort equals skill, and skill times effort equals achievement. Effort counts twice. Talent matters, but without sustained effort it stays potential. Someone less talented who keeps working can overtake someone gifted who doesn't. Today: think of one skill you've been relying on talent for, and commit to a regular practice routine to develop it further."],["Grit is passion plus perseverance","Grit, Duckworth explains, is a combination of passion and perseverance for long-term goals. Gritty people stick with their goals for years, through setbacks, boredom and plateaus. They don't necessarily work harder every day; they keep showing up over time. Grit can be developed. Today: identify one long-term goal you care about, and write down how you'll keep going when it gets boring or difficult."],["Interest comes first","Passion rarely arrives fully formed. Duckworth found that it usually starts with a spark of interest, then deepens through practice, and later grows into a sense of purpose, a feeling that your work matters to others. Interest comes first and needs to be nurtured. Today: notice one small interest that's been calling you, and give it some time this week, without pressure to turn it into a passion yet."],["Hope as a habit","Gritty people, Duckworth observes, tend to expect that their efforts will improve their future. That kind of hope isn't blind optimism; it's a habit of interpreting setbacks as temporary and fixable. When things go wrong, they ask what they can do differently rather than giving up. Today: think of a recent setback and write down one specific thing you could try next time."]]},{id:"pro-git",title:"Pro Git",author:"Scott Chacon & Ben Straub",kind:"book",year:2014,description:"The standard guide to Git, the version-control system almost every software team uses.",free:{url:"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2",license:"CC BY-NC-SA 3.0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/",note:"Free online, plus PDF and EPUB downloads from the official site."},ideas:[["Snapshots, not differences","Many people imagine version control as a list of small changes. Git works differently: each commit is a snapshot of your whole project at that moment. To save space, files that haven't changed are simply reused from earlier snapshots. This design makes switching between versions and comparing them fast and reliable. Today: if you use Git, try browsing an old commit with your project's history view, and notice how easily you can see the project as it once was."],["Three states","In Git, a file can be modified in your working folder, staged when you choose to include it in the next commit, and committed once it's saved permanently in history. The staging step lets you build clean, focused commits instead of saving every change at once. It's like packing a box deliberately rather than throwing everything in. Today: next time you commit, stage only the changes that belong together, and write a clear message describing them."],["Branches are cheap","In some version control systems, branches are heavy and slow. In Git, a branch is just a lightweight pointer to a commit, so creating one costs almost nothing. That encourages experimenting: try an idea on its own branch, and merge it only if it works. Today: if you're about to try something risky in a project, create a new branch first, so your main work stays safe."],["Everything is local","Almost every Git operation works on your own machine, using the full history stored locally. You can commit, compare and browse history without an internet connection, and it's fast because nothing travels over a network. Collaboration happens when you choose to push or pull. Today: if you're working somewhere with poor internet, remember that you can keep committing locally and sync later."],["Rewrite only unpublished history","Git lets you rewrite history with tools like rebase, to clean up messy commits before sharing them. But rewriting history that other people already have causes painful conflicts. The rule of thumb is simple: tidy up your local work before you share it, and leave published history alone. Today: before pushing your changes, review your recent commits and tidy any messy messages, but only on work you haven't shared yet."]]},{id:"think-python",title:"Think Python",author:"Allen B. Downey",kind:"book",year:2015,description:"A friendly introduction to programming and computational thinking, using Python.",free:{url:"https://greenteapress.com/wp/think-python-2e/",license:"CC BY-NC 3.0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/3.0/",note:"Free PDF and HTML from the publisher under a Creative Commons license."},ideas:[["Programming is problem solving","Learning to program isn't mainly about memorising syntax. Allen Downey emphasises that the core skill is problem solving: breaking a problem into small steps a computer can follow, then checking each step works. Programming teaches a way of thinking that's useful far beyond code. Today: take a small problem, even something like planning a trip, and break it into clear, simple steps as if you were writing instructions for a computer."],["Debugging is experimentation","Debugging, Downey suggests, is like doing science. When a program misbehaves, form a hypothesis about what's wrong, change one thing, and observe what happens. Read error messages carefully; they often point near the real problem. Patience and curiosity beat frustration. Today: next time something doesn't work, in code or elsewhere, change only one thing at a time and observe the result before trying the next."],["Functions tame complexity","Wrapping a few lines of code into a named function lets you reuse them, test them on their own and think about the program at a higher level. Instead of a long, tangled script, you get a set of clear building blocks. Downey shows how functions tame complexity. Today: if you write code, find one chunk you've repeated and turn it into a function with a descriptive name."],["Incremental development","Downey recommends incremental development: write a little code, run it, check that it works, then add a little more. If something breaks, you know it was the last small change. Writing hundreds of lines before testing often leads to confusing errors that are hard to trace. Today: in your next project, of any kind, work in small steps and check each one before moving on."]]}]}},887,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,n,a,i,s){a.exports={topic:{id:"communication",name:"Communication",emoji:"\ud83d\udcac",color:"#1F6F78"},books:[{id:"how-to-win-friends",title:"How to Win Friends and Influence People",author:"Dale Carnegie",kind:"book",year:1936,description:"The classic on getting along with people, with advice that still works nearly a century later.",ideas:[["Don't criticize, condemn or complain","When someone is criticised, they rarely think, \"You're right, I'll change.\" They get defensive, justify themselves and often resent the critic. Dale Carnegie observed that even people who have done terrible things usually believe they had good reasons. Criticism wounds pride and seldom changes behaviour. Understanding works better: try to see why the person acted as they did. Today: the next time you're tempted to criticise someone, pause and ask yourself what might have led them there, and respond with curiosity first."],["Become genuinely interested in others","Carnegie's famous line is that you'll make more friends in two months by becoming interested in other people than in two years of trying to get them interested in you. People light up when someone genuinely wants to know about their lives, work and passions. It's not a technique; it has to be real curiosity. And almost everyone has something fascinating to share if you ask. Today: in your next conversation, ask two follow-up questions about something the other person cares about, and really listen to the answers."],["Remember names","A person's name is, to them, one of the most important sounds in any language. Using it shows that you see them as an individual, not just another face. Carnegie tells of leaders who made a point of remembering names, and how much loyalty that earned. Forgetting someone's name, on the other hand, can feel like a small dismissal. Today: when you meet someone new, repeat their name in conversation and write it down later if needed. Use it the next time you see them."],["Talk in terms of their interests","If you want to persuade someone, Carnegie advises, start from what they care about, not what you want. A child doesn't care that you want them to eat vegetables; they might care about being strong enough to win at football. A colleague might not care about your deadline, but they care about a smooth week for their team. Speaking to people's interests isn't manipulation when it's honest; it's respect. Today: before asking someone for something, think about how it could genuinely benefit them, and lead with that."],["Let the other person save face","Even when you're completely right, embarrassing someone in front of others rarely helps. Carnegie notes that people remember being humiliated long after they've forgotten the issue. Letting someone save face, by correcting them privately, giving them an easy way to change their mind and acknowledging what they did well, keeps the relationship intact. Today: if you need to point out someone's mistake, do it privately and kindly, and give them room to fix it without losing dignity."]]},{id:"crucial-conversations",title:"Crucial Conversations",author:"Patterson, Grenny, McMillan & Switzler",kind:"book",year:2002,description:"Tools for talking when stakes are high, opinions differ and emotions run strong.",ideas:[["Start with heart","Before a high-stakes talk, it's easy to slip into wanting to win: proving you're right, getting the last word. The authors of Crucial Conversations suggest starting with heart. Ask yourself what you really want for yourself, for the other person and for the relationship. That question shifts your focus from scoring points to solving the problem together. Today: before a difficult conversation, write down your honest answers to those three questions."],["Make it safe","When people feel unsafe in a conversation, they tend to go silent or turn aggressive. Neither leads to good solutions. The authors recommend watching for signs that safety is breaking down, like sarcasm, withdrawal or raised voices, and then restoring it by showing respect and clarifying the shared goal. People can hear almost anything when they feel safe. Today: if a conversation turns tense, pause and say something like, \"I want us both to get what we need here.\""],["Separate facts from stories","\"You were late three times this week\" is a fact. \"You don't care about the team\" is a story we've built on top of it. The authors suggest separating the two: lead with the facts and offer your interpretation tentatively, as a possibility rather than a verdict. This invites the other person to explain instead of defend. Today: in your next disagreement, start with what you observed, then say, \"The story I'm telling myself is\u2026\", and ask how they see it."],["Fill the pool of shared meaning","Good decisions come from having everyone's views on the table, especially the uncomfortable ones. The authors call this filling the pool of shared meaning. When people hold back, decisions are worse and commitment is weaker. Inviting disagreement, and listening without punishing it, makes the pool richer. Today: in your next group discussion, ask specifically for opposing views, and thank people who offer them."]]},{id:"nonviolent-communication",title:"Nonviolent Communication",author:"Marshall B. Rosenberg",kind:"book",year:1999,description:"A four-part method for expressing yourself honestly and listening with empathy.",ideas:[["Observe without judging","Marshall Rosenberg noticed that much conflict begins with a judgment disguised as an observation. \"You're so lazy\" invites a fight; \"You left the dishes in the sink last night\" invites a conversation. His method starts by describing what happened as a camera would, without labels or exaggerations. It's harder than it sounds, because judgments come naturally. Today: in your next complaint, try to state only what you actually saw or heard, with no interpretation."],["Express feelings","After observing, Rosenberg suggests expressing your feelings with real feeling words: worried, frustrated, relieved, lonely. He notes that \"I feel that you're ignoring me\" isn't actually a feeling; it's a thought about the other person. Naming genuine feelings makes you more understandable and less threatening. Today: when you share a frustration, finish the sentence \"I feel\u2026\" with an emotion rather than an accusation."],["Connect feelings to needs","Behind every feeling, Rosenberg believed, is a need: for rest, respect, connection, safety or understanding. Linking your feelings to your needs helps others see what's really going on and makes it easier for them to help. \"I feel anxious because I need some predictability\" opens a door that \"You're so unreliable\" slams shut. Today: when you feel upset, ask yourself which need isn't being met, and mention it when you talk about it."],["Make clear requests","The final step in Rosenberg's method is a clear, specific, doable request, not a vague demand. \"Could you text me if you'll be late tonight?\" is far easier to say yes to than \"Be more considerate.\" A real request also leaves room for a no; otherwise it's a demand in disguise. Today: turn one complaint into a specific request that the other person could easily act on."]]},{id:"never-split-the-difference",title:"Never Split the Difference",author:"Chris Voss",kind:"book",year:2016,description:"A former FBI hostage negotiator's techniques for everyday negotiations.",ideas:[["Tactical empathy","Chris Voss, a former FBI hostage negotiator, says the most powerful tool in any negotiation is tactical empathy: showing the other side that you understand their position and feelings, even if you don't agree. People open up when they feel heard. It works with kidnappers, landlords and teenagers alike. Today: in your next disagreement, summarise the other person's view so well that they say, \"Exactly.\" Only then share your own."],["Mirroring","One of Voss's simplest techniques is mirroring: repeating the last few words someone said, with a curious tone, as a question. \"We need it by Friday.\" \"By Friday?\" It sounds almost too simple, but it encourages people to keep talking and reveal more about what they need. Today: try mirroring once in a conversation and notice how the other person expands on their point."],["Label emotions","Naming emotions out loud, \"It seems like you're frustrated about the timeline\", can defuse them. Voss calls this labelling. It shows understanding without agreeing, and it often lowers the temperature of a tense conversation. Interestingly, labelling negative emotions tends to weaken them, while labelling positive ones reinforces them. Today: in a tense moment, try a label that starts with \"It seems like\u2026\" or \"It sounds like\u2026\" and pause afterwards."],["Aim for \"that's right\"","Voss believes the words \"that's right\" are more valuable than \"yes\" in a negotiation. People often say yes just to end the conversation, but \"that's right\" means they genuinely feel understood. Reaching that point usually means summarising their view accurately, including their feelings. Real agreement becomes possible from there. Today: aim to get a \"that's right\" in your next difficult conversation by summarising the other person's perspective."],["Calibrated questions","Questions starting with \"how\" or \"what\" invite the other side to think about your problem. Voss calls them calibrated questions: \"How am I supposed to do that?\", \"What's most important to you here?\" They're softer than demands and harder to refuse, and they give the other person a sense of control while steering the conversation. Today: replace one demand in a negotiation with a \"how\" or \"what\" question, and see how the other person responds."]]},{id:"talk-like-ted",title:"Talk Like TED",author:"Carmine Gallo",kind:"book",year:2014,description:"Lessons from the most popular TED talks on giving presentations people remember.",ideas:[["Unleash your passion","The most memorable talks, Carmine Gallo found, come from speakers who clearly love their subject. Passion is contagious: audiences connect with enthusiasm more than with polish. If you're bored by your topic, your listeners will be too. Before giving a talk, find the part of the subject that genuinely excites you. Today: when you next have to present something, ask yourself what fascinates you about it and start there."],["Tell stories","Stories stick in memory far better than data alone. Gallo noticed that popular talks often wrap their key points in personal, vivid stories: a moment of failure, a surprising encounter, a turning point. Data persuades the mind; stories move the heart. Today: take one point you want to make at work or home and find a short story that illustrates it."],["Keep it to 18 minutes","TED talks are limited to about eighteen minutes, and Gallo argues that's no accident. Long talks overload the brain; shorter ones force speakers to cut ruthlessly and focus on what really matters. Constraints sharpen messages. Today: take your next presentation and challenge yourself to deliver it in half the time. Notice what you choose to keep."],["Deliver jaw-dropping moments","Audiences remember one striking moment more than a dozen good points: a surprising demonstration, a shocking statistic, an unexpected object or a powerful image. Gallo calls these jaw-dropping moments, and they're what people talk about afterwards. Today: think of one surprising fact, image or demonstration that could make your next presentation memorable."]]}]}},888,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,i,r,n){i.exports={topic:{id:"creativity",name:"Creativity",emoji:"\ud83c\udfa8",color:"#3D5A80"},books:[{id:"steal-like-an-artist",title:"Steal Like an Artist",author:"Austin Kleon",kind:"book",year:2012,description:"Ten short lessons on being creative in a world where everything builds on something else.",ideas:[["Nothing is completely original","Every song you love borrowed from songs that came before it. Every painter learned by copying other painters. Austin Kleon argues that nothing is completely original, and that's freeing rather than depressing. The trick is to collect influences generously, study them closely and combine them into something that's yours. Copy one person and it's imitation; blend ten and transform them, and a style is born. Creativity is remixing with care. Today: list five creators whose work you admire, and write down one specific thing you'd like to borrow from each.","steal-like-an-artist"],["Don't wait until you know who you are","Many people wait to feel ready, to know exactly who they are and what their style is, before they start making things. Kleon says that's backwards. You discover who you are by making things, not the other way round. Your voice appears after a hundred clumsy attempts, not before the first one. Waiting for clarity is often just fear in disguise. Today: make one small thing you've been putting off, a sketch, a paragraph or a song idea, without worrying whether it's \"you\" yet."],["Write the book you want to read","The old advice is \"write what you know\". Kleon prefers \"write what you like\". Think of the book, film, app or song you wish existed but can't find, and make that. Your taste, the frustrations and longings you feel as a fan, is a powerful guide. If you'd love to read it, someone else probably would too. Today: describe in one paragraph a piece of work you wish existed. That might be your next project."],["Use your hands","Screens are great for editing and sharing, but ideas often arrive when your hands are busy. Kleon keeps separate desks: an analogue one with paper, scissors and markers for generating ideas, and a digital one for refining them. Moving your body, cutting and pinning and scribbling, wakes up a different kind of thinking than typing does. Today: step away from your computer and work on a problem with pen, paper and maybe some sticky notes for fifteen minutes."],["Be boring","Creative work takes a lot of energy, and chaos in the rest of your life drains it. Kleon's advice sounds unexciting: be boring. Keep a steady routine, get enough sleep, eat well, look after your money and avoid needless drama. That stability gives you the security to take wild risks in your work. Many great artists had very ordinary days. Today: pick one small habit, a consistent bedtime or a simple budget, that would make your life calmer, and start it this week."]]},{id:"war-of-art",title:"The War of Art",author:"Steven Pressfield",kind:"book",year:2002,description:"A short, punchy book about the inner force that stops us from doing our creative work.",ideas:[["Beat Resistance by showing up","Steven Pressfield gives a name to the invisible force that stops us doing our real work: Resistance. It shows up as procrastination, sudden urges to clean the kitchen, self-doubt and the conviction that you'll start tomorrow. It's strongest right before something important. Professionals don't wait for Resistance to go away; they sit down and work anyway, every day. Inspiration usually arrives after you've started, not before. Today: set a timer for thirty minutes and begin the creative task you've been avoiding, without waiting to feel ready.","resistance"],["Resistance points to what matters","Pressfield offers an encouraging twist: the more important a project is to your growth, the more Resistance you'll feel toward it. You don't feel much resistance to watching television, but you feel enormous resistance to writing your first novel or starting your own business. So fear can work like a compass, pointing at the work that matters most. Today: notice the project you most dread starting, and consider that the dread might be a sign you're on the right track."],["Turn pro","Amateurs, Pressfield says, work when they feel like it. Professionals show up every day, whether they're inspired or not, sick or well, praised or ignored. They treat creative work as a job, with regular hours and a commitment to finishing. Turning pro isn't about making money from your art; it's about taking it seriously. Today: decide on a fixed time each day this week for your creative work, even twenty minutes, and keep the appointment like a professional would."],["The muse rewards commitment","Pressfield believes that once you commit to showing up every day, unexpected help begins to appear: ideas arrive, connections form, the right book falls into your hands. You might call it the muse, the unconscious or simply the effect of paying attention. Either way, inspiration tends to reward effort rather than precede it. Today: show up to your work as promised, even if nothing feels inspired, and keep a notebook nearby for whatever ideas arrive later."]]},{id:"big-magic",title:"Big Magic",author:"Elizabeth Gilbert",kind:"book",year:2015,description:"A warm, encouraging take on living a creative life beyond fear.",ideas:[["Let fear ride along, not drive","Fear will always come along on creative adventures, Elizabeth Gilbert writes. It's part of being human and trying something new. The goal isn't to get rid of it, which is impossible, but to keep it in its place. Imagine fear as a passenger in the car: it's welcome to come, but it doesn't get to touch the steering wheel or choose the music. You can be afraid and still create. Today: name the fear that's holding you back from a creative project, and then take one small step anyway."],["Ideas look for collaborators","Gilbert imagines ideas as living things, drifting around the world looking for a human partner to bring them to life. When one visits you, an urge to write, build or paint something, it's an invitation. If you ignore it for too long, it may move on to someone else. Whether or not you believe it literally, the image encourages us to act on inspiration while it's fresh. Today: write down the last creative idea that excited you, and take one concrete step to start it this week."],["Done is better than good","Perfectionism often looks like high standards, but Gilbert calls it fear in fancy shoes. Waiting until something is perfect is a great way to never finish anything. Done is better than good: finishing imperfect work teaches you more, builds confidence and makes room for the next project. Many beloved books, songs and films were finished in a hurry, flaws and all. Today: take something you've been polishing endlessly and declare it finished, even if it isn't perfect."],["Follow curiosity, not passion","\"Follow your passion\" can feel paralysing if you don't know what your passion is. Gilbert suggests a gentler path: follow your curiosity. Notice the small things that catch your interest, a craft, a topic or a kind of music, and explore them, one step at a time. Curiosity is easier to act on than passion, and it often leads somewhere surprising. Today: think of one thing you've been mildly curious about lately, and spend fifteen minutes exploring it."]]},{id:"creativity-inc",title:"Creativity, Inc.",author:"Ed Catmull",kind:"book",year:2014,description:"Pixar's co-founder on building a culture where creative people do their best work.",ideas:[["Every film starts ugly","Ed Catmull, co-founder of Pixar, admits that early versions of their films were, frankly, terrible: confused stories, flat characters, dead ends. What made Pixar great wasn't that ideas arrived fully formed, but that the studio protected fragile ideas while working tirelessly to improve them. Every great work starts ugly. Creativity is about patience and iteration, not instant genius. Today: if you have an early draft you're embarrassed by, remind yourself that's normal, and make one improvement instead of giving up."],["The Braintrust","At Pixar, directors regularly show their films in progress to a group of experienced storytellers called the Braintrust. The group gives candid notes, what's working and what isn't, but it has no authority to force changes; the director decides. Separating honest feedback from power keeps it useful rather than threatening. Today: find one or two people you trust and ask for honest feedback on something you're making, making clear that you'll decide what to do with it."],["Candour is essential","If people hold back their real opinions in meetings, Catmull warns, problems stay hidden until it's too late to fix them. Leaders need to make it safe to say what's not working, by welcoming bad news, admitting their own mistakes and never punishing honesty. Candour is uncomfortable, but it's cheaper than failure. Today: if you lead a team or project, ask people directly, \"What's not working that nobody's telling me?\" and thank them for the answer."],["Failure is part of the process","Catmull believes that if you're not failing, you're probably not trying anything new. Failure is an unavoidable part of creating something original, and the goal is to make it early, cheap and quick, so you learn before you've bet everything. Fear of failure makes teams play it safe and produce dull work. Today: choose one small experiment you could try this week in your work, something that might fail, and treat the outcome as a lesson either way."]]},{id:"the-artists-way",title:"The Artist's Way",author:"Julia Cameron",kind:"book",year:1992,description:"A twelve-week course for recovering creative confidence.",ideas:[["Morning pages","Every morning, Julia Cameron suggests, write three pages by hand of whatever comes into your head: worries, plans, complaints, nonsense. These morning pages aren't meant to be art or to be read by anyone. They're a way to drain the mental clutter that blocks creativity and to quiet the inner critic by outrunning it. Many people find unexpected ideas and clarity buried in the scribbles. Today: tomorrow morning, before checking your phone, write three pages by hand without stopping or rereading."],["The artist date","Once a week, Cameron recommends taking yourself on an artist date: a solo outing to something that delights or intrigues you. It could be a museum, a market, a walk somewhere new, a junk shop or a concert. The point is to play and to fill your mind with new images and impressions. Creativity needs input as well as output. Today: plan one artist date for this week, an hour alone doing something just because it sparks your curiosity."],["Fill the well","Cameron compares creativity to a well. Every time you write, paint or build, you draw water from it. If you only take and never refill, the well runs dry and you feel blocked and empty. Refilling means taking in beauty, experience and play: reading, wandering, music and nature. Creative blocks are often a sign of an empty well, not a lack of talent. Today: do one thing purely to refill your creative well, without any goal of producing something."],["Quiet the censor","The inner critic, the voice that says your work is stupid, unoriginal or not good enough, is loudest at the beginning. Cameron suggests treating it as noise rather than truth. You can even give it a silly name and picture it as a cartoon character. Keep going anyway, and edit later when you're further along. Today: when your inner critic speaks up, write down exactly what it says, then keep working. Notice that the work survives the criticism."]]},{id:"show-your-work",title:"Show Your Work!",author:"Austin Kleon",kind:"book",year:2014,description:"How sharing your process, not just finished products, helps you get discovered.",ideas:[["Share something small every day","You don't need to wait until you've finished something to share it. Austin Kleon suggests sharing something small every day: a sketch, a note about what you learned, a work-in-progress photo or a book you're reading. Over time these little shares add up to a body of work that people can follow, and they connect you with others who care about the same things. Consistency matters more than brilliance. Today: share one small thing about your current project with friends, online or in a group chat."],["Think process, not product","Finished products are impressive, but people love seeing how things are made: the messy desk, the rejected drafts, the lucky accident. Kleon encourages showing your process, not just your results. Behind-the-scenes glimpses make your work more human and relatable, and they build an audience long before the final piece is ready. Today: take a photo or write a few lines about how you're making something, and share it with someone who might be interested."],["Be an amateur","Amateurs, Kleon notes, are often better at sharing than experts. They're not worried about looking foolish, they're open about learning, and they're enthusiastic. That makes them easy to relate to and fun to follow. You don't need to be a master to share what you're figuring out; often people would rather learn from someone a few steps ahead than someone miles away. Today: share something you're learning as a beginner, mistakes included."],["Teach what you know","Some people worry that if they explain their methods, others will copy them and they'll become replaceable. Kleon argues the opposite: teaching what you know builds trust and makes people value you more. Chefs publish recipes and still fill their restaurants. Sharing your knowledge generously attracts people who want to learn from you and work with you. Today: write a short tip or tutorial about one thing you're good at, and share it."]]},{id:"free-culture",title:"Free Culture",author:"Lawrence Lessig",kind:"book",year:2004,description:"A law professor argues that ever-longer copyright and big media control are locking up culture that used to be free to build on.",free:{url:"https://lessig.org/product/free-culture/",license:"CC BY-NC 1.0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/1.0/",note:"Free PDF from the author under a Creative Commons license."},ideas:[["Culture builds on culture","Walt Disney's early films reworked old fairy tales, folk stories and even other filmmakers' ideas. Lawrence Lessig uses this to show how culture has always been built on culture: artists borrow, remix and transform what came before. When everything is locked up for generations, there's less raw material for new creators to work with. Today: think of a favourite book, song or film, and look up what older works inspired it. You'll likely find a long chain of borrowing."],["Copyright keeps getting longer","Copyright in the United States originally lasted fourteen years, renewable once. Today, Lessig points out, it typically lasts the author's lifetime plus seventy years. The result is that most works stay locked long after they've stopped earning anything, and many are simply forgotten because no one can legally share or restore them. Today: look up a book published a century ago that you'd like to read. Chances are it's now free in the public domain."],["Permission culture","Lessig describes a shift from a free culture, where building on the past was normal, to a permission culture, where every quote, sample or remix may require lawyers and payment. That makes creativity easier for big companies with legal teams and harder for individuals. He argues for balance: protecting creators while keeping room to build. Today: when you share or remix something, check whether its creator has offered it under an open license. Many have."],["Some rights reserved","Frustrated by the all-or-nothing choice of strict copyright, Lessig helped create Creative Commons, a set of simple licenses that let creators keep some rights and share others. An author can allow free copying for non-commercial use, or allow remixing as long as people give credit. Millions of photos, songs, books and courses now use these licenses. Today: if you publish anything online, consider choosing a Creative Commons license so others can build on your work legally."]]}]}},889,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,s,n,i){s.exports={topic:{id:"health",name:"Health",emoji:"\ud83d\udcaa",color:"#7A2E3A"},books:[{id:"why-we-sleep",title:"Why We Sleep",author:"Matthew Walker",kind:"book",year:2017,description:"A sleep scientist explains what sleep does for the brain and body, and what happens when we skimp on it.",ideas:[["Sleep is a performance tool","Sleep isn't the absence of activity; it's one of the busiest things your brain does. Matthew Walker, a sleep scientist, describes how the sleeping brain sorts memories, clears out waste and resets emotional balance. Regularly sleeping six hours or less impairs focus, mood and immunity, often without people noticing how much they've slipped. Many of us treat sleep as optional, the first thing we cut when life gets busy. Walker argues it's the foundation everything else rests on. Today: pick a consistent wake-up time and stick to it, even at weekends. It's one of the simplest ways to steady your sleep.","sleep-performance"],["Sleep before and after learning","Sleep before learning prepares the brain to soak up new information, like clearing a desk before work. Sleep after learning helps lock those memories in, moving them into long-term storage. Walker points out that pulling an all-nighter before an exam undermines both: you learn less and remember less of what you did learn. Students who sleep well often outperform those who cram. Today: if you're learning something new, a language, an instrument or a work skill, practise in the evening and let a full night's sleep finish the job."],["Caffeine lingers","Caffeine blocks a sleep-pressure chemical in the brain called adenosine, which is why coffee wakes you up. But it lingers: its half-life is around five to six hours, so half of an afternoon coffee may still be active at bedtime. Many people fall asleep anyway but get lighter, less refreshing sleep without realising why. Walker recommends being careful with caffeine after midday. Today: try moving your last coffee or tea to before lunch for a week and notice whether your sleep feels deeper."],["Keep the bedroom cool and dark","To fall asleep, your body needs to drop its core temperature by a degree or two. That's why it's harder to sleep in a stuffy room and why a warm bath before bed, which pulls heat to the skin, can help. Light matters too: bright screens in the evening tell the brain it's still daytime and delay the sleep hormone melatonin. A cool, dark, quiet bedroom is one of the cheapest health upgrades there is. Today: dim the lights an hour before bed, open a window or turn the heating down, and see how quickly you drift off."],["Dreams as overnight therapy","During REM sleep, the dreaming phase, the brain revisits emotional memories in a calmer chemical state, with stress chemicals turned down. Walker describes this as a kind of overnight therapy: it softens the emotional sting of difficult experiences while preserving their lessons. That's part of why problems often feel smaller in the morning and why poor sleep makes us more reactive. Today: if you're upset about something, resist the urge to settle it late at night. Sleep on it, and look at it again with a rested mind."]]},{id:"walking-study",title:"Give Your Ideas Some Legs",author:"Oppezzo & Schwartz",kind:"article",year:2014,description:"Stanford experiments on how walking affects creative thinking.",ideas:[["Walk to think better","Stuck on a problem? Go for a walk. In experiments at Stanford University, people came up with significantly more creative ideas while walking than while sitting, for example when asked to think of unusual uses for everyday objects. The boost even lingered for a short while after they sat back down. Philosophers like Nietzsche and writers like Dickens were famous walkers, and now there's data to support their habit. Movement seems to loosen the mind. Today: the next time you're stuck on a decision or a piece of writing, take a ten-minute walk and let your thoughts wander.","walk-to-think"],["Indoors works too","You might assume the creative boost from walking comes from fresh air and scenery. Surprisingly, it showed up even when participants walked on a treadmill facing a blank wall. The act of walking itself seemed to free up ideas. That's good news for anyone in a city, on a rainy day or stuck in an office: pacing a corridor or a lap of the building can be enough. Today: if you can't get outside, walk around your home or office for five minutes while thinking about a problem, and jot down whatever comes to mind."],["Walking meetings","Sitting across a table can make conversations feel formal or confrontational. Walking side by side often makes them easier: people talk more freely, ideas flow and silences feel less awkward. Some leaders hold walking meetings for brainstorming or difficult discussions. You get exercise, fresh perspectives and better ideas at the same time. It works for personal conversations too, like talking with a teenager or a struggling friend. Today: suggest a walking chat instead of a sit-down meeting for your next one-on-one conversation."]]},{id:"outlive",title:"Outlive",author:"Peter Attia",kind:"book",year:2023,description:"A physician's approach to extending not just lifespan but healthspan: the years you live in good health.",ideas:[["Healthspan over lifespan","Living longer is only half the goal. Peter Attia, a physician focused on longevity, emphasises healthspan: the years you spend in good physical and mental health. Many people live into their eighties but spend the last decade struggling with frailty, pain or memory loss. The aim is to stay strong, sharp and capable as long as possible, so that the extra years are worth living. Today: picture yourself at eighty-five. What do you want to still be able to do, carry groceries, travel, play with grandchildren? Let that picture guide your choices this week."],["Exercise is the strongest medicine","If a pill did what exercise does for health, Attia suggests, it would be the most prescribed medicine in the world. Cardiorespiratory fitness and muscle strength are among the strongest predictors of how long and how well people live, more than many risk factors we worry about. Being in the bottom fitness group carries more risk than smoking in some studies. The good news is that improving from very unfit to moderately fit brings big benefits. Today: schedule two sessions of exercise into your week, and treat them like appointments with a doctor."],["Train for your centenarian decathlon","Attia suggests a vivid exercise called the centenarian decathlon. Make a list of physical tasks you want to be able to do at ninety or a hundred: get up off the floor without help, lift a suitcase into an overhead bin, carry a grandchild, climb a flight of stairs. Then work backwards. Since strength and fitness decline with age, you need to build a big reserve now to have enough left later. Today: write your own list of five things you want to do at ninety, and choose one exercise that trains for it."],["Zone 2 cardio","Zone 2 cardio is steady exercise at an effort where you can still talk in full sentences but would rather not, like brisk walking, gentle cycling or easy jogging. Attia believes it's one of the best ways to build the aerobic base and metabolic health that protect against disease. It's not glamorous, and it doesn't leave you gasping, but a few hours a week pay off over decades. Today: go for a thirty-minute walk or ride at a pace where conversation is possible but a little effortful. That's zone 2."],["Prevention starts early","Heart disease, type 2 diabetes and dementia don't appear overnight. They develop silently over decades. Attia argues that waiting until symptoms show up is like trying to fix a leaking roof during the storm. The time to act is in your thirties and forties, or now, whatever your age, through exercise, sleep, nutrition and regular check-ups. Prevention is far more powerful than treatment. Today: if you haven't had a recent health check, book one, and ask about blood pressure, blood sugar and cholesterol."]]},{id:"in-defense-of-food",title:"In Defense of Food",author:"Michael Pollan",kind:"book",year:2008,description:"A clear argument for simple eating rules in a world of confusing nutrition advice.",ideas:[["Eat food. Not too much. Mostly plants.","Michael Pollan sums up his whole approach to eating in seven words: eat food, not too much, mostly plants. \"Food\" means real food rather than highly processed products; \"not too much\" means paying attention to portions; \"mostly plants\" means vegetables, fruits, beans and whole grains making up most of your plate. It's not a diet so much as a return to common sense, and it frees you from the confusing swings of nutrition headlines. Today: at your next meal, fill half your plate with vegetables or fruit before adding anything else."],["Beware of health claims","It's a strange pattern: foods that loudly announce their health benefits are usually processed. Breakfast cereals claim to boost immunity; snack bars promise protein and fibre. Meanwhile, broccoli and apples don't need labels at all. Pollan suggests being wary of health claims on packaging; they're often marketing that distracts from sugar, additives or heavy processing. Today: when shopping, notice which items have health claims on the front, and compare their ingredient lists with simple, unpackaged foods."],["Your great-grandmother test","Pollan offers a simple test: don't eat anything your great-grandmother wouldn't recognise as food. She'd know an egg, a carrot or a loaf of bread, but she'd be baffled by a tube of brightly coloured yogurt with a long list of unpronounceable ingredients. Another rule: avoid products with more than five ingredients, or ingredients you can't picture. These rules aren't perfect, but they quickly steer you toward real food. Today: pick one processed item you eat often, read its label, and look for a simpler alternative."],["Shop the edges","In many supermarkets, the fresh food, produce, meat, fish, dairy and bread, sits around the outer walls, while processed foods fill the middle aisles. Pollan suggests shopping mostly along the edges. It's not a perfect rule, since there are good things in the middle like beans, oats and frozen vegetables, but it's a useful habit for filling your basket with whole foods. Today: on your next shopping trip, start with the outer aisles and see how much of your list you can complete before going into the centre."],["Eat slowly, at a table","Pollan observes that how we eat matters as much as what we eat. Eating at regular meals, at a table, with other people and without screens tends to slow us down. We notice when we're full, enjoy flavours more and eat less mindlessly. Many cultures with strong food traditions eat this way and stay healthier despite rich foods. Today: eat one meal sitting at a table with no phone or TV, chew slowly, and put your fork down between bites."]]},{id:"breath",title:"Breath",author:"James Nestor",kind:"book",year:2020,description:"A journalist explores how the way we breathe affects health, sleep and performance.",ideas:[["Breathe through your nose","Your nose isn't just for smelling. It filters, warms and humidifies the air you breathe, and it helps your body absorb oxygen efficiently. James Nestor, who experimented with breathing only through his mouth for ten days, found his snoring and sleep got dramatically worse. Mouth breathing, especially at night, is linked to poorer sleep and dental problems. Today: notice how you breathe while working or walking. If your mouth is open, gently close it and breathe through your nose. Try it during light exercise too."],["Slow down your breathing","Most of us breathe quickly and shallowly without realising it, especially when stressed. Nestor describes research showing that slowing down to around six breaths a minute, about five seconds in and five seconds out, has a calming effect on the nervous system, heart rate and blood pressure. It's a practice found in many traditions, from yoga to prayer. Today: set a timer for three minutes and breathe in for five seconds, out for five seconds. Notice how your body feels afterwards."],["Exhale longer to relax","Breathing out more slowly than you breathe in activates the body's rest-and-digest system, the opposite of fight-or-flight. It's a quick, invisible tool for calming down before a stressful conversation, an exam or bedtime. One simple pattern is to breathe in for four counts and out for six or eight. You can do it anywhere, and no one will notice. Today: before your next stressful moment, take five breaths with a long, slow exhale. Feel your shoulders drop."],["Breathe less, not more","It sounds backwards, but Nestor reports that many people breathe more air than they need, and over-breathing can actually reduce how efficiently oxygen reaches our tissues. Several breathing methods train people to breathe lighter, slower and quieter, and athletes sometimes use them to improve endurance. The idea isn't to hold your breath until it hurts, but to stop gulping air. Today: sit quietly for two minutes and try making each breath a little softer and slower than feels natural, without straining."]]},{id:"the-body-keeps-the-score",title:"The Body Keeps the Score",author:"Bessel van der Kolk",kind:"book",year:2014,description:"A psychiatrist on how trauma lives in the body, and the approaches that help people heal.",ideas:[["Trauma is stored in the body","Bessel van der Kolk, a psychiatrist who has worked with trauma survivors for decades, argues that trauma isn't only a memory; it lives in the body. After overwhelming experiences, the body's alarm system can stay switched on, showing up as tension, exhaustion, sudden anger or numbness long after the danger has passed. Survivors may feel unsafe in their own skin without knowing why. Understanding this can bring relief: these reactions aren't weakness, they're the body trying to protect itself. Today: notice where you hold tension, your jaw, shoulders or stomach, and breathe gently into that place."],["Talking isn't always enough","Because trauma affects parts of the brain that sit below language, talking about it isn't always enough to heal. Van der Kolk describes how approaches that involve the body, such as yoga, movement, breathwork, rhythm, theatre and touch, can help people feel safe and in control again. These methods work alongside talking therapy, not instead of it. Healing often happens through experience, not just understanding. Today: if you carry stress in your body, try ten minutes of gentle stretching or slow movement and notice how you feel afterwards."],["Safety comes from connection","One of the strongest protections against the lasting effects of trauma is feeling seen, heard and supported by other people. Van der Kolk notes that we are social creatures and our nervous systems calm down in the presence of people we trust. Isolation makes recovery harder; connection makes it possible. This applies to everyday stress too. Today: reach out to someone you feel safe with, not necessarily to talk about anything difficult, just to share some time together."],["Reclaiming the body","Many trauma survivors learn to ignore their bodies, because paying attention to bodily feelings can bring back distress. Van der Kolk explains that healing involves gradually learning to notice sensations, a racing heart, warmth or tension, without being overwhelmed, and to feel at home in yourself again. It's a slow, gentle process, often best done with skilled support. Today: spend one minute noticing the sensations in your hands and feet. Just observe them, with curiosity rather than judgment."]]}]}},890,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,i,n,s){i.exports={topic:{id:"history",name:"History",emoji:"\ud83d\udcdc",color:"#6E4C2B"},books:[{id:"guns-germs-and-steel",title:"Guns, Germs, and Steel",author:"Jared Diamond",kind:"book",year:1997,description:"Why some societies came to dominate others, explained through geography rather than biology.",ideas:[["Geography shaped history","Why did some societies end up conquering others? Jared Diamond rejects the idea that it had anything to do with innate superiority. Instead, he points to geography: which plants and animals happened to be available for domestication, how easily crops and ideas could spread, and how dense populations became. Luck of location, not ability, shaped history's winners. Today: think of one advantage you have that came from where or when you were born, and how it has shaped your opportunities."],["The east-west axis","Eurasia stretches mostly east to west, Diamond observes, so crops, animals and inventions could spread across regions with similar climates and day lengths. The Americas and Africa run mostly north to south, crossing deserts, jungles and climate zones that made spreading much harder. A simple fact about the shape of continents changed the pace of history. Today: look at a world map and notice the direction each continent stretches. Small details can have huge consequences."],["Germs were decisive","Societies that lived closely with herds of livestock developed many infectious diseases, and over centuries some immunity to them. When Europeans reached the Americas, Diamond explains, those germs spread to people with no immunity and killed far more than weapons did. Disease shaped the conquest of continents. Today: think about how invisible factors, not just obvious ones, can decide the outcome of big events in history, health and even business."],["Food surpluses created specialists","Farming produced more food than farmers needed, Diamond points out, and that surplus could feed people who didn't farm: soldiers, priests, scribes, craftspeople and rulers. Specialists developed writing, metalwork, armies and governments, which gave farming societies enormous advantages. The surplus was the seed of civilisation. Today: think about the people whose work you depend on but never see, and how specialisation shapes your daily life."]]},{id:"the-histories",title:"The Histories",author:"Herodotus",kind:"book",year:-430,description:"The first great work of history in the Western tradition, covering the Greek-Persian wars.",ideas:[["The father of history","Herodotus, writing in the fifth century BC, set out to record great deeds so they wouldn't be forgotten, and to explain why Greeks and Persians went to war. He travelled widely, collected stories and tried to weigh different accounts. Asking why things happened, not just what happened, became the heart of the discipline we call history. Today: think about a family story or local event and ask not only what happened, but why."],["Call no one happy until they die","In one of Herodotus' most famous stories, the wealthy King Croesus asks the Athenian sage Solon who is the happiest man in the world, expecting to hear his own name. Solon refuses to name him: fortune can change at any moment, he says, so a life can only be judged as a whole. Later Croesus loses everything and remembers the warning. Today: when things go very well, enjoy it, but hold your success lightly."],["Customs differ","Herodotus described how different peoples each considered their own customs best. In one story, a Persian king asks Greeks what it would take for them to eat their dead fathers, which horrifies them, and then asks people who did eat their dead what it would take for them to burn them, which horrifies them equally. Recognising this was an early lesson in cultural humility. Today: think of one custom you consider obviously right, and imagine how it might look to someone from a different culture."],["Thermopylae","At Thermopylae, a narrow coastal pass, a small Greek force led by three hundred Spartans held back a vast Persian army for several days. They were eventually defeated, but their stand bought time and became a lasting symbol of courage and sacrifice. Herodotus' account turned a military defeat into a moral victory remembered for millennia. Today: think of a time when standing firm mattered more than winning."]]},{id:"the-wright-brothers",title:"The Wright Brothers",author:"David McCullough",kind:"book",year:2015,description:"How two bicycle makers from Ohio taught the world to fly.",ideas:[["Outsiders solved it","Wilbur and Orville Wright ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio. They had no college degrees, no government funding and no famous connections. Yet they beat well-funded experts in the race to build the first powered, controlled aeroplane. David McCullough shows how curiosity, careful study and relentless testing outperformed resources and prestige. Today: if you feel like an outsider in a field, remember that fresh eyes and hard work can sometimes see what experts miss."],["Control was the key","Many inventors of the time focused on powerful engines and big wings. The Wright brothers realised the real problem was control: how to balance and steer a machine in the air. Watching birds, they invented a way to twist the wings to turn, a breakthrough that made flight practical. They solved the right problem first. Today: when you're stuck, ask whether you're working on the real problem or just the most obvious one."],["Build your own data","When the brothers discovered that the existing tables for calculating wing lift were wrong, they didn't give up. They built their own small wind tunnel and tested hundreds of wing shapes, collecting their own data. That careful work gave them an edge no one else had. Today: if the information you're relying on doesn't match reality, consider gathering your own evidence, even in a simple, homemade way."],["Persistence through failure","The Wright brothers crashed gliders, faced public mockery and endured years of setbacks. On 17 December 1903, at Kitty Hawk in North Carolina, their machine finally made the first powered, controlled flights, the longest lasting under a minute. Decades of aviation history began with those few seconds. Today: think of a goal where you've faced setbacks, and take one more small step toward it."]]},{id:"team-of-rivals",title:"Team of Rivals",author:"Doris Kearns Goodwin",kind:"book",year:2005,description:"How Abraham Lincoln brought his political opponents into his cabinet and led the nation through civil war.",ideas:[["Hire your rivals","After winning the 1860 election, Abraham Lincoln did something remarkable: he appointed his main political rivals, the men who had competed against him for the nomination, to key cabinet positions. Doris Kearns Goodwin shows how Lincoln valued their talent more than his ego, and how he gradually won their respect. Today: think of someone who disagrees with you but is very capable, and consider how you might work together rather than against each other."],["Emotional intelligence in leadership","Lincoln was famous for sharing credit, taking blame and staying calm under pressure. When angry, he sometimes wrote fierce letters to people who'd frustrated him, then set them aside unsent. Goodwin describes this emotional intelligence as one of his greatest strengths as a leader. Today: if someone has upset you, try writing them a letter you won't send. Let the feelings out on paper before deciding what, if anything, to say."],["Learn from defeat","Before becoming president, Lincoln lost several elections and faced many personal tragedies. Rather than becoming bitter, he learned from each defeat, deepening his understanding of people, politics and himself. Goodwin shows how setbacks shaped the wisdom he later needed. Today: think of a past failure and write down one thing it taught you that you still use today."],["Timing matters","Lincoln waited for the right moment to issue the Emancipation Proclamation, until public opinion and the military situation could support it. Some criticised him for moving too slowly, but he believed that timing could determine whether a bold step succeeded or backfired. Goodwin shows how patience and conviction can work together. Today: think of a bold move you want to make, and ask whether the timing is right or whether a little more preparation would help."]]},{id:"the-silk-roads",title:"The Silk Roads",author:"Peter Frankopan",kind:"book",year:2015,description:"World history retold from the centre: the networks linking East and West.",ideas:[["The world's crossroads","Most history books put Europe at the centre of the story. Peter Frankopan shifts the view to the vast region between Europe and China, the lands of Persia, Central Asia and the Middle East, where the trade routes known as the Silk Roads met. For most of history, he argues, this was the true crossroads of the world. Today: look at a map centred on Central Asia rather than Europe, and notice how different the world looks."],["More than silk","The Silk Roads carried far more than silk. Spices, horses, paper, glass, technologies and slaves moved along them, and so did diseases like the plague that devastated Europe in the fourteenth century. These networks connected distant peoples for better and worse, much as global trade does today. Today: check the labels on a few objects around you and notice how far they've travelled."],["Ideas travelled too","Ideas travelled along the trade routes too. Buddhism spread from India into China; Christianity and Islam moved east and west; mathematics, astronomy and medicine passed between scholars in different cities. Frankopan shows how exchange, not isolation, drove progress. Today: learn about one idea or invention you use that originally came from a different part of the world."],["The pivot returns","Frankopan argues that power and influence are shifting back toward the regions along the old Silk Roads, as new railways, pipelines and trade agreements reconnect them. Understanding their long history, he suggests, is essential for understanding the present and future. Today: read one news story about Central Asia or the Middle East with this longer history in mind, and see whether it changes your view."]]}]}},891,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,n,i,s){n.exports={topic:{id:"leadership",name:"Leadership",emoji:"\ud83e\udded",color:"#40513B"},books:[{id:"start-with-why",title:"Start With Why",author:"Simon Sinek",kind:"book",year:2009,description:"Why some leaders inspire action while others don't, built around the idea of the Golden Circle.",ideas:[["Start with why","Most companies explain what they make and how it's better: faster processors, bigger screens. Simon Sinek noticed that the most inspiring leaders start somewhere else: with why. Why does this organisation exist? What does it believe? People connect to purpose before features, which is why some brands earn fierce loyalty while others compete on price. The same is true for individuals: people follow leaders who stand for something. Today: write one sentence that explains why you do your work, beyond the paycheque, and use it the next time someone asks what you do.","start-with-why"],["The Golden Circle","Sinek draws three rings, one inside another: why at the centre, how around it and what on the outside. Most communication goes from the outside in: here's what we do, here's how, and maybe why. Inspiring communication goes from the inside out: here's what we believe, here's how we act on it, and here's what we make as a result. It's a simple model for pitches, speeches and even job applications. Today: rewrite a short description of a project you care about, starting with why, then how, then what."],["Manipulation versus inspiration","Discounts, scare tactics and peer pressure can drive a sale. Sinek calls these manipulations. They work in the short term, but they don't build loyalty; the moment a cheaper or louder option appears, people leave. Inspiration is different: when people share your belief, they stay through ups and downs. Manipulation buys a transaction; inspiration earns a relationship. Today: think about why you keep buying from one brand or returning to one caf\xe9. Is it manipulation or genuine connection?"],["Trust comes from consistency","People trust leaders whose words and actions line up. When a company says it cares about customers and then hides fees, trust collapses. Sinek argues that clarity of why, discipline about how and consistency in what are the three things that keep the circle in balance. Trust isn't built by one grand gesture but by many small, consistent ones. Today: identify one small way you could make your actions match your stated values more closely, and do it this week."]]},{id:"extreme-ownership",title:"Extreme Ownership",author:"Jocko Willink & Leif Babin",kind:"book",year:2015,description:"Two former Navy SEAL officers apply battlefield leadership lessons to business and life.",ideas:[["Own everything in your world","Jocko Willink and Leif Babin, former Navy SEAL officers, describe a mission where a friendly-fire incident nearly killed their own men. At the debrief, Willink didn't blame anyone else; he took full responsibility as the commander. That act of ownership earned more respect than any excuse could. When things go wrong, a good leader asks what they could have done better and fixes it. Today: think of a recent problem at work or home and identify the part that was within your control. Own it out loud.","extreme-ownership"],["No bad teams, only bad leaders","In a famous training exercise, two boat crews compete in gruelling races. One consistently wins, the other consistently loses. When the leaders of the two crews swap places, the losing crew starts winning. Willink and Babin's conclusion: there are no bad teams, only bad leaders. Standards are set by what the leader tolerates. If you accept sloppy work, you'll get sloppy work. Today: identify one standard in a team you lead or belong to that's been slipping, and gently but clearly reset it."],["Prioritize and execute","In the chaos of combat, the authors explain, a leader who tries to solve every problem at once solves none. Their rule is to step back, take a breath, identify the single highest priority and focus all effort there, then move to the next. The same principle works in a busy office or a messy household. Panic spreads attention thin; prioritising concentrates it. Today: when you feel overwhelmed, stop, write down every pressing issue and choose the one to tackle first."],["Simple plans win","Complex plans look impressive in a meeting but fall apart under pressure, when people are tired, scared or confused. Willink and Babin insist that every member of the team should understand the plan and be able to explain it. If they can't, the plan is too complicated. Simplicity isn't dumbing down; it's making sure everyone can act when things go wrong. Today: explain your current project plan to someone in two minutes. If you can't, simplify it."],["Decentralized command","No leader can make every decision, especially when things move fast. The authors describe decentralised command: leaders explain the goal and the reasons behind it, then trust people on the ground to make decisions. Teams move faster and adapt better when they don't wait for permission. The key is making sure everyone truly understands the intent. Today: pick one task you'd normally micromanage, explain the goal clearly to the person doing it, and let them decide how."]]},{id:"leaders-eat-last",title:"Leaders Eat Last",author:"Simon Sinek",kind:"book",year:2014,description:"Why the best teams feel safe, and how leaders create that circle of safety.",ideas:[["The circle of safety","In the military, Simon Sinek observed, officers often eat after their troops. It's a symbol of a deeper idea: leaders create a circle of safety around their people. When team members feel protected from internal threats, such as backstabbing, blame and unfair layoffs, they stop watching their backs and turn their energy toward outside challenges. Trust inside the circle is what makes great teamwork possible. Today: think about what makes people on your team feel unsafe, and do one thing to reduce it."],["Leadership is a choice","A job title doesn't make someone a leader, Sinek argues; choosing to look after the people around you does. Anyone can lead, whether they're the boss or the newest employee, by taking responsibility for others' wellbeing and growth. Many workplaces have managers who aren't leaders, and leaders who aren't managers. Today: look for one way to support a colleague, friend or family member this week without being asked: offer help, credit them or teach them something."],["Leaders take the hit","Good leaders give up some of their own comfort for the people they're responsible for. They stay late to help, take blame in public and share credit freely. Sinek points out that this sacrifice is exactly what earns loyalty; people will go to great lengths for leaders who they know would do the same for them. Today: think of one small sacrifice you could make for your team, your family or a friend, and make it quietly."],["People are not numbers","When leaders treat people as numbers on a spreadsheet, costs to be cut and resources to be optimised, trust collapses. Sinek tells stories of companies that chose to protect jobs during hard times, asking everyone to share small sacrifices instead of firing some. Those teams often emerged stronger and more loyal. Seeing people as human beings is not soft; it's strategic. Today: in your next decision affecting others, ask how it will feel to the people involved, not just what it costs."]]},{id:"dare-to-lead",title:"Dare to Lead",author:"Bren\xe9 Brown",kind:"book",year:2018,description:"A researcher on vulnerability explains the courage-building skills behind brave leadership.",ideas:[["Clear is kind","Avoiding a difficult conversation feels kind, Bren\xe9 Brown notes, but it isn't. Vague feedback and unclear expectations leave people confused, anxious and unable to succeed. Being clear, with respect and care, is the truly kind option. Telling a colleague exactly what isn't working, and what good would look like, gives them a chance to improve. Today: think of one expectation you've left fuzzy, at work or at home, and make it clear in a calm, kind way."],["Vulnerability is courage","Saying \"I don't know\", \"I was wrong\" or \"I need help\" takes courage. Bren\xe9 Brown's research suggests that vulnerability isn't weakness; it's the birthplace of trust, creativity and change. Leaders who can admit uncertainty make it safe for others to take risks and share ideas. Pretending to be invulnerable shuts everyone down. Today: in a meeting or conversation, admit one thing you're unsure about, and see how others respond."],["Armoured versus daring leadership","Brown describes armoured leadership: perfectionism, cynicism, needing to be right and hiding behind busyness. Armour protects the leader from feeling exposed, but it also stops the team from learning, experimenting and being honest. Daring leadership chooses courage over comfort: curiosity instead of judgment, and owning mistakes instead of blaming. Today: notice one way you armour up when you feel threatened, such as sarcasm or perfectionism, and try putting it down once this week."],["Name your values","Many organisations list values on their walls that no one can recall. Brown suggests choosing just two core values for yourself and defining what they look like in action, including behaviours that support them and behaviours that betray them. Values only count when they guide hard decisions. Today: pick your two most important values and write down one specific behaviour for each that you'll practise this week."]]},{id:"the-five-dysfunctions",title:"The Five Dysfunctions of a Team",author:"Patrick Lencioni",kind:"book",year:2002,description:"A leadership fable about the five problems that keep teams from working together.",ideas:[["Absence of trust","In Patrick Lencioni's leadership fable, a struggling executive team discovers that their deepest problem is a lack of trust. Not trust that people will do their jobs, but the vulnerable kind: willingness to admit mistakes, weaknesses and needs. When team members can't do that, they waste enormous energy protecting themselves. Today: in your next team meeting, share one mistake you made recently and what you learned. It may encourage others to do the same."],["Fear of conflict","Teams without trust avoid conflict, Lencioni explains. Meetings stay polite and boring, real concerns are whispered in corridors and decisions are weaker because nobody challenged them. Healthy conflict, passionate debate about ideas rather than personal attacks, is a sign of a strong team. Artificial harmony is a warning sign. Today: in your next discussion, ask directly, \"What are we not saying?\" or \"Who disagrees?\" and welcome the answer."],["Lack of commitment","If people haven't had a chance to voice their opinions, Lencioni notes, they rarely buy into decisions, even if they nod along in the meeting. Commitment comes from feeling heard, not necessarily from getting your way. Good teams debate openly, then commit fully, even when not everyone agrees. Today: when a decision is made in a group, check that everyone has had a genuine chance to speak, and then clearly confirm what you've all agreed to."],["Avoidance of accountability","When commitments are vague, people hesitate to hold each other accountable. Standards quietly slip, and the leader ends up as the only one enforcing them. Lencioni argues that the best teams hold each other to high standards directly, as peers, out of respect rather than criticism. Today: if a teammate hasn't followed through on something that affects you, have a calm, private conversation about it instead of letting resentment build."],["Inattention to results","The final dysfunction, Lencioni explains, is when individuals care more about their own status, department or ego than the team's shared goals. Results suffer because everyone is protecting their own patch. The fix is to keep collective results visible and celebrated, so people see that they win or lose together. Today: write down the one shared result your team cares most about, and ask whether your recent work truly moved it forward."]]},{id:"leading-change",title:"Leading Change",author:"John P. Kotter",kind:"book",year:1996,description:"A Harvard professor's eight-stage process for leading change in organizations, and the common mistakes that make transformations fail.",ideas:[["Most change efforts fail for predictable reasons","Organisations announce bold transformations all the time, and most of them fizzle. John Kotter studied dozens of these efforts and found the same mistakes again and again: complacency, weak leadership teams, fuzzy visions, poor communication and declaring victory far too soon. The encouraging news is that predictable failures can be avoided. If you know the traps, you can step around them. Today: think of a change you're trying to make, at work or in your life, and ask which of these common mistakes might trip you up."],["Create a sense of urgency","People don't change while they believe things are fine. Kotter's first step is creating a real sense of urgency: helping enough people see, with honest evidence, why staying the same is riskier than moving. That might mean sharing customer complaints, competitor results or hard numbers. Without urgency, every later step stalls in a swamp of \"maybe next quarter\". Today: if you want to change something, gather one concrete piece of evidence that shows why it matters now."],["Build a guiding coalition","No single leader, however brilliant, can push through major change alone. Kotter recommends forming a guiding coalition: a group with enough authority, expertise, credibility and leadership skill to drive the effort, and enough trust in each other to work as a team. A lone champion burns out; a strong coalition builds momentum. Today: name two or three people whose support would make your change much more likely to succeed, and talk to one of them this week."],["Develop a clear vision and strategy","A good vision describes a future people can picture and want, and it's simple enough to explain in a few sentences. Kotter's rule of thumb: if you can't describe it in five minutes and get a reaction of understanding and interest, it isn't clear enough yet. A vision guides countless small decisions, so it must be clear enough to use. Today: write your vision for a change you care about in three sentences, and test it on a friend."],["Communicate the vision constantly","Leaders typically under-communicate their vision, Kotter observes, sometimes by a factor of ten or more. A single memo or presentation gets lost in the noise of everyday work. The vision needs repeating in meetings, emails, conversations and, above all, through leaders' own behaviour. People believe what you do far more than what you say. Today: find three different moments this week to mention your vision, and one way to visibly act on it."],["Remove obstacles and empower action","Even when people believe in a change, obstacles can block them: rigid procedures, outdated systems, unhelpful incentives or a manager who quietly resists. Kotter's advice is to clear those barriers so people can act on the vision. Enthusiasm dies quickly when every attempt hits a wall. Today: ask someone involved in a change what's making it hard for them, and remove one obstacle if you can."],["Generate short-term wins","Big transformations can take years, and people lose faith without visible proof along the way. Kotter recommends planning short-term wins: clear, unambiguous successes within the first months. They reward the people doing the work, convince sceptics and give critics less ammunition. A win doesn't have to be huge; it has to be real and visible. Today: identify one small, quick win your change could deliver soon, and make sure people see it."],["Don't declare victory too soon","After a few early successes, it's tempting to celebrate and relax. Kotter warns that declaring victory too soon is one of the most common ways change efforts fail. Old habits and resistance creep back quietly. Instead, use the credibility from early wins to tackle bigger problems and keep momentum going. Today: if you've made progress on a goal recently, celebrate briefly, then decide on the next, bigger step."],["Anchor change in the culture","New ways of working only last when they become \"the way we do things around here\". Kotter suggests showing people clearly how the new behaviours improved results, and making sure the next generation of leaders embodies them. If the culture doesn't change, the old ways return the moment pressure eases. Today: think about one change you've made recently and ask what would help it stick: a routine, a reminder or a story you can tell."],["Leadership versus management","Management keeps a complex system running smoothly through planning, budgeting and control. Leadership sets direction, aligns people and inspires them to move. Kotter argues that both are needed, but successful change is mostly about leadership, and many organisations are over-managed and under-led. Today: notice whether your days are full of managing tasks or leading people toward something better, and carve out time for the second."]]},{id:"the-art-of-war",title:"The Art of War",author:"Sun Tzu",kind:"book",year:-500,description:"An ancient Chinese treatise on strategy that is still read by leaders far beyond the military.",ideas:[["Know yourself and your opponent","Sun Tzu's most famous advice is that if you know both yourself and your opponent, you need not fear the result of a hundred battles. Many failures come from misjudging one side or the other: overestimating our strength, underestimating a rival or ignoring our own weaknesses. The principle applies to negotiations, job interviews and competitive markets. Today: before an important challenge, write down three honest strengths and weaknesses for yourself and for the other side."],["Win without fighting","The highest skill, according to Sun Tzu, is to achieve your goal without fighting at all, by outmanoeuvring, persuading or making the conflict unnecessary. Battles are costly even when you win. In everyday life, that might mean resolving a dispute through conversation rather than escalation, or finding a market no one else is fighting over. Today: think of a conflict you're in, and ask whether there's a way to reach your goal without a fight."],["Attack weakness, avoid strength","Like water flowing downhill, Sun Tzu advised, strategy should flow around strong points and toward weak ones. Attacking an opponent where they're strongest wastes energy; finding the gaps creates opportunity. In business, a small company might focus on customers the giants ignore; in sport, a team might target an opponent's weaker side. Today: identify where you're competing head-on against strength, and look for a gap where you could flow instead."],["Speed and preparation","Sun Tzu believed that battles are won or lost before they begin, in the preparation. The side that has studied the terrain, planned carefully and anticipated surprises usually prevails. Speed matters too: once the moment comes, act decisively. Preparation turns luck into opportunity. Today: choose one upcoming challenge, a presentation or an exam, and spend thirty minutes preparing for the questions or problems you're most likely to face."]]}]}},892,[]);
__d(function(e,t,n,o,i,a,s){i.exports={topic:{id:"mindfulness",name:"Mindfulness",emoji:"\ud83c\udf3f",color:"#2E5266"},books:[{id:"affect-labeling",title:"Putting Feelings Into Words",author:"Lieberman et al.",kind:"article",year:2007,description:"A brain-imaging study showing that naming an emotion helps calm it.",ideas:[["Name it to tame it","When a feeling sweeps through you, simply naming it, \"I'm anxious\" or \"this is anger\", turns down the brain's alarm. In brain-scanning studies, people who put words to an emotional image showed less activity in the amygdala, the region that sounds the alarm, and more in areas linked to calm control. Labeling creates a small step back: you're no longer drowning in the feeling, you're looking at it. It's one of the simplest emotional tools there is, and it works in seconds. Today: the next time a strong emotion hits, pause and say its name to yourself, silently or out loud.","name-the-emotion"],["Be specific with emotion words","\"I feel bad\" is a fog. \"I feel disappointed\", \"embarrassed\", \"left out\" or \"overwhelmed\" is a map. Researchers who study emotions find that people with a richer emotional vocabulary cope better with stress, because a precise label points toward what the feeling needs. Disappointment calls for adjusting expectations; loneliness calls for connection; overwhelm calls for subtraction. Vague words leave us stuck. Today: when you notice a negative mood, try to find a more exact word for it than \"bad\" or \"stressed\". If you're stuck, look at a list of emotion words and pick the closest one."],["Write it down","Spending fifteen or twenty minutes writing honestly about a difficult experience, for a few days in a row, has been linked in studies to lower stress, fewer doctor visits and better mood. Writing forces a swirl of feelings into sentences, and sentences have beginnings and ends. You start to see causes, patterns and even possible ways forward. It doesn't need to be beautiful or shown to anyone; it can be torn up afterwards. Today: set a timer for fifteen minutes and write without stopping about something that's been weighing on you. Notice how you feel afterwards."]]},{id:"zen-mind",title:"Zen Mind, Beginner's Mind",author:"Shunryu Suzuki",kind:"book",year:1970,description:"Informal talks by a Zen teacher on meditation practice and keeping a fresh, open mind.",ideas:[["Keep a beginner's mind","An expert walks into a familiar situation and sees what they expect. A beginner sees everything. Shunryu Suzuki, a Zen teacher who helped bring meditation to the West, encouraged students to keep this beginner's mind, open, curious and free of assumptions, even after years of practice. It's what makes a child fascinated by a puddle while an adult steps over it. Expertise is valuable, but it can also blind us. Today: choose one routine thing, your commute, a meal or a conversation with someone you know well, and approach it as if for the very first time. What do you notice?","beginners-mind"],["Don't try to stop thoughts","New meditators often think the goal is to stop thinking, then feel they've failed when thoughts keep coming. Suzuki taught the opposite: thoughts will come and go, like visitors at your door. Let them come, let them go, but don't serve them tea. Fighting thoughts gives them more energy; noticing them and returning gently to the breath lets them pass. The skill isn't an empty mind but a calm relationship with a busy one. Today: sit for five minutes and each time a thought appears, label it simply as \"thinking\", then return to your breath without judging yourself."],["Practice without gaining ideas","If you meditate to become calmer, more productive or enlightened, Suzuki warned, you turn it into another project to succeed at, with all the same anxiety. He encouraged practising without gaining ideas: sitting simply because sitting is worth doing. Paradoxically, the benefits arrive more easily when we stop grasping for them, like falling asleep or making a friend. The attitude of just doing it, without scoring yourself, can spread to other parts of life too. Today: do one ordinary activity, a walk or washing up, purely for its own sake, without trying to get anything out of it."],["Give your sheep a large meadow","To control a sheep, Suzuki said, give it a large meadow. Try to fence in a restless animal and it fights the fence; give it space and it settles. The same is true of a restless mind. If you clamp down on every thought and feeling, the mind struggles. If you give it room and simply watch, it gradually calms on its own. This applies to people too: those we try to control tightly often resist, while trust tends to create cooperation. Today: when your mind is racing, stop trying to force it quiet. Give it room, watch it run, and notice how it slows."]]},{id:"power-of-now",title:"The Power of Now",author:"Eckhart Tolle",kind:"book",year:1997,description:"A guide to stepping out of constant thinking and into presence in the current moment.",ideas:[["You are not your thoughts","There's a voice in your head that comments on everything: worrying, judging, planning, replaying. Eckhart Tolle points out that you can hear it, which means you are not only that voice. There's also the awareness that notices it. Simply realising this creates a little space. Instead of being swept away by every thought, you can watch thoughts come and go, like cars passing on a road. It's the beginning of inner calm. Today: for one minute, listen to your thoughts as if they were someone else's radio playing in the next room. Notice that you're the one listening."],["The present is all you ever have","The past exists only as memory and the future only as imagination. The only moment you ever actually live in is this one. Tolle argues that much of our anxiety comes from living mentally in a future that hasn't happened yet, and much of our regret from living in a past that's gone. That doesn't mean never planning; it means not abandoning the present while you do. The washing-up, the walk and the conversation are where your life is actually happening. Today: three times today, stop and notice five things you can see, hear or feel right now."],["Accept, then act","Resisting what has already happened, Tolle says, creates most of our suffering. Stuck in traffic, we fume; the traffic doesn't move faster, but we feel worse. His approach is to accept the present moment fully first, then, if action is needed, act from clarity instead of frustration. Acceptance isn't approval or passivity; it's dropping the inner argument with reality so your energy goes into what you can actually do. Today: when something annoys you, say inwardly, \"This is what's happening\", take a breath, and only then decide your next step."],["Watch the pain-body","Tolle describes a pain-body: an accumulation of old emotional hurt that can flare up suddenly and take over our reactions. A small comment triggers a flood of anger or sadness out of all proportion, because it touched something old. When the pain-body is active, it feeds on drama and negative thoughts. The way through is to notice it as it rises and to observe the feeling without adding stories to it. Watched calmly, it loses its grip. Today: if you overreact to something small, pause and ask, \"Is this about now, or about something older?\""]]},{id:"wherever-you-go",title:"Wherever You Go, There You Are",author:"Jon Kabat-Zinn",kind:"book",year:1994,description:"Simple guidance on mindfulness meditation in everyday life from the creator of MBSR.",ideas:[["Mindfulness defined","Mindfulness sounds mystical, but Jon Kabat-Zinn defines it simply: paying attention, on purpose, in the present moment, without judging. It isn't about emptying your mind or feeling blissful. It's about noticing what's actually here, the taste of your coffee, the tension in your shoulders, the irritation in your chest, instead of living on autopilot. Kabat-Zinn, a scientist, built a stress-reduction programme around this idea that has helped people with pain, anxiety and illness. Today: drink your next cup of tea or coffee as if it's the only thing you have to do. Notice warmth, taste and smell."],["You can't escape yourself","We often believe a change of place will fix our restlessness: a new city, a new job, a holiday. Then we arrive and find our worries waiting for us, because we brought them along. The title of Kabat-Zinn's book says it plainly: wherever you go, there you are. Changing your outer circumstances can help, but your relationship with your own mind travels with you. Working on that relationship is the change that lasts. Today: before planning an escape from something, spend five quiet minutes noticing what you're really feeling about it."],["Informal practice","You don't need a cushion, incense or an hour of silence to practise mindfulness. Kabat-Zinn encourages informal practice: bringing full attention to ordinary activities like walking, eating, showering or washing the dishes. These moments make up most of our lives, yet we usually spend them lost in thought. When you're actually there for them, they become surprisingly rich, and practice becomes something woven into the day rather than another chore. Today: choose one routine activity, such as brushing your teeth, and do it with complete attention, noticing every sensation."],["Non-doing","In a culture obsessed with doing, Kabat-Zinn praises non-doing: deliberately stopping, even for a moment, and simply being. It isn't laziness. It's the pause that interrupts the chain of automatic reactions, the moment before you fire off an angry email or reach for the snack. Non-doing creates room for choice. It's also restorative; stillness lets the body and mind settle. Today: set a reminder to stop for one minute three times today, doing nothing at all, just sitting and breathing. Notice what changes afterwards."],["Patience","A child once tried to help a butterfly out of its cocoon by cutting it open, and the butterfly never flew, because the struggle was what strengthened its wings. Kabat-Zinn uses images like this to describe patience: the understanding that things unfold in their own time. We can't rush healing, growth or understanding by force. Patience isn't passive waiting; it's trusting the process and staying present while it happens. Today: notice one thing you're trying to hurry, a recovery, a skill or a relationship, and let it take a little longer."]]},{id:"the-miracle-of-mindfulness",title:"The Miracle of Mindfulness",author:"Thich Nhat Hanh",kind:"book",year:1975,description:"A Vietnamese Zen monk's gentle manual for bringing awareness into every activity.",ideas:[["Wash the dishes to wash the dishes","If you wash the dishes only to get them done, Thich Nhat Hanh wrote, rushing toward the cup of tea waiting afterwards, then you aren't really alive while washing them, and you won't really be alive drinking the tea either, because you'll be thinking of the next thing. The Vietnamese Zen teacher suggested doing each task as if it were the only thing in the world: the warm water, the soap, the shine of a clean plate. Ordinary chores become moments of presence. Today: wash something, a cup or a pan, slowly and attentively, as if it were the most important task of your day."],["Return to the breath","Your breath is always with you, a bridge between body and mind. When you're scattered, anxious or overwhelmed, Thich Nhat Hanh taught, a few conscious breaths can bring you home: breathing in, knowing you're breathing in; breathing out, knowing you're breathing out. It's simple enough for a child and powerful enough for a crisis. You can do it in a meeting, in traffic or before a difficult phone call, and no one will notice. Today: take three slow, conscious breaths before you start your next task, and feel your body arrive."],["A day of mindfulness","Thich Nhat Hanh recommended setting aside a day of mindfulness, or even a single morning, each week. During that time you do everything slowly and with full awareness: waking, washing, eating, walking, cleaning. No rushing, no multitasking, maybe less talking. It's like recharging a battery that busy days drain. People who try it often find that the calm carries into the rest of the week. Today: choose a few hours this weekend to move slowly, put the phone away, and do one thing at a time."],["Smile to relax","A half-smile might sound too simple to matter, but Thich Nhat Hanh suggested it as a practice in itself. Relaxing the face into a gentle smile softens tension and sends a message of calm back to the mind. He recommended it on waking, during free moments, while listening to music and even when irritated. It's not about faking happiness; it's about giving your body a cue to let go. Today: next time you're waiting, at a red light or in a queue, try a small, relaxed smile for a few breaths. Notice whether your shoulders follow."]]},{id:"radical-acceptance",title:"Radical Acceptance",author:"Tara Brach",kind:"book",year:2003,description:"A psychologist and meditation teacher on meeting our own feelings with kindness instead of judgment.",ideas:[["The trance of unworthiness","Many people carry a quiet, constant sense that they're not good enough: not productive enough, not attractive enough, not doing life right. Tara Brach calls this the trance of unworthiness. It drives endless self-improvement and self-criticism, and it rarely lets us rest. Seeing it as a trance, a habit of mind rather than a truth about us, is the first step toward waking up from it. Today: catch one moment when you're judging yourself harshly, and ask, \"Would I speak to a friend this way?\" Then offer yourself the words you'd offer them."],["RAIN","When a hard emotion arrives, Tara Brach offers a simple four-step practice called RAIN. Recognise what's happening: \"This is fear.\" Allow it to be there, without pushing it away. Investigate it with kindness: where do you feel it in your body, and what does it believe? Nurture yourself, perhaps with a hand on your heart or a few warm words. It takes only a few minutes but moves you from reacting to caring. Today: the next time you feel overwhelmed, walk through the four letters of RAIN slowly, one at a time."],["The sacred pause","Between something happening and our reaction, there's a gap, often a tiny one. Brach calls a deliberate pause in that gap \"the sacred pause\". Instead of immediately reacting, fixing or defending, you stop, breathe and notice what's going on inside you. Even a few seconds can change what happens next: a gentler reply, a better decision, a moment of compassion. Pausing isn't weakness; it's where freedom lives. Today: when someone says something that stings, count three slow breaths before you answer."],["Acceptance is not resignation","Accepting a painful feeling doesn't mean liking it, agreeing with what caused it or giving up. Brach is careful to say that radical acceptance means seeing clearly what's here, right now, with kindness. Only then can real change begin, because we can't change what we refuse to look at. Fighting reality wastes the energy we need to respond to it. Acceptance is the ground we stand on before taking a step. Today: name one situation you've been resisting, say to yourself, \"This is how it is right now\", and then ask what one wise step would look like."]]}]}},893,[]);
__d(function(e,o,t,a,n,s,i){n.exports={topic:{id:"money",name:"Money",emoji:"\ud83d\udcb0",color:"#6B4E16"},books:[{id:"babylon",title:"The Richest Man in Babylon",author:"George S. Clason",kind:"book",year:1926,description:"Timeless money lessons told as parables set in ancient Babylon.",ideas:[["Pay yourself first","In George Clason's parables, the richest man in ancient Babylon reveals his secret: a part of everything you earn is yours to keep. Before you pay the baker, the landlord or the merchant, pay your future self, at least a tenth. It sounds obvious, but most people do the opposite: they spend first and save whatever is left, and usually nothing is left. Treating savings like a bill you owe yourself flips the order. Today: set up an automatic transfer of a fixed slice of your next pay, even 5 or 10 percent, into a separate account you don't touch.","pay-yourself-first"],["Make your gold work","Saving money is only the first step, Clason's Arkad explains. Each coin you keep should become a worker that brings in more coins, and those coins should work too, until you have an army of them. In modern terms, that means putting savings into investments that grow over time instead of leaving them idle. Money sitting in a drawer loses value to inflation; money invested patiently can multiply. Today: look at where your savings currently sit, and learn about one simple, low-cost way to let them grow, such as a broad index fund."],["Control your expenses","What we call necessary expenses, Clason warned, always grow to match our income unless we push back. Earn more and we find new necessities: a bigger flat, a nicer car, more takeaways. That's why some people on high salaries are always broke. The answer isn't misery but intention: decide what you truly value and budget for it, rather than letting spending happen by default. Today: look at last month's spending and find one \"necessary\" expense that's really a habit. Decide whether it still earns its place."],["Guard against loss","In one parable, a man loses his savings by lending them to a brickmaker who promises to buy rare jewels. The lesson is that before chasing high returns, protect what you already have. Seek advice from people who actually know the field, not from enthusiastic friends or salespeople, and be wary of schemes that promise quick riches. A small, safe return is better than a big loss. Today: before your next investment or loan, ask yourself who is advising you, what they know, and what you'd lose if it went wrong."],["Increase your ability to earn","Clason's characters also stress that wealth grows faster when your earning power grows. The more you learn, the more skilfully you work, and the more you're trusted, the more you can earn. Skills, unlike money, can't be stolen or lost in a bad deal. Investing in yourself, through courses, books, mentors and practice, is one of the most reliable investments there is. Today: pick one skill that would make you more valuable at work and spend thirty minutes learning it this week."]]},{id:"psychology-of-money",title:"The Psychology of Money",author:"Morgan Housel",kind:"book",year:2020,description:"Short stories about how people actually think about money, and why behaviour matters more than knowledge.",ideas:[["Compounding needs time, not genius","Warren Buffett is one of the richest people alive, but most of his fortune came after his sixties, because he started investing as a child and never stopped. Morgan Housel uses this to make a point that sounds boring but is powerful: long-term wealth comes less from brilliant returns than from decent returns sustained for a very long time. The secret ingredient is time, and time only works if you avoid ruin and stay invested through bad years. Today: if you're not investing yet, start with a small amount, and make your main goal to never stop.","compounding-time"],["Wealth is what you don't see","When you see someone driving a \xa3100,000 car, the only thing you know for sure is that they have \xa3100,000 less than before, or a large loan. Housel points out that wealth is mostly invisible: it's the car not bought, the money left in the account, the options kept open. Spending to look rich is one of the fastest ways to have less money. Real wealth is quiet. Today: think about one purchase you were considering mostly to impress other people, and imagine the freedom that money could buy you instead."],["Enough","The hardest financial skill, Housel says, is getting the goalpost to stop moving. If your expectations rise every time your income does, no amount will ever feel like enough, and you may risk what you have chasing what you don't need. Several talented people have gone to prison or gone broke because they wanted more when they already had plenty. Knowing what \"enough\" looks like for you is a form of freedom. Today: write down what \"enough\" would actually mean for you: income, savings, the life you want. Having a number helps you recognise when you've reached it."],["Room for error","Plans almost always meet surprises: a job loss, a sick parent, a market crash. Housel argues that the most important part of any financial plan is room for error. A cash buffer, modest assumptions and not borrowing to the limit let you survive bad luck long enough for good luck to arrive. People who plan for everything going right are often the ones wiped out when it doesn't. Today: if you don't have one, start an emergency fund, even a small one, and treat it as untouchable."],["Freedom is the highest dividend","Housel believes the highest dividend money pays is control over your time: the ability to do what you want, when you want, with whom you want. Studies on happiness find that a sense of control over one's life predicts wellbeing better than income itself. Savings give you choices: to leave a bad job, take a risk or spend a month with a newborn. That freedom is worth more than most things money can buy. Today: think about one decision you'd make differently if you had six months of expenses saved. Let that picture motivate your next saving step."],["Reasonable beats rational","On paper, the perfectly rational investment strategy might be very aggressive. In real life, it's useless if it scares you into selling everything at the worst moment. Housel suggests aiming to be reasonable rather than coldly rational: choose a plan you can actually stick with when markets fall and the news is frightening. A good strategy you follow beats a perfect one you abandon. Today: ask yourself honestly how you'd feel if your investments fell by a third tomorrow. If the answer is panic, your plan may be too risky for you."]]},{id:"rich-dad-poor-dad",title:"Rich Dad Poor Dad",author:"Robert T. Kiyosaki",kind:"book",year:1997,description:"A contrast between two father figures and their beliefs about money, work and assets.",ideas:[["Assets versus liabilities","Robert Kiyosaki offers a simple definition that changes how many people see their finances: an asset puts money in your pocket, and a liability takes money out. By that test, a rental flat that earns more than it costs is an asset, while a big house with a large mortgage and high bills may mostly be a liability, even if it feels like an investment. The goal is to gradually acquire things that pay you rather than things you pay for. Today: list what you own and mark each item as money in or money out. The result may surprise you."],["The rat race","Kiyosaki describes a cycle many people live in: earn more, spend more, take on bigger bills, so you need to earn even more. He calls it the rat race: running hard on a wheel that never gets you anywhere. Escaping it means building some income that doesn't depend on trading each hour for pay, from investments, a small business or something you created once that keeps earning. Today: brainstorm three small ways you could earn money that isn't tied directly to your hours, and pick one to explore this month."],["Financial literacy","Schools teach us how to work for money, Kiyosaki complains, but rarely how money works. Many people with good salaries still struggle because they never learned to read a budget, understand debt or think about cash flow. Financial literacy isn't about becoming an accountant; it's about understanding the basics well enough to make good decisions and not be fooled. A few books and some honest bookkeeping can change a life. Today: track every expense for one week and add up where your money really goes."],["Ask how you can afford it","Saying \"I can't afford it\" closes the conversation, Kiyosaki notes. Asking \"How could I afford it?\" opens one. The second question forces your brain to look for solutions: a side income, a cheaper alternative, a plan to save over time. It doesn't mean buying everything you want; it means not letting your current situation become a permanent limit on your thinking. Today: take something you've said you can't afford, a course or a trip, and spend ten minutes brainstorming realistic ways you might make it possible."]]},{id:"your-money-or-your-life",title:"Your Money or Your Life",author:"Vicki Robin & Joe Dominguez",kind:"book",year:1992,description:"A nine-step programme for transforming your relationship with money and reaching financial independence.",ideas:[["Money is life energy","Every time you buy something, you're paying with hours of your life, the hours you spent earning that money. Vicki Robin and Joe Dominguez suggest calculating your real hourly wage: take your pay, subtract commuting, work clothes and the takeaways you buy because you're exhausted, and divide by all the hours work really takes, including the commute. Suddenly that \xa360 jacket might cost five hours of your life. Today: work out your real hourly wage, then look at your next planned purchase and ask whether it's worth that many hours."],["Track every cent","For one month, the authors suggest, record every single penny that comes in and goes out, every coffee and every parking fee. No budget, no rules, just honest tracking. Many people find that awareness alone changes their spending, because it's hard to keep buying things you've had to write down. You also discover where money quietly leaks away. It's not about guilt but about seeing clearly. Today: start a simple note on your phone and log every expense for the next seven days."],["Fulfilment curve","Spending makes us happier up to a point. Money for survival makes a huge difference, comforts a big one, a few luxuries a pleasant one. Beyond that, the authors argue, more stuff brings clutter, worry and maintenance rather than joy. They call the peak of this curve \"enough\". Knowing where your enough lies lets you stop chasing more and redirect money to what truly matters: freedom, relationships and experiences. Today: walk through your home and notice three things you bought that added little to your happiness. What does that tell you about your enough?"],["The crossover point","Financial independence, in this book's terms, arrives at the crossover point: the moment when your monthly investment income is greater than your monthly expenses. From then on, work becomes optional. The surprising insight is that lowering your spending gets you there faster than earning more, because it shrinks the target and increases your savings at the same time. Today: calculate how much your monthly expenses are, and estimate how much you'd need invested to cover them. Even if the number seems far away, knowing it makes the path visible."]]},{id:"little-book-common-sense-investing",title:"The Little Book of Common Sense Investing",author:"John C. Bogle",kind:"book",year:2007,description:"The founder of Vanguard makes the case for low-cost index funds.",ideas:[["Own the whole market","Searching for the one brilliant stock is like hunting for a needle in a haystack. John Bogle, founder of Vanguard, had a simpler idea: buy the whole haystack. A broad index fund owns a small piece of every company in a market, so you get the market's overall return at very low cost, without needing to pick winners. It's boring, and that's the point. Over decades, this simple approach has beaten most professionals. Today: look up what a broad, low-cost index fund is in your country, and read one page about how it works."],["Costs matter enormously","A 1 percent annual fee sounds tiny. But over thirty or forty years, compounding works against you, and that small fee can swallow a large share of what you would have earned. Bogle hammered this point home: in investing, you get what you don't pay for. Every penny paid in fees, commissions and trading costs is a penny that doesn't grow for you. Choosing low-cost funds is one of the few investment decisions entirely within your control. Today: check the fees on any investments or pension you have. If you don't know them, find out."],["Most active funds lose","Most actively managed funds, run by professionals trying to beat the market, fail to do so over long periods once their fees are counted. Some succeed for a few years, but it's almost impossible to predict which ones will keep succeeding. Bogle saw this as a strong argument for index funds: instead of betting on finding the rare winner, simply own the market and keep costs low. Today: if you're paying a manager to pick investments for you, look at how their results compare with a simple index over ten years."],["Don't just do something, stand there","When markets fall, the instinct is to act: sell, switch funds, chase the latest trend. Bogle's advice was the opposite: don't just do something, stand there. Frequent trading adds costs and mistakes, and investors who jump in and out often end up buying high and selling low. The most effective strategy for most people is to buy sensible investments, keep adding regularly and leave them alone for a very long time. Today: if you invest, consider checking your account less often. A calm investor is usually a richer one."]]},{id:"i-will-teach-you-to-be-rich",title:"I Will Teach You to Be Rich",author:"Ramit Sethi",kind:"book",year:2009,description:"A practical six-week programme for automating your finances and spending guilt-free on what you love.",ideas:[["Automate your money","Willpower is a terrible financial plan. Ramit Sethi suggests making good decisions automatic. On payday, set up transfers that move money to savings, investments and bills before you ever see it in your spending account. You can't spend what isn't there, and you don't have to fight yourself every month. Once the system runs, it quietly builds wealth while you get on with life. Today: set up one automatic transfer for the day after your next payday, even a small one, so saving happens without you."],["Spend extravagantly on what you love","Sethi's version of frugality isn't about giving up all small pleasures. It's about spending extravagantly on the things you love and cutting mercilessly on the things you don't. If travel lights you up, spend on travel and skip the expensive gym you never use. If you love good food, enjoy it and drive an older car. The aim is a life that feels rich to you, not one that looks rich to others. Today: name your two money joys, the things you'd happily spend more on, and two expenses you wouldn't miss."],["Conscious spending plan","Instead of a complicated budget, Sethi proposes a conscious spending plan with four buckets: fixed costs like rent and bills, investments, savings goals such as a holiday or a deposit, and guilt-free spending on anything you like. If your fixed costs are reasonable and the first three buckets are funded automatically, you can spend the rest without guilt. It's simple enough to actually stick to. Today: roughly sort your monthly income into these four buckets and see whether the proportions feel right to you."],["Focus on big wins","People agonise over skipping a \xa33 coffee while ignoring decisions worth thousands: negotiating a salary, refinancing a loan, choosing a cheaper home. Sethi calls these big wins. A single successful salary negotiation can be worth more than a lifetime of skipped lattes. Focus your energy on the few decisions that really move the needle, and relax about the small stuff. Today: identify one big-ticket area, such as your pay, rent, insurance or bank fees, and spend an hour researching how to improve it."]]},{id:"almanack-of-naval",title:"The Almanack of Naval Ravikant",author:"Eric Jorgenson",kind:"book",year:2020,description:"Naval Ravikant's ideas on building wealth and finding happiness, collected from his tweets, podcasts and essays.",free:{url:"https://www.navalmanack.com/",note:"Free PDF and EPUB from the official site. Not licensed for redistribution, so download it there."},ideas:[["Seek wealth, not money or status","Naval Ravikant separates three things people chase. Money is how we move time and value between people. Status is our rank in a social hierarchy, and it's zero-sum: someone has to be lower for you to be higher. Wealth is assets that earn while you sleep, like businesses, investments, code or creative work that keeps paying off. Chasing status creates conflict; building wealth can lift everyone. Today: ask yourself whether your current ambitions are really about wealth and freedom, or about status and comparison."],["Specific knowledge","The skills that make you hard to replace usually can't be taught in a classroom, Naval argues. He calls this specific knowledge: the unusual mix of interests and talents that feels like play to you and looks like work to others. It's often found by following your genuine curiosity rather than what's fashionable. Someone who loves both biology and storytelling might become a brilliant science writer. Today: list three things you could talk about for hours without getting bored, and ask how they might combine into something valuable."],["Leverage multiplies judgment","In the past, multiplying your impact required permission: other people's labour or capital. Today there's a new kind of leverage that doesn't: code and media. A program or a video can serve millions of people at almost no extra cost. Naval's point is that combining good judgment with this kind of leverage can create huge value. One person with a laptop can build something that reaches the world. Today: think of one thing you know or do well, and imagine how a piece of writing, a video or a simple tool could share it with far more people."],["Play long-term games with long-term people","The biggest returns in life, Naval says, come from compound interest, and not just in money. Relationships, skills and reputation compound too. Someone you've worked with honestly for ten years trusts you in ways a stranger never will. That's why he advises playing long-term games with long-term people: choose partners, friends and projects you'd happily stick with for decades, and keep showing up. Today: think of one relationship worth investing in for the long run, and do something small to strengthen it."],["Happiness is a skill","Naval treats happiness less as something you find and more as a skill you can practise, like fitness. He suggests habits that train it: meditating, exercising, spending time in nature, reducing unnecessary desires and being grateful. He also notes that a desire is a little contract you make with yourself to be unhappy until you get what you want, so choosing desires carefully matters. Today: pick one craving that's been nagging you, and ask whether it's worth the discomfort of wanting it. Letting go of it might be the quicker route to feeling good."]]}]}},894,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,i,n,s){i.exports={topic:{id:"philosophy",name:"Philosophy",emoji:"\ud83c\udfdb\ufe0f",color:"#5C4033"},books:[{id:"enchiridion",title:"Enchiridion",author:"Epictetus",kind:"book",year:125,description:"A short Stoic handbook compiled by a student of Epictetus, a former slave who became one of Rome's great teachers.",ideas:[["Separate what you control","Epictetus was born a slave and once had his leg broken by his master. He went on to teach one of the most practical philosophies ever written, and it begins with a single sorting exercise. Some things are up to us: our judgments, our choices, our efforts. Many others are not: the weather, other people's opinions, our reputation, even our health in the end. Most misery comes from pouring energy into the second pile and neglecting the first. Peace begins when we stop wrestling with what we can't change. Today: write two columns, \"mine\" and \"not mine\", for something that's worrying you, and put your effort only into the first.","dichotomy-of-control"],["It's our judgments that disturb us","Someone cuts in front of you in traffic and your blood boils. Epictetus would point out that the car didn't make you angry; your judgment did: \"How dare they, this is outrageous, I've been wronged.\" Events themselves are neutral, he argued, and it's the story we tell about them that wounds us. That isn't a call to feel nothing; it's an invitation to examine the thought behind the feeling. Is it actually true? Does it help? Today: when something upsets you, pause and name the judgment underneath, then ask whether there's a calmer way to see the same event."],["Want things as they are","Epictetus offers advice that sounds strange at first: don't wish for things to happen the way you want; wish for them to happen the way they do. He doesn't mean giving up or pretending to like bad news. He means that once something has happened, fighting against it in your head only adds a second layer of suffering. Accept what is, then act wisely on what comes next. The rain has already started; the only question is whether you'll walk in it miserable or find your umbrella. Today: notice one thing you keep resisting that has already happened, and say quietly, \"This is how it is. What now?\""],["Remember what things are","If you're fond of a mug, Epictetus says, remind yourself that it's a mug, so that when it breaks you won't be shattered too. He extends this, gently but bravely, to the people we love: remember that they are mortal. This isn't meant to make us cold. It's meant to make us appreciate what we have while we have it, without the illusion that it's ours forever. Holding things lightly lets us enjoy them more and grieve them more cleanly. Today: look at something or someone you treasure and quietly remind yourself it won't last forever. Then enjoy it with fresh eyes."],["Act your part well","Epictetus compared life to a play. You didn't write the script or choose your role. You might be cast as a king, a beggar, a parent or a patient. Your task isn't to complain about the part but to play it well. This frees you from comparison: envying someone else's role helps no one, while performing your own with skill and grace is fully in your power. Every role has its dignity. Today: think about the roles you play, at work, at home and among friends, and choose one to play a little better this week."]]},{id:"meditations",title:"Meditations",author:"Marcus Aurelius",kind:"book",year:180,description:"Private notes a Roman emperor wrote to himself on how to live with virtue, calm and purpose.",ideas:[["Remember that you will die","Marcus Aurelius was the most powerful man in the Roman world, and he reminded himself constantly that he would die. Not to frighten himself, but to see clearly. If today might be among your last, would you really spend it nursing a grudge, scrolling aimlessly or postponing the conversation that matters? Remembering mortality strips away the trivial and brings the important into focus. It's less about death and more about living deliberately. Today: ask yourself, \"If this were my last week, what would I stop doing, and what would I finally do?\" Then do one small piece of the answer.","memento-mori"],["Prepare for difficult people","Marcus began some mornings by reminding himself that he would meet meddling, ungrateful, arrogant and dishonest people that day. It sounds gloomy, but it was a form of kindness. If you expect difficult people, they don't ambush your peace. He also reminded himself that they act out of ignorance, not because they're monsters, and that we're all made for cooperation, like two hands or two eyes. Preparing for friction makes patience possible. Today: before a meeting or family visit you're dreading, picture the most annoying moment and decide calmly how you'll respond."],["The obstacle becomes the way","Marcus noticed that what stands in the way of an action can itself become the way forward. If a colleague blocks your plan, the obstacle becomes a chance to practise patience or find a better plan. Illness becomes a lesson in endurance; a cancelled trip becomes time for a book you've put off. This isn't pretending problems are good. It's refusing to let them have the last word. Every obstacle carries material for some virtue. Today: take one frustration from this week and ask what quality it gives you a chance to practise: courage, patience, creativity or kindness."],["Retreat into yourself","People long for holidays by the sea or a cabin in the mountains, Marcus wrote, but you can retreat into yourself at any moment. A calm mind is a refuge you carry everywhere. He kept a few short principles in mind, like reminders that things don't touch the soul and that everything changes, so he could return to them in seconds, even in the middle of a crisis. You don't need a perfect setting to find peace; you need a quiet place inside. Today: choose one short phrase that steadies you and repeat it slowly the next time you feel overwhelmed."],["Do the work of a human being","On mornings when he didn't want to get out of bed, the emperor argued with himself in his notebook. Was he made to lie under warm blankets? Ants, bees and birds all do their work; why wouldn't a human do theirs? We were made, he concluded, to work with others and to be useful, and comfort isn't the goal of a life. It's oddly comforting that even Marcus Aurelius struggled with the snooze button. Today: if you're dragging your feet, name the person or purpose your work serves, and get up for that."],["Everything is change","Rivers wear down mountains, empires rise and fall, and the people we admire grow old. Marcus saw change as the basic nature of the universe; nature, he said, loves nothing so much as turning things into new things. Seeing this clearly makes loss less shocking and clinging less tempting. Even our worries are temporary weather. It can also make the present more precious: this exact moment will never return. Today: look at something around you, a tree, a coffee cup or your own hand, and remind yourself it's in the middle of changing. Let that make you gentler with today."]]},{id:"letters-from-a-stoic",title:"Letters from a Stoic",author:"Seneca",kind:"book",year:65,description:"Seneca's letters to his friend Lucilius, full of practical advice on time, friendship, fear and wealth.",ideas:[["Life is long if you know how to use it","Seneca, a Roman statesman and writer, insisted that life isn't short; we make it short by wasting it. We guard our money carefully but let others take our hours freely, through meetings, small talk and endless distractions, and we never get time back. If we used our days well, he argued, life would be long enough for great things. The point isn't to cram every minute but to notice where time goes and spend it on purpose. Today: track how you spend your free hours for one evening, then ask which of them you'd buy back if you could."],["Rehearse poverty","Seneca suggested a strange exercise for the wealthy: every so often, live for a few days on the cheapest food, wear rough clothes and sleep simply, then ask, \"Is this what I was afraid of?\" Practising discomfort shrinks the fear of losing comforts. You discover you need far less than you think, and that freedom gives you courage. You can take more risks, say no more often and enjoy luxuries without being enslaved by them. Today: try a small voluntary hardship, a cold shower or a day of simple meals, and notice how much less frightening \"less\" feels afterwards."],["We suffer more in imagination","Most of what we fear never happens, Seneca observed, and what does happen is rarely as bad as we imagined. Yet we suffer in advance, sometimes for weeks, rehearsing disasters in our heads. He advised dealing with real troubles when they arrive, and asking calmly whether imagined ones are even likely. Worry feels like preparation, but often it's just suffering twice. Today: write down a worry, then add two lines: \"How likely is this, honestly?\" and \"If it happens, what would I do?\" Often, seeing it on paper takes away half its power."],["Choose friends carefully","\"Choose your company carefully,\" Seneca advised his friend, because we absorb the habits, attitudes and values of those around us without noticing. Spend time with people who complain constantly and you'll start complaining; spend it with people who are curious, kind and disciplined and you'll drift that way too. Seek out people who make you better, he wrote, and people you can make better, since we learn by teaching. Friendship is not just comfort; it's quiet education. Today: think of one person whose presence lifts you, and reach out to arrange time together soon."],["Read deeply, not widely","Seneca warned against flitting from book to book like a restless traveller who has many acquaintances but no friends. Reading widely but shallowly leaves nothing behind. He preferred to stay with a few great writers, read them slowly and take away one idea each day to chew on. Wisdom comes from digestion, not consumption. In an age of endless feeds and summaries, this advice feels even more urgent. Today: instead of skimming many articles, choose one thoughtful passage, read it twice and write down one sentence about what it means for your life."]]},{id:"nicomachean-ethics",title:"Nicomachean Ethics",author:"Aristotle",kind:"book",year:-340,description:"Aristotle's foundational work on happiness, virtue and what it means to live a good life.",ideas:[["Happiness is an activity","For Aristotle, happiness isn't a mood that comes and goes. It's something you do over a whole life: living well, using your abilities fully and acting with virtue. He used the Greek word eudaimonia, often translated as flourishing. A good life, in this sense, looks less like a series of pleasant moments and more like a craft practised with skill, through friendships, work, courage and honesty. One sunny day doesn't make a summer, he noted, and one good day doesn't make a happy life. Today: ask yourself not \"Am I happy right now?\" but \"Am I living in a way I'd be proud of over many years?\""],["The golden mean","Courage, Aristotle said, sits between two failures: cowardice, too little, and recklessness, too much. Generosity sits between stinginess and wastefulness; confidence between timidity and arrogance. He called this balancing point the golden mean. It isn't a simple average; the right amount depends on the person and the situation. Telling a joke at a party is charming, while telling the same joke at a funeral is not. Virtue is the skill of hitting the right note. Today: think of a quality you're working on and ask whether you tend to overshoot or undershoot it, and adjust slightly toward the middle."],["We become what we repeatedly do","We become just by doing just acts, brave by doing brave acts and generous by giving, Aristotle argued, much as we become builders by building. Character isn't a gift you're born with; it's formed by repetition until good actions feel natural. This is both humbling and hopeful. It means our habits are shaping who we are every day, for better or worse, and that change is always possible through practice. Today: choose one virtue, such as patience, honesty or kindness, and act it out deliberately three times, even when it feels a bit forced."],["Three kinds of friendship","Aristotle described three kinds of friendship. Some are based on usefulness: colleagues, business contacts. Some are based on pleasure: the friend who's fun at parties. The rarest are based on admiring each other's character, wanting the best for the other person for their own sake. The first two are fine but fragile; when the usefulness or fun ends, so does the friendship. The third kind lasts through changes in circumstances. Such friends are few, and they take time and trust to grow. Today: think of one friend you truly admire, and tell them specifically what you value in them."],["Practical wisdom","Knowing the rules of ethics is not enough. Aristotle believed the most important virtue was practical wisdom: the ability to see what a particular situation calls for and act on it well. A doctor with textbook knowledge but no judgment can still harm a patient. Practical wisdom grows with experience, reflection and good examples, which is why we often trust older mentors with situations no rulebook covers. Today: when you face a tricky decision, ask how a wise person you know would see it, and what they would notice that you might be missing."]]},{id:"tao-te-ching",title:"Tao Te Ching",author:"Lao Tzu",kind:"book",year:-400,description:"A brief, poetic classic of Chinese philosophy about living in harmony with the natural way of things.",ideas:[["Be like water","Nothing in the world is softer than water, Lao Tzu observed, yet nothing is better at wearing down the hard and strong. Water doesn't fight the rock; it flows around it, finds the low places and keeps going until the rock gives way. This is a picture of strength through gentleness and persistence rather than force. In conflicts, the person who stays calm and flexible often outlasts the one who pushes hardest. Today: in a situation where you're tempted to force your way through, try the way of water: stay patient, look for another path, and keep moving."],["Wu wei: effortless action","Wu wei is often translated as \"non-action\", but it's closer to effortless action: acting in harmony with how things naturally unfold instead of straining against them. Think of a skilled carpenter who follows the grain of the wood, or a swimmer who uses the current rather than thrashing against it. It isn't laziness; it's a kind of wisdom about where effort will actually help. Many of our struggles come from forcing outcomes before their time. Today: notice one area where you're pushing hard and getting nowhere, and ask what would happen if you worked with the situation instead of against it."],["The usefulness of emptiness","A bowl is useful because of its empty space; a room because of the openings for doors and windows; a wheel because of the hole at its centre. Lao Tzu pointed out that emptiness, what isn't there, can be as valuable as what is. A calendar with some empty hours leaves room for the unexpected; a mind with some quiet leaves room for new ideas; a conversation with pauses leaves room for the other person. Today: create one small emptiness on purpose: an unscheduled hour, a cleared shelf or a silence in a conversation. Notice what fills it."],["Knowing when enough is enough","Those who know they have enough are rich, Lao Tzu wrote. Endless wanting is a kind of poverty no income can cure. If more is always the goal, satisfaction keeps sliding away, like a horizon that retreats as you walk toward it. Contentment isn't the same as giving up on growth; it's being at peace with what you have while you work toward what's next. Today: write down five things you already have that you once longed for: a skill, a friendship, a home, good health. Let yourself feel, for a minute, that this is enough."],["The best leaders are barely noticed","The best leaders, says the Tao Te Ching, are those whose people barely notice they're there. When the work is done, the people say, \"We did it ourselves.\" Lesser leaders are loved and praised; worse ones are feared; the worst are despised. Great leadership creates conditions where others can thrive, then steps back and lets them take ownership. It's a lesson for managers, parents and teachers alike. Today: in a project you lead, find one decision you can hand over to someone else, and let them succeed in their own way."]]},{id:"the-republic",title:"The Republic",author:"Plato",kind:"book",year:-375,description:"Plato's dialogue on justice, the ideal city and the nature of knowledge, featuring Socrates.",ideas:[["The allegory of the cave","Plato imagined prisoners chained in a cave since birth, facing a wall. Behind them a fire casts shadows of objects onto the wall, and the prisoners believe the shadows are all of reality. One prisoner is freed, climbs out into the sunlight and, after painful adjustment, sees the real world. When he returns to tell the others, they mock him and refuse to believe him. Education, Plato suggests, is this turning toward the light, and it's often uncomfortable and unwelcome. Today: think of a belief you hold mostly because everyone around you does, and look for one good argument against it."],["The ring of Gyges","A shepherd named Gyges finds a ring that makes him invisible. Soon he uses it to commit crimes and seize a throne. Plato uses this story to ask a sharp question: if you could never be caught, would you still be good? Some argue that people behave justly only because they fear punishment. Plato's Socrates disagrees, arguing that a just life is better in itself, because injustice ruins the soul of the person who commits it. Today: notice one small moment when no one would know whether you did the right thing, and do it anyway."],["The tripartite soul","Plato pictured the human soul as having three parts: reason, which thinks; spirit, which feels pride and anger; and appetite, which wants food, comfort and pleasure. He compared them to a charioteer, reason, steering two horses. A just person lets reason guide, with spirit as its ally and appetite kept in check. When appetite or anger takes the reins, life goes off the road. It's an old image, but it still describes inner conflicts, like the late-night snack you know you'll regret. Today: when you feel pulled in two directions, ask which part of you is holding the reins."],["Philosopher kings","Plato argued that cities would be best governed by philosopher kings: people who love wisdom and truth, and who don't particularly want power. His suspicion was that those who crave power most are often the least fit to hold it. Whether or not you agree with his ideal state, the point is worth considering: good leadership requires knowledge and character, not just ambition. Today: when you judge a leader in politics, at work or in your community, ask not only how much they want the role, but how wise and fair they've been."]]},{id:"openstax-intro-philosophy",title:"Introduction to Philosophy",author:"Nathan Smith et al. (OpenStax)",kind:"book",year:2022,description:"A free, peer-reviewed university textbook covering the big questions of philosophy, from logic and ethics to the mind and society.",free:{url:"https://openstax.org/details/books/introduction-philosophy",license:"CC BY 4.0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",note:"Free from OpenStax at Rice University, online and as a PDF."},ideas:[["Philosophy starts with questions","Children ask \"why?\" until adults run out of answers. Philosophy keeps asking. It examines assumptions most people never question: What is real? What can we know for sure? What makes an action right or wrong? It doesn't always deliver final answers, but asking clearly is already powerful. A well-posed question can dissolve a confusion or reveal a choice you didn't know you had. Philosophy is less a set of facts than a way of thinking carefully. Today: pick a belief you hold strongly and ask \"why?\" three times in a row. See where you end up."],["Arguments have structure","An argument in philosophy isn't a shouting match; it's a set of reasons, called premises, offered in support of a conclusion. If an argument is valid and its premises are true, the conclusion must be true. So when you disagree with a conclusion, you have two jobs: find a premise you think is false, or show that the reasoning doesn't follow. This structure makes disagreements more productive and less personal. Today: when you hear a strong opinion, try to write the argument behind it as a few short premises leading to a conclusion. You'll see much more clearly where you agree and where you don't."],["Three big approaches to ethics","Imagine you could save five people by causing harm to one. Three classic approaches answer differently. Consequentialism judges actions by their results, so saving five seems better. Deontology focuses on duties and rules, such as never using a person merely as a means. Virtue ethics asks what a good person, with compassion, courage and wisdom, would do. Each captures something important, and real decisions often need all three lenses. Today: take a dilemma you're facing and look at it through each lens in turn. Notice which one you naturally lean on, and which you tend to ignore."],["Philosophy is global","Philosophy is often taught as a story of ancient Greek men, but people everywhere have wrestled with the same deep questions. Confucian thinkers in China explored how to live in harmony with others; Indian philosophers built sophisticated theories of knowledge and the self; African traditions developed ideas like ubuntu, the belief that a person is a person through other people; Indigenous thinkers in the Americas reflected on our relationship with the land. Today: read about one philosophical idea from a tradition you know little about, and notice what it adds to how you see the world."]]}]}},895,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,i,n,s){i.exports={topic:{id:"productivity",name:"Productivity",emoji:"\u26a1",color:"#2F5D50"},books:[{id:"gtd",title:"Getting Things Done",author:"David Allen",kind:"book",year:2001,description:"A complete system for capturing every commitment outside your head so your mind is free to focus on the work in front of you.",ideas:[["The two-minute rule","Some tasks cost more to remember than to do. Replying \"yes, Thursday works\", filing a receipt, putting the scissors back: each takes less time than writing it on a list, rereading the list tomorrow and feeling the small pinch of guilt every time you see it. Unfinished trivia doesn't stay small; it sits in the back of your mind and hums. David Allen's rule is simple: if something will take two minutes or less, finish it the moment it reaches you. You'll be surprised how many of your \"to-dos\" vanish this way, and how much lighter a clean desk of small things feels. Today: the next time a quick task lands in front of you, don't park it. Do it.","two-minute-rule"],["Your mind is for having ideas","Your brain is a brilliant thinker and a terrible secretary. It reminds you to buy batteries while you're in the shower and forgets them when you're standing in the shop. Every open promise you try to hold in your head keeps a little light blinking, and dozens of blinking lights add up to a vague, constant unease. Allen's answer is a trusted inbox outside your head: a notebook, an app, a tray on your desk. Capture everything, the big and the silly, the moment it appears. Once your mind believes nothing will be lost, it relaxes and starts doing what it's actually good at: making connections and having ideas. Today: empty your head onto paper for ten minutes, every loose thought, and notice the quiet afterwards."],["Define the next physical action","\"Sort out the car\" sits on a list for weeks because it isn't really a task. It's a fog. Your brain looks at it, can't see where to begin, and slides away to something easier. The fix is to name the very next visible action: \"call the garage on Elm Street and ask for a Friday slot.\" Now there is something your hands can actually do, and the fog becomes a five-minute phone call. Allen argues that most procrastination isn't laziness at all; it's unclear thinking dressed up as a chore. Clarity does the motivating for you. Today: pick the item on your list you've been avoiding longest and rewrite it as a single physical action you could start in the next hour."],["Projects are anything with two steps","\"Plan holiday\" looks like one item, but it hides a dozen: choose dates, check the budget, book flights, ask a neighbour to water the plants. When we treat outcomes like tasks, they stall, because there's no obvious place to start and no sense of progress. Allen's definition is wonderfully plain: anything that needs more than one action is a project. Keep a separate list of these outcomes, and make sure each one always has at least one next action sitting on your action list. That one small link is what keeps big things moving instead of quietly dying. Today: write down every project you've got going, even the small ones, and give each a single next step. You'll probably find a few that have been frozen for months."],["Do a weekly review","Systems don't fail all at once; they rot quietly. A list that isn't reviewed slowly fills with things you've already done, things you no longer care about, and things you're avoiding, until you stop trusting it and go back to juggling everything in your head. The weekly review is the maintenance that keeps the whole method alive. Once a week, for about an hour, you empty every inbox, look at your calendar back and forward, walk through each project and decide what's next. It feels like admin, but people who do it describe a strange calm afterwards: the sense that nothing important is slipping through the cracks. Today: block an hour this Friday afternoon, label it \"weekly review\", and protect it like a meeting."],["Organize by context","A single long to-do list is a cruel tool. You stand in the supermarket and scroll past \"draft report\" and \"call Mum\" looking for \"buy milk\". Allen suggests sorting actions by the place, tool or person they need: at the computer, on the phone, errands, at home, to discuss with Sam. When you arrive somewhere, you see only what you can actually do there, a short list instead of a wall. It sounds like a small tweak, but it changes how you move through a day: in a waiting room you knock off three phone calls; in town you clear all your errands in one loop. Today: split your list into three or four contexts and see how much easier it is to pick what's next."]]},{id:"eat-that-frog",title:"Eat That Frog!",author:"Brian Tracy",kind:"book",year:2001,description:"Short, practical rules for beating procrastination by tackling your most important task first.",ideas:[["Eat the frog first","Mark Twain supposedly joked that if you eat a live frog first thing in the morning, nothing worse can happen to you for the rest of the day. Brian Tracy turns the joke into a method. Your frog is the one task you're most likely to put off and most likely to matter: the difficult email, the report, the conversation you've been rehearsing in the shower. Do it before anything else, before the inbox and the news. Two things happen. The day's biggest weight is lifted while your energy is highest, and you carry a small glow of victory into everything that follows. Today: decide tonight what tomorrow's frog is, write it on a card, and start on it before you open any app.","eat-the-frog"],["Plan every day in advance","Ten minutes of planning in the evening can save an hour of wandering the next day. When you sit down in the morning without a plan, the day plans itself, usually around whatever is loudest. Tracy recommends writing tomorrow's list the night before, in order, while today is still fresh in your mind. Something else happens too: your mind keeps working on the list while you sleep, and you often wake with solutions or a sense of readiness instead of dread. The list doesn't have to be fancy. A sheet of paper beside the kettle works. Today: before you finish work, write down the three most important things for tomorrow and put the hardest one first."],["Apply the 80/20 rule","On most lists, a couple of items carry almost all the value. Finishing the client proposal may be worth more than tidying twenty emails, yet the emails are tempting because they're quick and give a feeling of progress. The Pareto principle suggests that roughly one fifth of what we do produces most of the results. Tracy's advice is to look honestly at your list and ask which items belong to that vital few, then refuse to start on the trivial many until at least one of them is done. Busy is not the same as effective. Today: circle the two tasks on your list that would make the biggest difference if they were finished by tonight, and give them your best hours."],["Think about consequences","One useful way to rank tasks is to ask: what happens if I do this, and what happens if I don't? Studying for a certification might change your career in five years; reorganising your desktop folders changes nothing by next week. Tracy notes that successful people tend to think further ahead, and this long view makes today's choices obvious. A task with big long-term consequences deserves attention even when it feels dull, and one with no consequences can be dropped without guilt. This simple question cuts through a lot of anxious busyness. Today: look at your list and mark each item with the consequence of skipping it. Anything with no consequence can go."],["Slice the task","Huge tasks scare us because we picture all of them at once. Writing a book, cleaning out the garage, learning a language: the mind sees the whole mountain and decides to make a cup of tea instead. Tracy's trick is to slice the work thin, like salami, and commit to just one slice: one paragraph, one shelf, ten flashcards. A slice is too small to feel threatening, so you start. And once you've started, a curious thing often happens: you want to finish what you began, and one slice becomes three. Momentum is easier to keep than to create. Today: take the task you're dreading and define the smallest possible first slice. Do only that, and see where it leads."],["Single-handle each task","Every time you put down an important task and pick it up later, you pay a price: you have to remember where you were, reload the details and rebuild your focus. Do that five times and an hour's job becomes an afternoon. Tracy calls the alternative single-handling: once you've started a key task, stay with it until it's done or a natural stopping point arrives. It takes some discipline to ignore the ping of a message, but the reward is huge. Work done in one sitting is faster, better and more satisfying. Today: when you sit down to your most important task, close every other window, set a timer for an hour and stay with it until it rings."]]},{id:"parkinsons-law",title:"Parkinson's Law",author:"C. Northcote Parkinson",kind:"article",year:1955,description:"A satirical essay on bureaucracy that gave us one of the most quoted rules about time and work.",ideas:[["Work expands to fill the time","Give a student a month for an essay and it takes a month; give them a weekend and, somehow, it takes a weekend. C. Northcote Parkinson noticed that work stretches to fill whatever time we give it. With a loose deadline we polish, reconsider, wander off and come back. With a tight one we cut straight to what matters. This isn't an argument for stress; it's an argument for choosing your deadlines on purpose instead of letting them choose themselves. Often the extra time doesn't make the work better, just slower. Today: take one task you planned to spend the afternoon on and give yourself ninety minutes. Notice what you leave out, and whether anyone misses it.","parkinsons-law"],["Organizations grow regardless of work","Parkinson's essay started as a joke about the British civil service, and it's still funny because it's still true. He observed that the number of officials tended to grow year after year, whether the work itself was rising, falling or disappearing. Managers like adding subordinates, who need supervising, who then create memos and meetings for each other. Organisations grow because growth feels like importance, not because the work demands it. It's a useful lens for any team, club or household. Today: look at one recurring meeting, report or process in your life and ask honestly whether it still exists for the work or just for itself."],["The law of triviality","A committee will spend forty-five minutes arguing about the colour of a bike shed and approve a nuclear reactor in three. Parkinson called this the law of triviality. Everyone feels qualified to have an opinion on the shed, so everyone does; the reactor is so complex that people stay quiet and nod. The result is that the most important decisions often get the least scrutiny. Once you see it, you'll find it everywhere: hours on a logo, minutes on strategy. Today: in your next meeting, notice which topic draws the most debate, and gently ask whether it deserves that time compared with the big item everyone is avoiding."],["Use artificial deadlines","If work expands to fill the time available, then shrinking the time is one of the cheapest productivity tools there is. Give yourself a firm, visible deadline even when no one else has, and the work starts to organise itself around it. Writers set word sprints; cooks plan around when the guests will arrive; some people use a simple kitchen timer. The point isn't to rush. It's to stop the slow drift where a two-hour job leaks across the whole day. Today: choose one task, set a timer for half the time you'd normally allow, and see how close you get. You may be surprised how often half is enough."]]},{id:"deep-work",title:"Deep Work",author:"Cal Newport",kind:"book",year:2016,description:"Why the ability to focus without distraction is becoming rare and valuable, and how to train it.",ideas:[["Protect blocks of deep work","The most valuable things most of us produce, a clear piece of writing, a hard bug fixed, a new skill learned, all come from long stretches of unbroken focus. Cal Newport calls this deep work, and he argues that it's becoming both rarer and more valuable, because the modern workday chops our attention into tiny slices. The answer isn't willpower; it's scheduling. Block out deep-work time like an important meeting, close your email, put your phone in another room and tell people when you'll be back. The shallow stuff, replies and admin, can fill the gaps around it. Today: put a two-hour block on your calendar tomorrow for your most important work and treat it as untouchable.","deep-work"],["Attention residue","You glance at an email \"just for a second\" in the middle of writing a report. When you come back, it takes a surprising amount of time to find your thread again. Researchers call this attention residue: part of your mind stays stuck on the last thing you looked at. Each quick check leaves a little sediment, and a morning of quick checks leaves you working at half power without noticing why. Newport's response is to batch the shallow stuff into set times instead of sprinkling it everywhere. Focus isn't just about the hours you work; it's about how cleanly you move between tasks. Today: check messages only at three set times and watch your concentration in between."],["Embrace boredom","If you reach for your phone every time you queue for coffee, you're training your brain to expect a hit of novelty every few seconds. Then, when you sit down to concentrate, it rebels. Newport argues that the ability to focus is like a muscle, and constant distraction lets it waste away. The training is uncomfortable but simple: let yourself be bored. Wait in line with nothing in your hands. Walk without headphones now and then. At first your mind will itch; after a while it starts producing thoughts of its own. Today: the next time you have five idle minutes, leave your phone in your pocket and just notice what your mind does."],["Choose a depth philosophy","Not everyone can disappear into a cabin for a month to think. Newport describes several ways to build depth into a real life. Some people go monastic and cut almost all shallow work. Others use a bimodal approach, with deep weeks or days set aside. Many choose a rhythm, like two focused hours every morning at the same time, so focus becomes a habit rather than a heroic effort. A few journalists work the journalistic way, dropping into depth whenever a gap appears. The best method is the one that fits your job and family. Today: pick one of these patterns, try it for a week, and adjust it to what your days actually look like."],["Have a shutdown ritual","Without a clear end to the workday, work leaks into the evening: you answer one more email at dinner and lie awake replaying a meeting. Newport recommends a shutdown ritual. At the end of each day, review your open tasks, capture anything unfinished, glance at tomorrow's calendar, then say a fixed phrase, even something as silly as \"shutdown complete\". It sounds odd, but it gives your mind permission to let go, because it trusts that nothing has been forgotten. Rest isn't a reward for productivity; it's what makes tomorrow's focus possible. Today: before you finish work, spend ten minutes closing your loops and end with a clear signal that the workday is over."],["Quit low-value networks","Almost any app can claim some benefit: you might find a useful article, reconnect with an old friend or hear about a job. Newport says that's the wrong test. The question isn't whether a tool has any benefit, but whether its benefits clearly outweigh its costs to the few things that matter most to you. By that stronger test, many networks fail; they eat hours in exchange for scraps of value. He suggests a simple experiment: quit a service for thirty days without announcing it, then ask whether your life got worse and whether anyone noticed. Today: list the apps you open most often and ask, for each one, what it really gives back for the time it takes."]]},{id:"essentialism",title:"Essentialism",author:"Greg McKeown",kind:"book",year:2014,description:"The disciplined pursuit of less: doing fewer things, but doing the right ones much better.",ideas:[["Less but better","Picture someone who says yes to every request. They spread themselves a millimetre deep in a hundred directions, make a little progress on everything and real progress on nothing. Greg McKeown calls the alternative essentialism: doing less, but better. It means deliberately choosing the few things that truly matter and giving them your full effort, which requires the uncomfortable courage to disappoint people over the many things that don't. The trade-offs exist whether we admit them or not; the essentialist simply makes them on purpose. Today: write down the three things that matter most to you this month, and notice how much of your time currently goes to none of them."],["If it isn't a clear yes, it's a no","When an opportunity appears, McKeown suggests rating it from zero to one hundred. Anything below ninety becomes an automatic zero. It sounds extreme, and it is, because most of our overload comes from things that were \"fine\", \"probably useful\" or \"hard to say no to\". By refusing the merely good, you protect room for the rare things that are truly great, which tend to arrive when your calendar is already full. A clear yes feels obvious; a maybe is usually a no in disguise. Today: look at one commitment you're considering, give it an honest score, and if it isn't a ninety, practise a polite no."],["You always have a choice","We say \"I have to\" all day long: I have to go to that meeting, I have to answer tonight, I have to say yes. McKeown points out that almost every \"have to\" is really a choice we've stopped noticing. Admitting that is uncomfortable, because it means taking responsibility for how we spend our time, but it's also freeing. Once you see that you're choosing, you can choose differently. People who forget they have a choice let others choose for them. Today: catch yourself saying \"I have to\" and swap it for \"I choose to\". If the sentence feels wrong, it's worth asking why you're doing it."],["Create a buffer","Almost everything takes longer than we think. The trip to the airport hits traffic, the report needs one more revision, the child loses a shoe. Yet we plan as if the best case will happen every time, and spend our lives running late and apologising. McKeown recommends a simple habit: add a buffer of about fifty percent to your time estimates for anything important. It feels wasteful until you notice the calm it buys. Buffers turn small surprises from crises into shrugs, and they're the reason some people always seem unhurried. Today: take your next deadline or departure time and build in a cushion you'd normally consider excessive."],["Remove obstacles","When work stalls, the instinct is to push harder: longer hours, more effort, more coffee. McKeown suggests a different question: what is slowing this down? Often one constraint holds everything back, like an unclear decision, a missing tool or a single bottleneck person. Removing it can speed things up more than any amount of extra effort. Think of a car with the handbrake on; pressing the accelerator harder isn't the answer. The essentialist looks for the handbrake. Today: pick a project that's dragging and write down the one obstacle that, if it disappeared, would make the rest easy. Spend your next hour on that."],["Protect your sleep","The usual trade looks sensible: sleep an hour less, gain an hour of work. McKeown argues that it's a terrible bargain. Tired minds make worse decisions, notice fewer options and lose their sense of what matters, which is exactly the skill an essentialist needs most. Protecting sleep isn't laziness; it's guarding the asset that does all your choosing. Many of the most effective people treat their bedtime as non-negotiable. Today: set a firm time to stop screens tonight, and treat tomorrow's clearer head as part of your work rather than a break from it."]]},{id:"make-time",title:"Make Time",author:"Jake Knapp & John Zeratsky",kind:"book",year:2018,description:"Two former Google designers share a daily framework for focusing on what matters amid busy work and endless screens.",ideas:[["Pick a daily highlight","Most days end with a strange feeling: busy from morning to night, yet unable to say what you actually did. Jake Knapp and John Zeratsky suggest a simple anchor called the daily highlight. Each morning, choose one thing you'll make time for today. It might be finishing a presentation, a bike ride or an unhurried dinner with a friend. It doesn't replace your to-do list; it gives the day a centre. When you look back, you remember highlights, not inbox counts. Today: before breakfast, ask yourself what you'd like to be the best part of today, write it down, and plan your other hours around it."],["Laser mode","Willpower is a poor shield against apps built by teams of engineers to keep you scrolling. Knapp and Zeratsky's answer is to change the environment instead of relying on discipline. They call it laser mode: log out of social media, remove email from your phone, turn off notifications and keep the phone in another room while you work. Each step adds a little friction, and friction is often enough. You still can check things, but now it's a choice rather than a reflex. Today: delete one distracting app from your phone's home screen and see how often your thumb reaches for an empty spot."],["Busy Bandwagon and Infinity Pools","The authors describe two forces that quietly eat our days. The Busy Bandwagon is the culture of constant busyness: packed calendars, instant replies and the belief that being busy proves you're important. Infinity Pools are apps with endless content, feeds that refresh forever and video that autoplays. Both feel like the natural way things are, but they're defaults, not laws. You can step off the bandwagon and climb out of the pools by choosing different defaults. Naming them makes them easier to resist. Today: notice one moment when you're pulled into an Infinity Pool, and ask what you'd rather be doing with those twenty minutes."],["Energize with movement and real food","Focus is not just in your head; it runs on your body's energy. Knapp and Zeratsky point out that our ancestors moved all day, ate real food, napped and spent time outdoors, and our brains still work best with those basics. A short walk, a glass of water, real food instead of snacks from a packet, daylight and a little quiet do more for concentration than another productivity app. None of this is complicated, which is exactly why it's easy to ignore. Today: take a ten-minute walk outside before your hardest task, and notice whether it's easier to begin when you come back."],["Reflect each night","There is no perfect system for everyone, so the book treats each day as a small experiment. Before bed, jot down whether you made time for your highlight, what helped and what got in the way. Did laser mode work? Did a long lunch drain your afternoon? Over a few weeks these notes reveal patterns no expert could tell you, and your routine slowly fits your real life rather than an ideal one. Reflection turns ordinary days into feedback. Today: before sleep, write three short lines: what was your highlight, what helped, what would you change tomorrow."]]},{id:"four-thousand-weeks",title:"Four Thousand Weeks",author:"Oliver Burkeman",kind:"book",year:2021,description:"Time management for mortals: accepting that you will never get everything done, and choosing what matters anyway.",ideas:[["You will never clear the list","If you live to eighty, you get roughly four thousand weeks. Oliver Burkeman uses that startling number to make a liberating point: you will never get everything done. There will always be more emails, more books, more ambitions and more good causes than hours to give them. Most productivity advice promises that one day, with enough efficiency, you'll catch up. You won't, and accepting that is a relief. The question changes from \"how do I do it all?\" to \"what will I choose to do, knowing I can't?\" Today: write down three things you'll knowingly leave undone this week, and notice how much lighter the rest of the list feels."],["The efficiency trap","Here's the cruel twist of efficiency: the faster you clear your inbox, the more replies you trigger, and the more email you get. Become known as the person who gets things done, and you'll be given more things to do. Burkeman calls this the efficiency trap. Speed doesn't create spare time; it creates more demands. The way out isn't to work faster but to decide in advance what you'll neglect, on purpose, so that something important gets your attention. Today: pick one category of task, say low-priority emails, and let it pile up a little. See whether the sky falls."],["Pay yourself first, in time","If you wait until the urgent work is done before starting on what truly matters to you, that time never comes. The novel, the course, the difficult conversation all wait for a gap that never appears. Burkeman borrows a rule from personal finance: pay yourself first. Give your most important project the first and best hour of the day, before the world gets its hands on you, even if it's only thirty minutes. The rest can expand to fill whatever remains. Today: set your alarm half an hour earlier tomorrow and spend that time on the thing you keep saying you'll get to someday."],["Limit work in progress","Juggling fifteen half-finished projects feels productive but rarely is; each one progresses so slowly that none brings real satisfaction. Burkeman suggests a fixed limit on work in progress, for example three active projects at a time. Nothing new starts until one of them is finished or deliberately abandoned. The constraint forces honest choices, the kind we usually avoid, and it means the projects you do choose actually move forward. Today: list everything you're currently \"working on\", choose the three that matter most, and formally pause the rest. Write them on a waiting list so they're not lost, just not active."],["Cosmic insignificance therapy","Look up at the night sky and your worries shrink. Burkeman calls this cosmic insignificance therapy, and he means it kindly. In the vastness of time, almost nothing we do will be remembered, and that takes the crushing pressure off. You don't need to write a masterpiece or change the world to have lived a meaningful life. Cooking a good meal, raising a decent kid or making a friend laugh already counts. Ordinary life, done with attention, is enough. Today: do one small, ordinary thing with your full attention, like making tea or walking to the shop, and let that be sufficient."],["Practice doing nothing","Sit still for five minutes, with no phone and no goal, and resist every urge to fix, plan or improve anything. Most of us find it surprisingly hard. Burkeman suggests this practice of doing nothing as training for a deeper skill: tolerating discomfort. A lot of procrastination and distraction comes from fleeing small discomforts like boredom, uncertainty or the hard first minutes of a task. Learn to sit with them and focus becomes much easier. Today: set a timer for five minutes, sit by a window and do nothing at all. Notice the itch to do something, and let it pass."]]},{id:"the-one-thing",title:"The ONE Thing",author:"Gary Keller & Jay Papasan",kind:"book",year:2013,description:"Extraordinary results come from narrowing your focus to the single most important task.",ideas:[["The focusing question","When everything feels important, nothing gets your best. Gary Keller and Jay Papasan offer one question to cut through the noise: \"What's the one thing I can do such that, by doing it, everything else will be easier or unnecessary?\" It works at every scale: for today, this week, this year, your relationship, your health. Asked honestly, it often points to something uncomfortable, a hard phone call or a skill you've been avoiding, because the lead domino is rarely the easiest one. Today: ask yourself the focusing question about your work this week, write down the answer and do it before anything else tomorrow."],["The domino effect","A single domino can knock over another about half again as large, and a line of them growing step by step can eventually topple something astonishingly big. Keller uses this image to describe how extraordinary results are built: not by doing everything at once, but by lining up the right small things in the right order and knocking them over one at a time. Today's small win makes tomorrow's bigger one possible. Success is sequential, not simultaneous. Today: think about a big goal and identify the very first small domino in the chain. Push that one over, even if it's tiny."],["Multitasking is a lie","You can walk and chew gum, but you can't really focus on two demanding things at once. When we multitask, we're actually switching rapidly between tasks, and every switch costs time, energy and accuracy. Keller points to the familiar experience of reading an email while on a phone call and remembering neither. What feels like efficiency is a slow leak. Giving one task your full attention is faster, and the work is usually better. Today: when you sit down to something important, close every other tab and silence your phone. See how much you get done in an hour of genuinely single-minded work."],["Time block your one thing","Knowing your most important task isn't enough; you have to protect the time to do it. Keller suggests time blocking, reserving a large chunk of the day, ideally four hours, for your one thing, and defending it like an appointment with someone you'd never cancel on. Meetings, messages and favours can fit around it. People who build extraordinary results usually aren't superhuman; they simply spend their best hours on what matters most. Today: open your calendar, block out tomorrow morning for your one thing, and give the block a name so it feels as real as any meeting."],["Willpower is a battery","Willpower works like a phone battery: full in the morning, drained by evening. Every decision, temptation and frustration uses a bit of it, which is why we eat badly, snap at people and procrastinate later in the day. Keller's advice is to do your most important work early, while the battery is full, and to avoid wasting willpower on trivial decisions, for example by keeping routines for meals or clothes. Don't schedule your hardest thinking for four in the afternoon and then blame yourself for failing. Today: move your most demanding task to the first part of your day and keep evenings for easier work."]]}]}},896,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,a,n,s,i){n.exports={topic:{id:"psychology",name:"Psychology",emoji:"\ud83e\udde0",color:"#4B3F72"},books:[{id:"atomic-habits",title:"Atomic Habits",author:"James Clear",kind:"book",year:2018,description:"How tiny changes in behaviour compound into remarkable results, with a simple four-step model of how habits work.",ideas:[["Stack new habits on old ones","The hardest part of a new habit is remembering it. James Clear's fix is to hang it on a hook that already exists. You already pour coffee every morning, brush your teeth and sit down on the train, so anchor the new behaviour to one of them: \"After I pour my coffee, I'll write one line in my journal.\" The old routine becomes a reliable cue, and you no longer depend on memory or motivation. Over time you can chain several habits together into a small morning ritual. Today: pick one habit you want and write a sentence in the form \"After I ___, I will ___.\" Stick it somewhere you'll see it.","habit-stacking"],["Tiny gains compound","Improving by one percent a day feels like nothing; on any single day you won't notice it. But small improvements compound like interest, and over a year they add up to something remarkable. The same is true in reverse: tiny bad choices repeated daily quietly add up to a big problem. Clear's point is that results are a lagging measure of habits. Your weight, your savings and your skills today reflect what you did repeatedly in the past. Fix the daily system and the outcomes follow. Today: choose one thing you could do one percent better, such as five more minutes of reading, and do it again tomorrow.","one-percent"],["The four laws of behaviour change","Why do some habits stick while others vanish within a week? Clear breaks every habit into a loop: a cue, a craving, a response and a reward. To build a good habit, make it obvious, attractive, easy and satisfying: put the running shoes by the door, pair the workout with a favourite podcast, start with five minutes and tick off a calendar afterwards. To break a bad habit, flip each law: make it invisible, unattractive, difficult and unsatisfying. Most failures come from fighting one of these four forces without realising it. Today: take a habit you're struggling with and ask which of the four laws you could change."],["Identity-based habits","\"I'm trying to quit smoking\" and \"I'm not a smoker\" sound similar, but they come from different places. The first is a goal you're fighting for; the second is an identity you're living. Clear argues that the deepest habit change starts with who you believe you are. Each action is a small vote for a type of person: every workout is a vote for \"I'm someone who exercises\", every page written is a vote for \"I'm a writer\". You don't need a unanimous vote, just a majority. Today: decide who you want to become in one area of life and cast a single small vote for that person."],["The two-minute version","\"Read every night\" is a big promise; \"read one page\" is almost impossible to fail. Clear's two-minute rule says to scale any new habit down until it takes about two minutes to do. Put on your running shoes rather than run five miles; write one sentence rather than a chapter. It sounds too small to matter, but the point is to master the art of showing up. A habit has to exist before it can be improved, and the tiny version builds the identity and the routine. Often, once you've started, you'll keep going. Today: shrink one habit to its two-minute version and do it, even if it feels silly."],["Design your environment","Motivation is unreliable; environments are not. If the biscuits are on the counter, you'll eat biscuits. If the guitar is in its case in the cupboard, you won't play it. Clear suggests designing your surroundings so the good choice is the obvious, easy one: fruit in a bowl at eye level, the book on your pillow, the phone charging in the kitchen overnight. Disciplined people often aren't more heroic; they've just arranged their lives so that they face fewer temptations. Today: make one small change to a room so a good habit becomes easier and a bad one a little harder."]]},{id:"thinking-fast-slow",title:"Thinking, Fast and Slow",author:"Daniel Kahneman",kind:"book",year:2011,description:"A Nobel-winning psychologist explains the two systems that drive how we think, and the biases that trip us up.",ideas:[["Two systems of thinking","Read \"2 + 2\" and the answer appears effortlessly. Read \"17 \xd7 24\" and you feel yourself slow down and strain. Daniel Kahneman calls these two modes System 1 and System 2. System 1 is fast, automatic and intuitive, brilliant at recognising faces and reading moods. System 2 is slow, effortful and logical, needed for sums and careful decisions. Trouble comes when System 1 answers a question that really needed System 2, and does it with complete confidence. Most of our biases live in that gap. Today: when a big decision feels obvious straight away, pause and ask your slow brain to check the fast one's work.","system-1-2"],["Anchoring","In one experiment, people spun a wheel that landed on a random number, then guessed how many African countries were in the United Nations. Those who saw a high number guessed higher. The random number shouldn't have mattered, but it did. Kahneman calls this anchoring: the first number we encounter pulls our estimates toward it, even when we know it's meaningless. Shops use it with \"was \xa3200, now \xa3120\"; negotiators use it by naming a price first. Today: before you hear the first offer in a negotiation or see a price tag, decide your own number. It protects you from someone else's anchor."],["Losses loom larger than gains","Losing \xa350 hurts more than finding \xa350 feels good, roughly twice as much, according to Kahneman and Tversky's research. This loss aversion shapes behaviour in surprising ways. We hold on to falling shares to avoid locking in a loss, keep subscriptions we never use, and turn down fair bets because the downside feels bigger than the upside. Knowing about the bias doesn't remove it, but you can reframe decisions. Ask what you'd choose if you were starting fresh today. Today: look at one thing you keep only because giving it up feels like losing, and ask whether you'd choose it again from scratch."],["What you see is all there is","Our minds are storytellers. Given a few facts, they build a confident, coherent story and rarely ask what's missing. Kahneman calls this \"what you see is all there is\". Hear one glowing review of a restaurant and you assume it's great; meet someone charming for five minutes and you assume they're trustworthy. The less we know, the easier it is to make the story neat, which is why confidence and accuracy often have little to do with each other. Today: before your next judgment about a person or decision, write down one thing you'd need to know but currently don't."],["The planning fallacy","Ask people how long their kitchen renovation will take and they'll describe the plan; ask how long renovations usually take and you'll get a far more realistic answer. Kahneman calls the gap the planning fallacy: we imagine our project going smoothly and ignore how long similar projects took for others. The cure is to look outward first. Find the base rate, the typical outcome for comparable cases, and only then adjust for what's special about yours. Today: for your next project estimate, ask yourself how long something like this took last time, or for someone you know, and start from that number."],["The experiencing and remembering self","Two people have the same painful medical procedure. One's ends quickly at its worst moment; the other's lasts longer but tapers off gently. Surprisingly, the second patient remembers it as less unpleasant. Kahneman found that memory isn't a recording; it judges experiences mostly by their peak and their ending, largely ignoring duration. The self that lives through moments and the self that remembers them want different things. This is why a holiday with a lovely last day feels better in hindsight. Today: if you're planning something for someone, put care into how it ends. That's the part they'll keep."]]},{id:"influence",title:"Influence",author:"Robert Cialdini",kind:"book",year:1984,description:"The classic study of the psychology of persuasion and the six principles that make people say yes.",ideas:[["Reciprocity","A waiter who brings two mints with the bill gets noticeably bigger tips than one who brings none. Robert Cialdini explains why: reciprocity is one of the deepest social rules. When someone gives us something, we feel a strong pull to give something back, even when we didn't ask for the gift. That's why free samples, small favours and helpful tips are so effective in marketing. Used honestly, it's also the heart of good relationships: generosity creates goodwill. Used cynically, it's a hook. Today: notice the next time a small gift nudges you to say yes, and then think about how you could give first, sincerely."],["Commitment and consistency","Once we've said or done something publicly, we feel pressure to stay consistent with it. Cialdini describes a study in which homeowners who first agreed to put a tiny sign in their window were later far more likely to accept a huge, ugly billboard in their garden. The small yes changed how they saw themselves. This is why salespeople ask for small commitments first. It also works in your favour: write down a goal and tell a friend, and you're more likely to follow through. Today: make one small, public commitment to something you want to do, and let your need for consistency carry you."],["Social proof","Laugh tracks on sitcoms are irritating, yet they make audiences laugh more. When we're unsure what to do, we look at what others are doing and copy them. Cialdini calls this social proof. It's usually a useful shortcut, since crowds often know something, but it's easy to exploit with phrases like \"most popular\" and \"thousands of satisfied customers\". It's also why everyone in a crowd can fail to help someone in trouble: each person waits for the others. Today: when you catch yourself choosing something because \"everyone\" does, pause and ask whether it fits you."],["Liking","We're more easily persuaded by people we like, and we like people who are similar to us, who pay us compliments and who cooperate with us. Cialdini tells of a car salesman who became famously successful largely by sending every customer a simple greeting card every month. Liking isn't a trick in itself; genuine warmth makes cooperation easier. But it means we should be careful when we feel unusually fond of someone who's trying to sell us something. Today: in your next important decision, separate how you feel about the person from how you feel about the offer."],["Authority","In one famous series of experiments, ordinary people were willing to deliver what they believed were painful electric shocks simply because a man in a lab coat told them to continue. Cialdini shows how strongly we defer to signs of authority: titles, uniforms, expensive suits and confident language. Deferring to real experts is usually sensible, since we can't check everything ourselves, but the symbols of authority are easy to fake. Today: when someone's credentials are persuading you, ask two questions. Is this person truly an expert on this specific thing, and how honest are they likely to be with me?"],["Scarcity","The cookie from a nearly empty jar tastes better than the identical cookie from a full one. That's not just a feeling; in experiments, people rate it more highly. Scarcity makes things seem more valuable, and the threat of losing a chance hits us harder than the chance itself. \"Only two rooms left\" and \"offer ends at midnight\" are designed to rush us past careful thought. Today: when you feel the pressure of a countdown or a shrinking supply, ask yourself whether you'd want this just as much if there were plenty of it."]]},{id:"mindset",title:"Mindset",author:"Carol S. Dweck",kind:"book",year:2006,description:"How believing that abilities can grow, rather than being fixed, changes how we learn, work and relate to others.",ideas:[["Fixed versus growth mindset","Two students fail the same test. One concludes, \"I'm just not a maths person.\" The other thinks, \"I need a different way to study.\" Carol Dweck calls these the fixed and growth mindsets. With a fixed mindset, ability is a trait you have or don't, so every challenge is a risky exam of your worth. With a growth mindset, ability is something you build, so challenges become chances to improve. The mindset you hold shapes whether you seek out hard things or avoid them. Today: catch one fixed-mindset thought about yourself and rewrite it as something you could get better at."],["Praise effort, not intelligence","In Dweck's studies, children who were praised for being clever after a puzzle tended to choose easy puzzles next time, to protect the label. Children praised for their effort and strategy chose harder ones and enjoyed them more. Praise for intelligence teaches people that mistakes reveal a lack of talent; praise for process teaches them that mistakes are part of learning. The same applies to adults at work. Today: when you praise someone, a child, a colleague or yourself, point to what they did, such as the practice, the approach or the persistence, rather than what they supposedly are."],["The power of \"yet\"","\"I can't do this\" is a verdict. \"I can't do this yet\" is a progress report. Dweck describes a school that began giving the grade \"not yet\" instead of a fail, and saw students treat it as a step on a path rather than a dead end. One small word shifts attention from what you are to where you're heading. It's a simple tool, but it changes how a setback feels in your body. Today: listen for moments when you say you can't do something, and quietly add \"yet\" at the end. Then name the next step that would get you closer."],["Failure as information","For someone with a fixed mindset, failure is a mirror: it reveals that they're not good enough. With a growth mindset, it's a map: it shows exactly what to work on next. Dweck's research found that people who treat setbacks as information recover faster, try new strategies and end up achieving more. That doesn't mean failure stops hurting. It means the hurt is followed by curiosity. Today: think about a recent disappointment and write down two things it taught you, plus one thing you'll try differently next time."],["Mindsets can be mixed","No one has a pure growth mindset. You might be wonderfully open to learning at work and completely fixed about drawing, dancing or speaking another language. Dweck stresses that mindsets are mixed and that a fixed-mindset voice is normal; it often shows up when we're criticised, when someone else is better or when we try something new. The goal isn't to pretend you never feel it, but to notice what triggers it and answer back. Today: think of an area where you've decided you're just bad at it, and spend fifteen minutes practising it anyway."]]},{id:"mans-search-for-meaning",title:"Man's Search for Meaning",author:"Viktor E. Frankl",kind:"book",year:1946,description:"A psychiatrist's account of surviving Nazi concentration camps, and the therapy he built around finding meaning.",ideas:[["The last human freedom","Viktor Frankl, a psychiatrist imprisoned in Nazi concentration camps, saw almost everything taken from people: their families, their names, their health, their dignity. Yet he noticed that some prisoners still shared their last piece of bread or offered a kind word. He concluded that one freedom remains even in the worst conditions: the freedom to choose our attitude toward what happens to us. We can't always control events, but we can decide how we meet them. Today: when something frustrating happens, pause before reacting and ask, \"How do I want to meet this?\" That small space is yours."],["Meaning makes suffering bearable","In the camps, Frankl observed that people who held onto a reason to live, a child waiting at home, a book to finish or a loved one to see again, often endured what broke others. He came to believe that suffering itself isn't what destroys us; suffering without meaning does. When we can see a purpose in what we're going through, even pain becomes bearable. This doesn't mean pain is good, only that meaning can carry us through it. Today: if you're struggling with something hard, ask what or who you're enduring it for. Naming that reason can give you strength."],["Three sources of meaning","Frankl suggested three places where people find meaning. The first is creating something or doing a deed: work, art or service. The second is experiencing something or loving someone: beauty, nature, truth, another person. The third is the attitude we take toward suffering we cannot avoid. This means meaning is open to everyone, not only to the successful or the lucky. A nurse, a grandparent and a patient in hospital can all find it. Today: think about where meaning shows up in your life now, through what you make, whom you love and how you face hardship, and give a little more time to one of them."],["Life asks the questions","Many people wait for life to tell them its meaning, as if it were a hidden answer to discover. Frankl turned the question around: stop asking what you expect from life and ask what life expects from you. Meaning, he argued, is found in responding well to the specific situations in front of us: this person who needs help, this task only you can do, this moment. It's practical, not abstract. Today: instead of asking what your purpose is, ask what this day is asking of you, and answer it as well as you can."],["Success as a side effect","Frankl warned that happiness and success can't be pursued directly. The harder you aim at them, the more they slip away, like trying to fall asleep on command. Instead, they come as side effects of dedicating yourself to something bigger than yourself: a cause, a craft or another person. When you lose yourself in that, happiness tends to arrive uninvited. Today: shift your focus from \"How can I be happier?\" to \"What can I give myself to?\" Spend an hour on something you care about, and let happiness take care of itself."]]},{id:"predictably-irrational",title:"Predictably Irrational",author:"Dan Ariely",kind:"book",year:2008,description:"Experiments showing our irrational choices aren't random: they follow patterns we can learn to spot.",ideas:[["The decoy effect","A magazine once offered three subscriptions: web-only for $59, print-only for $125, and print plus web for $125. Almost nobody picked print-only, but its presence made print plus web look like a bargain, and that's what most people chose. Dan Ariely calls the useless middle option a decoy. When we can't judge value directly, we compare, and a cleverly placed worse option steers us toward the one the seller wants. You'll see decoys in coffee sizes, phone plans and restaurant menus. Today: next time you face three options, ask whether one exists only to make another look good."],["The power of free","Offer people a fancy chocolate for 15 cents or a basic one for 1 cent and many will choose the fancy one. Drop both prices by one cent, making the basic chocolate free, and most suddenly switch to the free one, even though the price difference is identical. Ariely shows that zero isn't just a low price; it's an emotional trigger that makes us forget the downside. Free shipping that requires spending more and free trials that roll into subscriptions both work this way. Today: when something is free, ask what it really costs you in time, attention, data or future spending."],["Social norms versus market norms","Help a friend move house and you'll do it gladly. Offer to pay them \xa310 and they may feel insulted. Ariely describes two worlds: social norms, built on goodwill and favours, and market norms, built on prices and contracts. Mixing them causes trouble. In one study, a nursery fined parents for picking children up late, and lateness increased, because the fine turned guilt into a fee. Once money enters a relationship, it's hard to get the warmth back. Today: be careful about putting a price on things that work through goodwill, at home and at work."],["The IKEA effect","People who assembled simple IKEA boxes themselves were willing to pay much more for them than for identical boxes someone else had built, even when their own versions were wobbly. Ariely calls this the IKEA effect: effort creates attachment. It explains why we cling to our own ideas in meetings, love our home-made bread and overvalue projects we've sunk work into. It's also why involving people in creating something makes them care about it. Today: when you feel defensive about one of your ideas, ask whether it's better, or just yours."],["Keeping doors open","In one experiment, people played a game with several doors that slowly shrank and disappeared unless they kept clicking on them. Players wasted effort keeping useless doors open, even when it cost them money. Ariely uses this to show how much we hate losing options: we keep too many projects, subscriptions and maybes alive, and spread ourselves thin. Choosing something means closing other doors, and that's not a loss but the price of focus. Today: pick one option you've been keeping open out of fear rather than real interest, and close it deliberately."]]},{id:"openstax-psychology",title:"Psychology 2e",author:"Rose M. Spielman et al. (OpenStax)",kind:"book",year:2020,description:"A free, peer-reviewed introductory psychology textbook covering the brain, learning, memory, emotion, personality and mental health.",free:{url:"https://openstax.org/details/books/psychology-2e",license:"CC BY 4.0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/",note:"Free from OpenStax at Rice University, online and as a PDF."},ideas:[["Psychology is a science","It's tempting to think psychology is mostly common sense. But common sense says both that birds of a feather flock together and that opposites attract. Psychology tests such claims with careful methods: observation, surveys and controlled experiments. A crucial lesson is that correlation doesn't prove causation. Ice-cream sales and drowning both rise in summer, but one doesn't cause the other; hot weather drives both. Only well-designed experiments can show cause and effect. Today: the next time you read a headline claiming one thing causes another, ask whether it's just two things that happen together."],["Memory is reconstructive","Memory doesn't work like a video camera. Each time you recall an event, you rebuild it from fragments, and it can quietly change in the process. In classic studies, people who watched a car crash and were asked how fast the cars were going when they \"smashed\" remembered seeing broken glass that wasn't there. This is why eyewitnesses can be confident and wrong, and why family members remember the same holiday differently. Today: when you and someone else remember something differently, consider that you might both be partly right, and partly reconstructing."],["Learning through consequences","A rat presses a lever and gets food, so it presses again. A child tidies up and gets praise, so tidying becomes more likely. This is operant conditioning: behaviours followed by rewards tend to be repeated, and behaviours followed by unpleasant results tend to fade. Interestingly, rewards that come unpredictably, like a slot machine or a social media feed, create the most persistent habits. Understanding this helps you shape your own behaviour, and notice when apps are shaping it for you. Today: reward yourself immediately after finishing one difficult task, and notice whether it gets easier to start next time."],["Nature and nurture interact","Is intelligence born or made? The modern answer is: both, together. Genes set tendencies and ranges, while environment, nutrition, education and relationships influence how those genes are expressed. Studies of identical twins raised apart show striking similarities and important differences. Very few traits come purely from nature or purely from nurture. This is hopeful: your starting point isn't your destiny, and the environments you choose and create matter. Today: think of a trait you've assumed was simply \"how you are\", and consider which parts of your environment might be shaping it."],["Stress affects the whole body","Stress isn't only a feeling; it's a whole-body response. When we face a threat, hormones like cortisol and adrenaline speed up the heart and sharpen attention, useful for escaping a predator. But when stress lasts for weeks or months, that same response can weaken the immune system, disturb sleep and raise the risk of illness. Psychologists have found that social support, exercise, adequate sleep and a sense of control all soften stress's effects. Today: pick one stress buffer, such as a walk, a call with a friend or an early night, and give it to yourself on purpose."]]}]}},897,[]);
__d(function(e,o,t,a,n,i,s){n.exports={topic:{id:"relationships",name:"Relationships",emoji:"\u2764\ufe0f",color:"#7A3E5E"},books:[{id:"the-five-love-languages",title:"The 5 Love Languages",author:"Gary Chapman",kind:"book",year:1992,description:"A simple framework for understanding how different people give and receive love.",ideas:[["People feel love differently","Gary Chapman noticed that couples often love each other deeply yet feel unloved, because they express love in different ways. He described five love languages: words of affirmation, quality time, gifts, acts of service and physical touch. Most people have one or two that matter most. Someone who longs for quality time may barely notice a gift; someone who values acts of service may feel cherished when you take out the bins. Today: think about which love language you speak most naturally, and which one makes you feel most loved."],["Speak their language, not yours","We tend to show love the way we'd like to receive it. If you feel loved through words, you might write long messages, while your partner, who values acts of service, wishes you'd simply help with the washing up. Chapman encourages learning the other person's language and speaking it, even if it doesn't come naturally. Today: ask someone close to you what makes them feel most appreciated, and do one small thing in that language this week."],["Keep the love tank full","Chapman uses the image of a love tank: when people feel loved in their own language, the tank fills, and they handle stress, disagreements and hard days much better. When it runs empty, small irritations become big fights. Small daily gestures, a kind word, ten minutes of attention or a hug, keep it topped up. Today: do one small thing to fill the love tank of someone you care about, before any problem arises."],["Love is a choice","The early rush of infatuation fades in every relationship, often within a couple of years. Chapman argues that lasting love isn't a feeling that happens to us but a choice we make every day: choosing to care for someone in ways that matter to them. That choice can rekindle feelings, not just follow them. Today: choose one act of love for a partner, friend or family member that you'll do whether or not you feel like it."]]},{id:"attached",title:"Attached",author:"Amir Levine & Rachel Heller",kind:"book",year:2010,description:"How attachment theory explains the way we behave in romantic relationships.",ideas:[["Three attachment styles","Attachment theory, described by Amir Levine and Rachel Heller, suggests that adults fall into roughly three styles in relationships. Secure people feel comfortable with closeness and trust easily. Anxious people crave closeness and worry about losing it. Avoidant people value independence highly and tend to pull away when things get too close. Knowing your style, and your partner's, can explain patterns that otherwise feel mysterious. Today: think about how you react when a partner or close friend goes quiet for a while. What does that reveal about your style?"],["Needing others isn't weakness","Many of us were taught that needing others is a weakness. Levine and Heller argue the opposite: depending on a partner is part of being human, and secure relationships actually make people more independent, not less. When you know someone has your back, you feel braver exploring the world. Today: let someone you trust help you with something, without apologising for needing it."],["The anxious-avoidant trap","Anxious and avoidant people often attract each other, Levine and Heller observe, and then trigger each other's fears. The anxious partner reaches for closeness, the avoidant partner pulls back, which makes the anxious partner reach harder. The cycle can feel like passion but is often painful. Recognising it is the first step to breaking it. Today: if you see this pattern in a relationship, name it gently with your partner, without blame."],["Effective communication","Saying clearly and calmly what you need, \"I feel better when we text during the day\", isn't needy; it's effective. Levine and Heller suggest that this kind of open communication quickly shows you who is willing and able to meet your needs. A good partner will respond with care; someone who mocks or ignores you is giving you important information. Today: express one need clearly to someone close to you, and notice how they respond."]]},{id:"seven-principles-marriage",title:"The Seven Principles for Making Marriage Work",author:"John M. Gottman & Nan Silver",kind:"book",year:1999,description:"A relationship researcher's evidence-based guide to what makes couples last.",ideas:[["The four horsemen","John Gottman, who has studied thousands of couples, identified four behaviours that strongly predict break-ups: criticism, contempt, defensiveness and stonewalling. He called them the four horsemen. Contempt, treating your partner as beneath you through sarcasm, eye-rolling and mockery, is the most damaging. The good news is that each has an antidote, like gentle complaints instead of criticism and appreciation instead of contempt. Today: notice if any of the four horsemen show up in your next disagreement, and choose the softer alternative."],["Turn toward bids","Partners constantly make small bids for attention: \"Look at that bird\", \"Guess what happened at work?\", a hand on the shoulder. Gottman found that couples who stay together turn toward these bids far more often, by responding with interest even briefly, while couples heading for trouble tend to ignore or dismiss them. These tiny moments build or erode connection over time. Today: notice the small bids people around you make, and respond warmly to as many as you can."],["Love maps","Strong couples, Gottman found, know each other's worlds in detail: their worries, their friends, their dreams and their stresses this week. He calls this a love map. Maps go out of date as lives change, so they need updating with curious questions. Couples with detailed love maps cope better with difficult times. Today: ask your partner or a close friend an open question about something you don't know about their life right now."],["Five to one","In stable couples, Gottman observed roughly five positive interactions for every negative one, even during conflict. Positive doesn't have to mean grand; it can be a joke, a touch, a nod or a moment of agreement. Kindness doesn't need to be dramatic, just frequent. A relationship with plenty of small positives can absorb the occasional argument. Today: add a few small positive moments to your interactions with someone close, like thanks, compliments or humour."],["Some problems are permanent","Gottman found that many disagreements in relationships never fully go away. They come from deep differences in personality or values, like neatness, money or how social to be. Successful couples don't solve these perpetual problems; they learn to talk about them with humour, acceptance and understanding, instead of getting stuck in gridlock. Today: think of a recurring disagreement in one of your relationships, and approach it with curiosity about the other person's view rather than a goal of winning."]]},{id:"the-art-of-loving",title:"The Art of Loving",author:"Erich Fromm",kind:"book",year:1956,description:"A psychologist argues that love is a skill to be learned, not a feeling we fall into.",ideas:[["Love is an art","We often think of love as something we fall into, a matter of luck and finding the right person. Erich Fromm argued that love is an art, like music or painting: it requires knowledge, practice, patience and effort. We spend a lot of energy on being lovable and very little on learning how to love. Today: think of one way to practise loving better, such as listening more carefully, being more patient or showing more appreciation, and practise it this week."],["Love is active","For Fromm, love isn't primarily about receiving; it's about giving: care, responsibility, respect and knowledge of the other person. Giving, in this sense, isn't sacrifice but an expression of strength and aliveness. Love is something we do, not just something we feel. Today: do one active act of love for someone, like taking care of something for them, learning more about their world or showing respect for their choices."],["Mature love keeps two people whole","Immature love says, \"I love you because I need you.\" Mature love says, \"I need you because I love you.\" Fromm described mature love as a union that preserves each person's individuality and integrity. Two whole people choose each other, rather than two halves clinging together. Today: reflect on one relationship and ask whether it lets both people grow, or whether one of you is shrinking to fit."],["Self-love isn't selfishness","Fromm insisted that loving yourself isn't selfishness. Selfish people, he argued, often don't love themselves at all; they're anxious and empty. Genuine self-respect and care for your own growth are the ground from which you can love others well. You can't give what you don't have. Today: do one thing that shows care for yourself, like rest, a healthy meal or setting a boundary, without feeling guilty about it."]]},{id:"the-good-life",title:"The Good Life",author:"Robert Waldinger & Marc Schulz",kind:"book",year:2023,description:"Lessons from the Harvard Study of Adult Development, which has followed lives for over 80 years.",ideas:[["Relationships keep us healthy","The Harvard Study of Adult Development has followed hundreds of people for more than eighty years. Robert Waldinger and Marc Schulz, its current directors, report a clear finding: the quality of people's relationships predicted their health and happiness in old age better than wealth, fame or even cholesterol levels. People who felt connected at fifty were healthier at eighty. Today: think about which relationships nourish you most, and plan time with one of those people this week."],["Loneliness is a health risk","Chronic loneliness isn't just painful; it's a real health risk, linked to poorer sleep, weaker immunity and shorter lives. The researchers note that you can feel lonely in a crowd and connected when alone; what matters is the quality of our connections, not the number. Social connection deserves as much attention as diet and exercise. Today: reach out to someone you haven't spoken to in a while, just to say you were thinking of them."],["Social fitness","Waldinger and Schulz suggest thinking of social fitness like physical fitness. Relationships need regular exercise: checking in, making time, showing interest and working through conflicts. Neglected, they slowly weaken. It's easy to assume friendships will take care of themselves, but they rarely do. Today: do a quick social fitness check. Which relationships have you neglected lately, and what's one small step to strengthen one of them?"],["Small moments matter","Not all connection needs to be deep. Brief, warm interactions, a chat with a neighbour, a joke with a barista or a friendly word to a colleague, boost mood and sense of belonging more than people expect. The study's authors point out that these small moments add up across a life. Today: start a short, friendly conversation with someone you usually just pass by."]]}]}},898,[]);
__d(function(e,t,a,o,i,n,s){i.exports={topic:{id:"science",name:"Science",emoji:"\ud83d\udd2d",color:"#234E70"},books:[{id:"a-brief-history-of-time",title:"A Brief History of Time",author:"Stephen Hawking",kind:"book",year:1988,description:"The physicist's best-selling tour of the universe, from the Big Bang to black holes.",ideas:[["The universe had a beginning","In the 1920s, astronomers discovered that distant galaxies are rushing away from us, and the further away they are, the faster they're moving. Stephen Hawking explains what that implies: run the film of the universe backwards and everything converges to an incredibly hot, dense starting point about 13.8 billion years ago, the Big Bang. The universe, it seems, had a beginning. Today: next time you look at the night sky, remember that the light from some of those stars set off before humans existed."],["Black holes","When a very massive star runs out of fuel, it can collapse under its own gravity until it becomes a black hole, a region where gravity is so strong that not even light can escape. The boundary of no return is called the event horizon. Hawking describes how, near a black hole, time itself slows down relative to the outside. It sounds like science fiction, but astronomers have now photographed the shadow of one. Today: look up the first image of a black hole, taken in 2019, and consider how strange and real it is."],["Hawking radiation","Hawking's most famous discovery was that black holes aren't completely black. Thanks to quantum effects near the event horizon, they slowly emit a faint glow, now called Hawking radiation, and over unimaginably long times they could evaporate entirely. It was a remarkable bridge between the physics of the very large and the very small. Today: remember that even the most permanent-seeming things in the universe slowly change, and let that perspective soften one worry."],["Space and time are one","Einstein's theory of relativity joined space and time into a single fabric called spacetime. Massive objects like the Sun bend this fabric, and what we feel as gravity is objects following those curves, like a marble rolling around a dip in a trampoline. Hawking explains how this means time runs slightly differently at different heights and speeds. GPS satellites must correct for it to stay accurate. Today: next time you use a map on your phone, remember that it works partly because Einstein was right about time."]]},{id:"cosmos",title:"Cosmos",author:"Carl Sagan",kind:"book",year:1980,description:"A poetic journey through the history of science and our place in the universe.",ideas:[["We are made of star stuff","The carbon in your cells, the oxygen in your lungs and the iron in your blood were forged inside stars that lived and died long before the Sun existed. Their explosions scattered these elements across space, where they eventually became planets and people. Carl Sagan loved to point out that we are, quite literally, made of star stuff, a way for the universe to know itself. Today: next time you feel small, remember that you're made of the same material as the stars."],["Science as a candle in the dark","For Sagan, science wasn't a collection of facts but a method for not fooling ourselves. Test ideas against evidence, be willing to change your mind and be suspicious of claims that can't be tested. In a world full of misinformation, this way of thinking is like a candle in the dark. It's available to everyone, not just scientists. Today: when you hear a surprising claim, ask what evidence supports it and how you could check."],["The cosmic calendar","Sagan suggested a vivid way to grasp cosmic time. Imagine the entire history of the universe squeezed into one calendar year. The Big Bang happens on 1 January; the Milky Way forms in May; the Earth appears in September; dinosaurs arrive on Christmas Day. All of recorded human history fits into the last few seconds before midnight on 31 December. Today: think of something that's been stressing you, and place it on that cosmic calendar. It might feel a little lighter."],["Eratosthenes measured the Earth","Over two thousand years ago, a Greek scholar named Eratosthenes noticed that at noon on a certain day, the Sun shone straight down a well in one Egyptian city while casting a shadow in another. Using the angle of the shadow and the distance between the cities, he estimated the size of the entire Earth with impressive accuracy. Sagan uses the story to show how much careful observation and simple reasoning can achieve. Today: notice something ordinary around you and ask what it could tell you if you looked more closely."]]},{id:"the-selfish-gene",title:"The Selfish Gene",author:"Richard Dawkins",kind:"book",year:1976,description:"A gene's-eye view of evolution that changed how we think about natural selection.",ideas:[["Genes as the unit of selection","Richard Dawkins proposed looking at evolution from the gene's point of view. Bodies, he suggested, are like vehicles built by genes that have been good at copying themselves through generations. The genes that survive are the ones that build bodies and behaviours that help them get passed on. It's a startling shift in perspective that explains many puzzling features of life. Today: think of one instinct you have, like fear of heights or love of sweet food, and ask how it might once have helped your ancestors survive."],["Selfish genes can make kind animals","\"Selfish\" genes don't necessarily make selfish animals. Dawkins explains that genes encouraging an animal to help its relatives can spread, because those relatives share copies of the same genes. That's why birds warn their flocks of predators and parents sacrifice for their children. Cooperation and kindness can evolve from genetic self-interest. Today: notice one act of kindness you witness, and consider how deep the roots of cooperation run in living things."],["Memes","Dawkins coined the word \"meme\" for ideas, tunes, fashions and habits that spread from mind to mind, competing and evolving a bit like genes. A catchy song, a joke or a new word survives if people want to copy and share it. Long before internet memes, this was a way to think about how culture changes. Today: think about an idea or phrase that's spread through your friends recently, and ask why it caught on."],["Tit for tat","In experiments with repeated games, where players can cooperate or cheat, a simple strategy called tit for tat did remarkably well: cooperate on the first move, then copy whatever the other player did last time. It's nice, retaliatory, forgiving and clear. Dawkins uses this to show how cooperation can evolve even among self-interested players. Today: in a relationship where trust has been shaken, consider starting with a cooperative move, and responding fairly to what comes back."]]},{id:"the-gene",title:"The Gene",author:"Siddhartha Mukherjee",kind:"book",year:2016,description:"The story of the gene, from Mendel's peas to gene editing.",ideas:[["Mendel's peas","In the 1860s, an Augustinian friar named Gregor Mendel spent years crossing pea plants in his monastery garden, carefully counting traits like colour and shape. He discovered that traits pass down in separate units that follow predictable patterns, what we now call genes. His work was ignored for decades, then rediscovered and recognised as the foundation of genetics. Siddhartha Mukherjee tells his story with affection. Today: remember that patient, unglamorous work can change the world, even if no one notices at first."],["DNA's double helix","In 1953, scientists revealed the structure of DNA: two strands twisted around each other in a double helix, with chemical letters pairing up, A with T and C with G. The beauty of the structure explained how life copies itself: each strand serves as a template to rebuild the other. Mukherjee describes how this discovery opened the door to modern biology. Today: look at an image of the double helix and marvel that this tiny spiral holds the instructions for building you."],["Genes are recipes, not destinies","Most human traits, like height, personality and risk of disease, come from many genes interacting with each other, with the environment and with chance. Having a particular gene variant usually changes probabilities, not certainties. Mukherjee warns against thinking of genes as a fixed destiny. Today: if you've ever thought, \"It's in my genes, so there's nothing I can do\", ask what environmental choices might still make a difference."],["The power and danger of editing","Tools like CRISPR now let scientists edit DNA with astonishing precision, like using a word processor on the genetic code. This could cure terrible inherited diseases. It also raises deep ethical questions: should we change the genes of future generations, and who decides which traits are \"better\"? Mukherjee encourages careful, informed public debate. Today: read one article about gene editing and think about where you'd draw the line."]]},{id:"sapiens",title:"Sapiens",author:"Yuval Noah Harari",kind:"book",year:2011,description:"A brief history of humankind, from foragers to the scientific revolution.",ideas:[["The cognitive revolution","Around seventy thousand years ago, Yuval Noah Harari argues, something changed in how humans thought and spoke. We began talking about things that don't physically exist: spirits, legends and future plans. This cognitive revolution let large groups of strangers cooperate around shared stories, something no other species can do. Today: notice how many of your daily decisions depend on stories shared with others, like laws, brands, nations and money."],["Shared fictions","Nations, companies, human rights and money don't exist in the physical world the way rivers and trees do. They exist because millions of people believe in them together. Harari calls them shared fictions or imagined orders, and argues they're humanity's superpower, allowing cooperation on a massive scale. That doesn't make them fake or unimportant; it makes them fragile and powerful. Today: think about one institution you rely on, and how much it depends on people continuing to believe in it."],["The agricultural trap","Farming allowed far more people to live, but Harari suggests that for many individuals it meant harder work, a narrower diet and more disease than the foraging life before it. He jokes that wheat domesticated humans rather than the other way round. Progress for a species isn't always progress for each person. Today: think about one modern convenience that made life easier in some ways and harder in others."],["Money is trust","Why would anyone accept a piece of paper in exchange for food or hours of work? Because they trust that others will accept it too. Harari calls money the most universal system of mutual trust ever invented. It lets strangers from different cultures, religions and languages cooperate. Today: next time you pay for something, notice the invisible web of trust that makes the exchange possible."],["The scientific revolution began with ignorance","For most of history, Harari notes, people believed that everything important was already known through tradition or religion. The scientific revolution began when people started admitting their ignorance, saying \"we don't know\", and set out to find answers through observation and experiment. Admitting ignorance turned out to be a source of tremendous power. Today: say \"I don't know\" about something, and then look it up properly."]]},{id:"the-structure-of-scientific-revolutions",title:"The Structure of Scientific Revolutions",author:"Thomas S. Kuhn",kind:"book",year:1962,description:"How science really changes: not steadily, but through revolutionary shifts in worldview.",ideas:[["Paradigms","Thomas Kuhn argued that scientists don't just collect facts; they work inside a shared framework of assumptions, methods and examples. He called this a paradigm. For centuries, astronomers worked within the paradigm of an Earth-centred universe; later, within one where the Earth orbits the Sun. Paradigms shape what questions seem worth asking. Today: think about an assumption in your own field or life that everyone takes for granted, and ask what would change if it were wrong."],["Normal science","Most scientific work, Kuhn said, is normal science: solving puzzles within the existing paradigm, filling in details and refining measurements. It isn't glamorous, but it's essential, and it's usually what scientists spend their careers doing. Revolutions happen rarely. Today: appreciate the steady, detailed work in your own life or job that rarely gets attention but keeps things running."],["Anomalies pile up","Over time, results that don't fit the paradigm, called anomalies, start to pile up. At first they're ignored or explained away. Eventually there are too many to dismiss, and the field enters a period of crisis where scientists question their foundations. Kuhn's insight applies far beyond science. Today: notice whether there's a small inconsistency in your plans or beliefs that you've been brushing aside, and give it a closer look."],["Paradigm shifts","When a crisis deepens, a new framework can replace the old one, as when Copernicus moved the Sun to the centre of the solar system, or Einstein reshaped our understanding of time and space. Kuhn called this a paradigm shift. Afterwards, scientists literally see the world differently, and the old view can seem hard to imagine. Today: think of a time your own view of something changed completely. What made the shift possible?"]]},{id:"little-brother",title:"Little Brother",author:"Cory Doctorow",kind:"book",year:2008,description:"A teenage hacker fights back after a terror attack turns San Francisco into a surveillance state. A novel about privacy, technology and civil liberties.",free:{url:"https://craphound.com/littlebrother/download/",license:"CC BY-NC-SA 3.0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/",note:"Released by the author under a Creative Commons license, so the full novel is included."},ideas:[["Surveillance changes behaviour","In Cory Doctorow's novel, after a terror attack, San Francisco is covered in cameras, tracking and checkpoints. The hero discovers that constant monitoring doesn't only catch wrongdoers; it makes ordinary people afraid to act normally, to protest or even to joke. Being watched all the time shrinks everyone's freedom, guilty or not. Today: notice how you might behave differently if you knew you were being recorded, and what that tells you about the value of privacy."],["Security theatre versus real security","The novel shows security measures that look tough, like long queues, invasive searches and impressive gadgets, but barely make anyone safer, while inconveniencing millions. Doctorow calls this security theatre. Real security is often quieter: good design, smart policies and trust. The book asks readers to judge safety measures by their results, not by how dramatic they look. Today: think of a rule or measure you encounter regularly and ask whether it actually prevents the problem it claims to."],["The false-positive problem","Imagine a test for a rare disease that's 99 percent accurate. Use it on a million people, of whom only a hundred actually have the disease, and it will flag about ten thousand innocent people as sick. Doctorow's hero uses this false-positive paradox to show why mass surveillance overwhelms investigators with false alarms. Today: when you hear about screening everyone for something rare, remember that accuracy alone doesn't tell the whole story."],["Privacy is not about hiding wrongdoing","\"If you've got nothing to hide, you've got nothing to fear\" is a common argument against privacy. The novel answers it: everyone does private things that aren't wrong, like using the bathroom, writing in a diary or talking to a doctor. Privacy protects the space where people think, explore and grow without an audience. Today: think of something private in your life that isn't wrong, and consider how you'd feel if it were public."],["Understand the tools to stay free","The main character survives and fights back because he understands how the technology around him works: encryption, networks and their weak points. Doctorow suggests that technical literacy is a form of civic power in the modern world. You don't need to be a hacker, but understanding the basics makes you harder to fool and easier to protect. Today: learn one small thing about how your phone or apps handle your data, like checking which apps can see your location."]]},{id:"down-and-out",title:"Down and Out in the Magic Kingdom",author:"Cory Doctorow",kind:"book",year:2003,description:"In a post-scarcity future where death is cured and money is replaced by reputation, a man fights to keep his favourite Disney World ride from being changed.",free:{url:"https://craphound.com/down/download/",license:"CC BY-NC-SA 1.0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/1.0/",note:"Released by the author under a Creative Commons license, so the full novel is included."},ideas:[["Reputation as currency","Doctorow's first novel imagines a future where money has been replaced by \"Whuffie\", a live score of how much other people respect you, visible to everyone. At first it seems like a fair system: be good, and you'll be rewarded. But the book shows how a reputation economy can be just as unequal and anxious as a cash one, with people performing for approval. Today: notice how much social media likes and follower counts already work a bit like Whuffie in your own life."],["Abundance doesn't end conflict","In the novel's future, food, health and housing are essentially free and unlimited. Yet people still compete, fiercely, for attention, status and control over the things they care about, like who runs a beloved Disney World ride. Abundance doesn't end conflict; it changes what people fight over. Today: think about what you'd still strive for if all your basic needs were met forever."],["Immortality changes ambition","In Doctorow's future, death is optional: your mind can be backed up and restored into a new body. The result is that some characters lose urgency, drift for decades and postpone everything. The story asks whether limits, including mortality, are part of what gives our choices meaning. Today: think of one thing you've been postponing \"until later\", and consider doing a small part of it now."],["Free distribution as strategy","Doctorow released this book free online under a Creative Commons license, at the same time as the printed edition. He argued that for most creators, obscurity, not piracy, is the bigger threat: giving work away helps it find readers, and some of them buy copies or support the author. His experiment worked. Today: if you create something, consider sharing a part of it freely to help it find the people who'll love it."]]}]}},899,[]);
__d(function(e,i,s,a,t,o,n){t.exports={"the-lean-startup":7104760,"zero-to-one":9002334,"good-to-great":7431270,"the-innovators-dilemma":9274687,"the-hard-thing":7279515,rework:6679955,"so-good-they-cant-ignore-you":9215292,range:8782615,"designing-your-life":8760239,"the-7-habits":10079937,grit:7438753,"how-to-win-friends":13314878,"crucial-conversations":1711809,"nonviolent-communication":940686,"never-split-the-difference":8365942,"talk-like-ted":7316010,"steal-like-an-artist":7244512,"war-of-art":288439,"big-magic":7434843,"creativity-inc":13124793,"the-artists-way":10840008,"show-your-work":7284188,"why-we-sleep":8814155,outlive:13191259,"in-defense-of-food":2960867,breath:10096454,"the-body-keeps-the-score":8315367,"guns-germs-and-steel":7884018,"the-histories":6909537,"the-wright-brothers":7317364,"team-of-rivals":9286949,"the-silk-roads":8963642,"start-with-why":6395237,"extreme-ownership":12835042,"leaders-eat-last":8246311,"dare-to-lead":10304768,"the-five-dysfunctions":549590,"the-art-of-war":4849549,"zen-mind":7025011,"power-of-now":551262,"wherever-you-go":749813,"the-miracle-of-mindfulness":8262944,"radical-acceptance":7020283,babylon:10491331,"psychology-of-money":10389354,"rich-dad-poor-dad":8315603,"your-money-or-your-life":6975229,"little-book-common-sense-investing":1239500,"i-will-teach-you-to-be-rich":6305971,enchiridion:12622080,meditations:211529,"letters-from-a-stoic":103759,"nicomachean-ethics":12593945,"tao-te-ching":662232,"the-republic":14377522,gtd:109288,"eat-that-frog":847534,"deep-work":7988607,essentialism:7285986,"make-time":14179816,"four-thousand-weeks":11990973,"the-one-thing":10351762,"atomic-habits":12539702,"thinking-fast-slow":13290711,influence:431011,mindset:746414,"mans-search-for-meaning":11203708,"predictably-irrational":2314080,"the-five-love-languages":14370772,attached:8620497,"seven-principles-marriage":390037,"the-art-of-loving":4741809,"the-good-life":15196837,"a-brief-history-of-time":14589690,cosmos:8283901,"the-selfish-gene":133936,"the-gene":11320163,sapiens:8634250,"the-structure-of-scientific-revolutions":6566386,"leading-change":668100,"classic:meditations":211529,"classic:enchiridion":12622080,"classic:art-of-war":4849549,"classic:tao-te-ching":662232,"classic:republic":14377522,"classic:nicomachean-ethics":12593945,"classic:analects":7080846,"classic:discourse-on-method":662305,"classic:the-prince":12726168,"classic:zarathustra":12467848,"classic:problems-of-philosophy":6947403,"classic:on-liberty":966821,"classic:walden":11248037,"classic:emerson-essays":6593538,"classic:franklin-autobiography":5647361,"classic:art-of-money-getting":756095,"classic:as-a-man-thinketh":6268048,"classic:24-hours-a-day":1760143,"classic:common-sense":5921030,"classic:the-prophet":418324,"classic:gitanjali":8246100,"classic:dickinson-poems":5568135,"classic:pride-and-prejudice":14348537,"classic:frankenstein":12356249,"classic:alice-in-wonderland":10527843,"shape-up":12600273,"getting-real":6742980,"pro-git":7892827,"think-python":7363360,"free-culture":872235,"almanack-of-naval":10449931,"openstax-intro-philosophy":15230452,"little-brother":5728633,"down-and-out":524060,"classic:little-brother":5728633,"classic:down-and-out":524060,"classic:seneca-dialogues":2327812}},900,[]);
__d(function(e,t,o,i,s,a,n){s.exports=[{topic:"science",title:"Light from the Sun is eight minutes old",body:"Sunlight takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to travel the roughly 150 million kilometres to Earth. If the Sun vanished, we wouldn't know for over eight minutes."},{topic:"science",title:"Octopuses have three hearts",body:"Two hearts pump blood through the gills and a third pumps it to the rest of the body. Their blood is blue because it uses a copper-based molecule, hemocyanin, to carry oxygen."},{topic:"science",title:"Venus spins backwards",body:"Venus rotates in the opposite direction to most planets, and so slowly that a day on Venus (one full rotation) lasts longer than its year."},{topic:"science",title:"Water expands when it freezes",body:"Most substances shrink as they solidify, but water expands by about 9%. That's why ice floats, which keeps lakes from freezing solid and lets life survive beneath."},{topic:"science",title:"You share DNA with bananas",body:"Many basic genes that run cell life are shared across living things. A large share of human genes have recognizable counterparts in plants like bananas, a sign of common ancestry."},{topic:"science",title:"Honey doesn't spoil",body:"Honey is low in water and slightly acidic, and bees add an enzyme that produces small amounts of hydrogen peroxide. Sealed properly, it can stay edible for an extremely long time."},{topic:"science",title:"Atoms are mostly empty space",body:"If an atom's nucleus were the size of a marble in the middle of a football stadium, the electrons would be zipping around the outer stands. Nearly all of an atom's mass sits in that tiny nucleus."},{topic:"science",title:"Trees talk through fungi",body:"Underground fungal networks connect the roots of many trees. Through them, trees can exchange water, sugars and chemical signals, sometimes nicknamed the \"wood wide web\"."},{topic:"science",title:"A teaspoon of neutron star",body:"Neutron stars are the collapsed cores of giant stars. Their matter is so dense that a teaspoon of it would weigh billions of tonnes on Earth."},{topic:"science",title:"Why the sky is blue",body:"Air molecules scatter short blue wavelengths of sunlight much more than long red ones. That scattered blue light reaches your eyes from every direction, so the whole sky looks blue."},{topic:"science",title:"Sharks are older than trees",body:"The earliest sharks appeared around 400 million years ago, while the first true trees appeared a little later, around 385 million years ago."},{topic:"science",title:"The Moon is drifting away",body:"Tidal interactions slowly push the Moon outward by about 3.8 centimetres a year, roughly the speed your fingernails grow. Earth's day is lengthening very slightly as a result."},{topic:"history",title:"Cleopatra lived closer to the Moon landing than to the pyramids",body:"The Great Pyramid of Giza was built around 2560 BC. Cleopatra lived around 30 BC, about 2,500 years later, and less than 2,000 years before Apollo 11."},{topic:"history",title:"Oxford University is older than the Aztec Empire",body:"Teaching at Oxford existed by 1096. The Aztec capital Tenochtitlan was founded in 1325, more than two centuries later."},{topic:"history",title:"The shortest war in history",body:"The Anglo-Zanzibar War of 1896 lasted somewhere between 38 and 45 minutes before the Sultan's forces surrendered."},{topic:"history",title:"The printing press changed everything",body:"Around 1440, Johannes Gutenberg developed a movable-type printing press in Europe. Books became far cheaper, literacy spread, and ideas could travel faster than ever."},{topic:"history",title:"Ancient Romans used concrete that heals itself",body:"Roman concrete contained lime fragments that react with water seeping into cracks, forming new minerals that seal them. Some Roman harbours have survived two thousand years of seawater."},{topic:"history",title:"The Great Library wasn't burned in one day",body:"The Library of Alexandria declined over centuries through budget cuts, political purges and several fires, rather than disappearing in a single dramatic blaze."},{topic:"history",title:"Paper money started in China",body:"During the Song dynasty, around the 11th century, China began issuing government paper money, centuries before it became common in Europe."},{topic:"history",title:"Napoleon wasn't especially short",body:"Napoleon was about 1.69 m, average for a Frenchman of his time. The myth came partly from confusion between French and English inches and from British cartoons."},{topic:"history",title:"The Berlin Wall fell by accident",body:"On 9 November 1989, an East German spokesman mistakenly announced that new travel rules took effect immediately. Crowds rushed the checkpoints, and guards eventually opened the gates."},{topic:"history",title:"Vikings reached North America first",body:"Around the year 1000, Norse explorers led by Leif Erikson reached Newfoundland. Archaeologists found their settlement at L'Anse aux Meadows, nearly 500 years before Columbus."},{topic:"health",title:"Your body replaces its cells constantly",body:"Different cells renew at different speeds: some gut lining cells last only days, skin cells a few weeks, red blood cells about four months, while many neurons last a lifetime."},{topic:"health",title:"The gut has its own nervous system",body:"The enteric nervous system, with hundreds of millions of neurons, lines the digestive tract. It's sometimes called the \"second brain\" because it can work semi-independently."},{topic:"health",title:"Muscle protects you as you age",body:"After about age 30, people tend to lose muscle mass gradually. Strength training is one of the best ways to slow that loss and stay independent later in life."},{topic:"health",title:"Daylight sets your body clock",body:"Bright light in the morning helps reset your internal 24-hour rhythm, making it easier to feel alert in the day and sleepy at night."},{topic:"health",title:"Dehydration hits focus quickly",body:"Even mild dehydration, around 1\u20132% of body weight, has been linked to lower concentration and more fatigue. Thirst is a late signal, so sip regularly."},{topic:"health",title:"Laughing is a mini workout",body:"Laughter briefly raises heart rate and breathing, then leaves muscles relaxed. Shared laughter also strengthens social bonds."},{topic:"psychology",title:"The spotlight effect",body:"We overestimate how much others notice our appearance and mistakes. In studies, people wearing an embarrassing T-shirt guessed twice as many people noticed it as actually did."},{topic:"psychology",title:"The Zeigarnik effect",body:"Unfinished tasks stay on our minds more than finished ones. That's why cliffhangers are so effective and why writing down open loops helps you relax."},{topic:"psychology",title:"The Dunning\u2013Kruger effect",body:"People with low skill in an area often overestimate their ability, partly because the knowledge needed to perform well is the same knowledge needed to judge performance."},{topic:"psychology",title:"The mere exposure effect",body:"The more often we see something, the more we tend to like it, even without noticing. Familiarity breeds fondness, which is why ads repeat."},{topic:"psychology",title:"The Pygmalion effect",body:"When teachers were led to expect certain students to bloom, those students often performed better. Expectations can shape performance."},{topic:"psychology",title:"Choice overload",body:"In a famous jam-tasting study, shoppers offered 24 flavours were less likely to buy than those offered 6. Too many options can paralyse decisions."},{topic:"psychology",title:"Your brain is a prediction machine",body:"The brain constantly guesses what will happen next and compares the guess with what the senses report. Surprises, the prediction errors, are what grab your attention."},{topic:"money",title:"The rule of 72",body:"Divide 72 by an annual interest rate to estimate how many years it takes money to double. At 6% a year, it doubles in about 12 years."},{topic:"money",title:"Inflation silently shrinks cash",body:"At 3% inflation, prices roughly double in 24 years. Money left in a no-interest account loses about half its buying power over that time."},{topic:"money",title:"The first coins came from Lydia",body:"The kingdom of Lydia, in today's Turkey, minted some of the first known coins around the 7th century BC from electrum, a natural mix of gold and silver."},{topic:"money",title:"Most money is digital",body:"In modern economies, physical cash makes up only a small fraction of the money supply. Most money exists as bank account balances created through lending."},{topic:"money",title:"Lifestyle creep",body:"As income rises, spending tends to rise with it, so people earning more can feel just as stretched. Saving a fixed share of each raise prevents this."},{topic:"productivity",title:"The Pomodoro Technique",body:"Developed by Francesco Cirillo in the late 1980s, it uses 25-minute work sprints followed by 5-minute breaks. The name comes from his tomato-shaped kitchen timer."},{topic:"productivity",title:"The Eisenhower matrix",body:"Sort tasks by urgent and important into four boxes: do now, schedule, delegate, delete. It's named after U.S. President Dwight Eisenhower's approach to priorities."},{topic:"productivity",title:"Decision fatigue",body:"Making many decisions in a row can wear down the quality of later choices. Routines, like a fixed breakfast or outfit, save mental energy for decisions that matter."},{topic:"productivity",title:"Context switching has a cost",body:"Research on task switching shows that jumping between tasks slows you down and increases errors, because your brain has to reload the rules for each task."},{topic:"creativity",title:"The incubation effect",body:"Stepping away from a problem, especially to do something undemanding like showering or walking, often helps the solution pop up later."},{topic:"creativity",title:"Constraints boost creativity",body:"Dr. Seuss wrote Green Eggs and Ham on a bet that he couldn't write a book using only 50 different words. Limits force unusual solutions."},{topic:"creativity",title:"Edison's many failures",body:"Thomas Edison's lab tested thousands of materials for the light bulb filament. Breakthroughs often come from systematic trial and error, not flashes of genius."},{topic:"communication",title:"Listening beats talking",body:"People tend to like those who ask follow-up questions. In studies of conversations, asking more questions, especially follow-ups, made people more likeable."},{topic:"communication",title:"The 7-38-55 myth",body:"The claim that words carry only 7% of meaning comes from narrow experiments about inconsistent messages of feeling. It doesn't apply to everyday conversation."},{topic:"communication",title:"There are about 7,000 languages",body:"Roughly 7,000 languages are spoken today, but about 40% are endangered, many with fewer than a thousand speakers."},{topic:"relationships",title:"Dunbar's number",body:"Anthropologist Robin Dunbar suggested humans can maintain about 150 stable relationships, with smaller inner circles of around 5 close and 15 good friends."},{topic:"relationships",title:"Friendship takes time",body:"One study estimated it takes around 50 hours together to go from acquaintance to casual friend, and around 200 hours to become close friends."},{topic:"relationships",title:"Hugs release oxytocin",body:"Warm physical contact like hugging triggers the release of oxytocin, a hormone linked to bonding, trust and lower stress."},{topic:"mindfulness",title:"Your mind wanders half the time",body:"A large smartphone study found people's minds were wandering nearly 47% of their waking hours, and they tended to be less happy when it did."},{topic:"mindfulness",title:"Box breathing",body:"Inhale for four seconds, hold for four, exhale for four, hold for four. This simple pattern is used by athletes and soldiers to calm down under pressure."},{topic:"mindfulness",title:"Nature lowers stress",body:"Spending around two hours a week in nature, in one go or spread out, has been associated with better health and wellbeing in large surveys."},{topic:"business",title:"The first web page",body:"Tim Berners-Lee published the first website at CERN in 1991. It explained what the World Wide Web was and how to use it."},{topic:"business",title:"Network effects",body:"Some products become more valuable as more people use them, like phones or social networks. This creates winner-take-most markets."},{topic:"business",title:"Most famous startups pivoted",body:"Many well-known companies started as something else: Slack began as a game company, and YouTube was briefly a video dating site."},{topic:"leadership",title:"Psychological safety",body:"Google's Project Aristotle found that the most important factor in effective teams was psychological safety: members felt safe to take risks and speak up."},{topic:"leadership",title:"Two-pizza teams",body:"Amazon popularized the idea that a team should be small enough to be fed by two pizzas. Smaller teams communicate faster and own their work."},{topic:"career",title:"The 10,000-hour rule is a simplification",body:"The research behind it showed elite performers had practised a lot, but the number varies widely by field. Quality of practice matters as much as quantity."},{topic:"career",title:"Weak ties find jobs",body:"Sociologist Mark Granovetter found people often get jobs through acquaintances rather than close friends, because acquaintances move in different circles."},{topic:"philosophy",title:"The ship of Theseus",body:"If every plank of a ship is replaced over time, is it still the same ship? This ancient puzzle asks what makes something keep its identity through change."},{topic:"philosophy",title:"Occam's razor",body:"When two explanations fit the facts equally well, prefer the one with fewer assumptions. It's named after the 14th-century friar William of Ockham."},{topic:"philosophy",title:"The trolley problem",body:"Would you pull a lever to divert a runaway trolley so it kills one person instead of five? The puzzle explores how we weigh outcomes against actions."}]},901,[]);
__d(function(e,n,t,o,a,r,s){a.exports=[{title:"\"Hope\" is the thing with feathers",author:"Emily Dickinson",year:1891,text:"\"Hope\" is the thing with feathers \u2013\nThat perches in the soul \u2013\nAnd sings the tune without the words \u2013\nAnd never stops \u2013 at all \u2013\n\nAnd sweetest \u2013 in the Gale \u2013 is heard \u2013\nAnd sore must be the storm \u2013\nThat could abash the little Bird\nThat kept so many warm \u2013\n\nI've heard it in the chillest land \u2013\nAnd on the strangest Sea \u2013\nYet \u2013 never \u2013 in Extremity,\nIt asked a crumb \u2013 of me."},{title:"Because I could not stop for Death",author:"Emily Dickinson",year:1890,text:"Because I could not stop for Death \u2013\nHe kindly stopped for me \u2013\nThe Carriage held but just Ourselves \u2013\nAnd Immortality.\n\nWe slowly drove \u2013 He knew no haste\nAnd I had put away\nMy labor and my leisure too,\nFor His Civility \u2013\n\nWe passed the School, where Children strove\nAt Recess \u2013 in the Ring \u2013\nWe passed the Fields of Gazing Grain \u2013\nWe passed the Setting Sun \u2013\n\nOr rather \u2013 He passed Us \u2013\nThe Dews drew quivering and Chill \u2013\nFor only Gossamer, my Gown \u2013\nMy Tippet \u2013 only Tulle \u2013\n\nWe paused before a House that seemed\nA Swelling of the Ground \u2013\nThe Roof was scarcely visible \u2013\nThe Cornice \u2013 in the Ground \u2013\n\nSince then \u2013 'tis Centuries \u2013 and yet\nFeels shorter than the Day\nI first surmised the Horses' Heads\nWere toward Eternity \u2013"},{title:"I'm Nobody! Who are you?",author:"Emily Dickinson",year:1891,text:"I'm Nobody! Who are you?\nAre you \u2013 Nobody \u2013 too?\nThen there's a pair of us!\nDon't tell! they'd advertise \u2013 you know!\n\nHow dreary \u2013 to be \u2013 Somebody!\nHow public \u2013 like a Frog \u2013\nTo tell one's name \u2013 the livelong June \u2013\nTo an admiring Bog!"},{title:"Success is counted sweetest",author:"Emily Dickinson",year:1878,text:"Success is counted sweetest\nBy those who ne'er succeed.\nTo comprehend a nectar\nRequires sorest need.\n\nNot one of all the purple Host\nWho took the Flag today\nCan tell the definition\nSo clear of Victory\n\nAs he defeated \u2013 dying \u2013\nOn whose forbidden ear\nThe distant strains of triumph\nBurst agonized and clear!"},{title:"If I can stop one heart from breaking",author:"Emily Dickinson",year:1890,text:"If I can stop one heart from breaking,\nI shall not live in vain;\nIf I can ease one life the aching,\nOr cool one pain,\nOr help one fainting robin\nUnto his nest again,\nI shall not live in vain."},{title:"Sonnet 18",author:"William Shakespeare",year:1609,text:"Shall I compare thee to a summer's day?\nThou art more lovely and more temperate:\nRough winds do shake the darling buds of May,\nAnd summer's lease hath all too short a date;\nSometime too hot the eye of heaven shines,\nAnd often is his gold complexion dimm'd;\nAnd every fair from fair sometime declines,\nBy chance or nature's changing course untrimm'd;\nBut thy eternal summer shall not fade,\nNor lose possession of that fair thou ow'st;\nNor shall Death brag thou wander'st in his shade,\nWhen in eternal lines to time thou grow'st:\nSo long as men can breathe or eyes can see,\nSo long lives this, and this gives life to thee."},{title:"Sonnet 116",author:"William Shakespeare",year:1609,text:"Let me not to the marriage of true minds\nAdmit impediments. Love is not love\nWhich alters when it alteration finds,\nOr bends with the remover to remove.\nO no! it is an ever-fixed mark\nThat looks on tempests and is never shaken;\nIt is the star to every wandering bark,\nWhose worth's unknown, although his height be taken.\nLove's not Time's fool, though rosy lips and cheeks\nWithin his bending sickle's compass come;\nLove alters not with his brief hours and weeks,\nBut bears it out even to the edge of doom.\nIf this be error and upon me proved,\nI never writ, nor no man ever loved."},{title:"Sonnet 29",author:"William Shakespeare",year:1609,text:"When, in disgrace with fortune and men's eyes,\nI all alone beweep my outcast state,\nAnd trouble deaf heaven with my bootless cries,\nAnd look upon myself and curse my fate,\nWishing me like to one more rich in hope,\nFeatured like him, like him with friends possessed,\nDesiring this man's art and that man's scope,\nWith what I most enjoy contented least;\nYet in these thoughts myself almost despising,\nHaply I think on thee, and then my state,\n(Like to the lark at break of day arising\nFrom sullen earth) sings hymns at heaven's gate;\nFor thy sweet love remembered such wealth brings\nThat then I scorn to change my state with kings."},{title:"If\u2014",author:"Rudyard Kipling",year:1910,text:"If you can keep your head when all about you\n    Are losing theirs and blaming it on you,\nIf you can trust yourself when all men doubt you,\n    But make allowance for their doubting too;\nIf you can wait and not be tired by waiting,\n    Or being lied about, don't deal in lies,\nOr being hated, don't give way to hating,\n    And yet don't look too good, nor talk too wise:\n\nIf you can dream\u2014and not make dreams your master;\n    If you can think\u2014and not make thoughts your aim;\nIf you can meet with Triumph and Disaster\n    And treat those two impostors just the same;\nIf you can bear to hear the truth you've spoken\n    Twisted by knaves to make a trap for fools,\nOr watch the things you gave your life to, broken,\n    And stoop and build 'em up with worn-out tools:\n\nIf you can make one heap of all your winnings\n    And risk it on one turn of pitch-and-toss,\nAnd lose, and start again at your beginnings\n    And never breathe a word about your loss;\nIf you can force your heart and nerve and sinew\n    To serve your turn long after they are gone,\nAnd so hold on when there is nothing in you\n    Except the Will which says to them: 'Hold on!'\n\nIf you can talk with crowds and keep your virtue,\n    Or walk with Kings\u2014nor lose the common touch,\nIf neither foes nor loving friends can hurt you,\n    If all men count with you, but none too much;\nIf you can fill the unforgiving minute\n    With sixty seconds' worth of distance run,\nYours is the Earth and everything that's in it,\n    And\u2014which is more\u2014you'll be a Man, my son!"},{title:"The Road Not Taken",author:"Robert Frost",year:1916,text:"Two roads diverged in a yellow wood,\nAnd sorry I could not travel both\nAnd be one traveler, long I stood\nAnd looked down one as far as I could\nTo where it bent in the undergrowth;\n\nThen took the other, as just as fair,\nAnd having perhaps the better claim,\nBecause it was grassy and wanted wear;\nThough as for that the passing there\nHad worn them really about the same,\n\nAnd both that morning equally lay\nIn leaves no step had trodden black.\nOh, I kept the first for another day!\nYet knowing how way leads on to way,\nI doubted if I should ever come back.\n\nI shall be telling this with a sigh\nSomewhere ages and ages hence:\nTwo roads diverged in a wood, and I\u2014\nI took the one less traveled by,\nAnd that has made all the difference."},{title:"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening",author:"Robert Frost",year:1923,text:"Whose woods these are I think I know.\nHis house is in the village though;\nHe will not see me stopping here\nTo watch his woods fill up with snow.\n\nMy little horse must think it queer\nTo stop without a farmhouse near\nBetween the woods and frozen lake\nThe darkest evening of the year.\n\nHe gives his harness bells a shake\nTo ask if there is some mistake.\nThe only other sound's the sweep\nOf easy wind and downy flake.\n\nThe woods are lovely, dark and deep,\nBut I have promises to keep,\nAnd miles to go before I sleep,\nAnd miles to go before I sleep."},{title:"Nothing Gold Can Stay",author:"Robert Frost",year:1923,text:"Nature's first green is gold,\nHer hardest hue to hold.\nHer early leaf's a flower;\nBut only so an hour.\nThen leaf subsides to leaf.\nSo Eden sank to grief,\nSo dawn goes down to day.\nNothing gold can stay."},{title:"Fire and Ice",author:"Robert Frost",year:1920,text:"Some say the world will end in fire,\nSome say in ice.\nFrom what I've tasted of desire\nI hold with those who favor fire.\nBut if it had to perish twice,\nI think I know enough of hate\nTo say that for destruction ice\nIs also great\nAnd would suffice."},{title:"Ozymandias",author:"Percy Bysshe Shelley",year:1818,text:"I met a traveller from an antique land,\nWho said\u2014\"Two vast and trunkless legs of stone\nStand in the desert. . . . Near them, on the sand,\nHalf sunk a shattered visage lies, whose frown,\nAnd wrinkled lip, and sneer of cold command,\nTell that its sculptor well those passions read\nWhich yet survive, stamped on these lifeless things,\nThe hand that mocked them, and the heart that fed;\nAnd on the pedestal, these words appear:\nMy name is Ozymandias, King of Kings;\nLook on my Works, ye Mighty, and despair!\nNothing beside remains. Round the decay\nOf that colossal Wreck, boundless and bare\nThe lone and level sands stretch far away.\""},{title:"Invictus",author:"William Ernest Henley",year:1888,text:"Out of the night that covers me,\n    Black as the pit from pole to pole,\nI thank whatever gods may be\n    For my unconquerable soul.\n\nIn the fell clutch of circumstance\n    I have not winced nor cried aloud.\nUnder the bludgeonings of chance\n    My head is bloody, but unbowed.\n\nBeyond this place of wrath and tears\n    Looms but the Horror of the shade,\nAnd yet the menace of the years\n    Finds and shall find me unafraid.\n\nIt matters not how strait the gate,\n    How charged with punishments the scroll,\nI am the master of my fate,\n    I am the captain of my soul."},{title:"I Wandered Lonely as a Cloud",author:"William Wordsworth",year:1815,text:"I wandered lonely as a cloud\nThat floats on high o'er vales and hills,\nWhen all at once I saw a crowd,\nA host, of golden daffodils;\nBeside the lake, beneath the trees,\nFluttering and dancing in the breeze.\n\nContinuous as the stars that shine\nAnd twinkle on the milky way,\nThey stretched in never-ending line\nAlong the margin of a bay:\nTen thousand saw I at a glance,\nTossing their heads in sprightly dance.\n\nThe waves beside them danced; but they\nOut-did the sparkling waves in glee:\nA poet could not but be gay,\nIn such a jocund company:\nI gazed\u2014and gazed\u2014but little thought\nWhat wealth the show to me had brought:\n\nFor oft, when on my couch I lie\nIn vacant or in pensive mood,\nThey flash upon that inward eye\nWhich is the bliss of solitude;\nAnd then my heart with pleasure fills,\nAnd dances with the daffodils."},{title:"The World Is Too Much With Us",author:"William Wordsworth",year:1807,text:"The world is too much with us; late and soon,\nGetting and spending, we lay waste our powers;\u2014\nLittle we see in Nature that is ours;\nWe have given our hearts away, a sordid boon!\nThis Sea that bares her bosom to the moon;\nThe winds that will be howling at all hours,\nAnd are up-gathered now like sleeping flowers;\nFor this, for everything, we are out of tune;\nIt moves us not. Great God! I'd rather be\nA Pagan suckled in a creed outworn;\nSo might I, standing on this pleasant lea,\nHave glimpses that would make me less forlorn;\nHave sight of Proteus rising from the sea;\nOr hear old Triton blow his wreath\xe8d horn."},{title:"The Tyger",author:"William Blake",year:1794,text:"Tyger Tyger, burning bright,\nIn the forests of the night;\nWhat immortal hand or eye,\nCould frame thy fearful symmetry?\n\nIn what distant deeps or skies.\nBurnt the fire of thine eyes?\nOn what wings dare he aspire?\nWhat the hand, dare seize the fire?\n\nAnd what shoulder, & what art,\nCould twist the sinews of thy heart?\nAnd when thy heart began to beat,\nWhat dread hand? & what dread feet?\n\nWhat the hammer? what the chain,\nIn what furnace was thy brain?\nWhat the anvil? what dread grasp,\nDare its deadly terrors clasp!\n\nWhen the stars threw down their spears\nAnd water'd heaven with their tears:\nDid he smile his work to see?\nDid he who made the Lamb make thee?\n\nTyger Tyger burning bright,\nIn the forests of the night:\nWhat immortal hand or eye,\nDare frame thy fearful symmetry?"},{title:"O Captain! My Captain!",author:"Walt Whitman",year:1865,text:"O Captain! my Captain! our fearful trip is done,\nThe ship has weather'd every rack, the prize we sought is won,\nThe port is near, the bells I hear, the people all exulting,\nWhile follow eyes the steady keel, the vessel grim and daring;\n    But O heart! heart! heart!\n        O the bleeding drops of red,\n            Where on the deck my Captain lies,\n                Fallen cold and dead.\n\nO Captain! my Captain! rise up and hear the bells;\nRise up\u2014for you the flag is flung\u2014for you the bugle trills,\nFor you bouquets and ribbon'd wreaths\u2014for you the shores a-crowding,\nFor you they call, the swaying mass, their eager faces turning;\n    Here Captain! dear father!\n        This arm beneath your head!\n            It is some dream that on the deck,\n                You've fallen cold and dead.\n\nMy Captain does not answer, his lips are pale and still,\nMy father does not feel my arm, he has no pulse nor will,\nThe ship is anchor'd safe and sound, its voyage closed and done,\nFrom fearful trip the victor ship comes in with object won;\n    Exult O shores, and ring O bells!\n        But I with mournful tread,\n            Walk the deck my Captain lies,\n                Fallen cold and dead."},{title:"A Noiseless Patient Spider",author:"Walt Whitman",year:1868,text:"A noiseless patient spider,\nI mark'd where on a little promontory it stood isolated,\nMark'd how to explore the vacant vast surrounding,\nIt launch'd forth filament, filament, filament, out of itself,\nEver unreeling them, ever tirelessly speeding them.\n\nAnd you O my soul where you stand,\nSurrounded, detached, in measureless oceans of space,\nCeaselessly musing, venturing, throwing, seeking the spheres to connect them,\nTill the bridge you will need be form'd, till the ductile anchor hold,\nTill the gossamer thread you fling catch somewhere, O my soul."},{title:"Bright Star",author:"John Keats",year:1819,text:"Bright star, would I were stedfast as thou art\u2014\nNot in lone splendour hung aloft the night\nAnd watching, with eternal lids apart,\nLike nature's patient, sleepless Eremite,\nThe moving waters at their priestlike task\nOf pure ablution round earth's human shores,\nOr gazing on the new soft-fallen mask\nOf snow upon the mountains and the moors\u2014\nNo\u2014yet still stedfast, still unchangeable,\nPillow'd upon my fair love's ripening breast,\nTo feel for ever its soft fall and swell,\nAwake for ever in a sweet unrest,\nStill, still to hear her tender-taken breath,\nAnd so live ever\u2014or else swoon to death."},{title:"When I Have Fears That I May Cease to Be",author:"John Keats",year:1818,text:"When I have fears that I may cease to be\nBefore my pen has gleaned my teeming brain,\nBefore high-pil\xe8d books, in charactery,\nHold like rich garners the full ripened grain;\nWhen I behold, upon the night's starred face,\nHuge cloudy symbols of a high romance,\nAnd think that I may never live to trace\nTheir shadows with the magic hand of chance;\nAnd when I feel, fair creature of an hour,\nThat I shall never look upon thee more,\nNever have relish in the faery power\nOf unreflecting love\u2014then on the shore\nOf the wide world I stand alone, and think\nTill love and fame to nothingness do sink."},{title:"The Lake Isle of Innisfree",author:"W. B. Yeats",year:1890,text:"I will arise and go now, and go to Innisfree,\nAnd a small cabin build there, of clay and wattles made;\nNine bean-rows will I have there, a hive for the honey-bee,\nAnd live alone in the bee-loud glade.\n\nAnd I shall have some peace there, for peace comes dropping slow,\nDropping from the veils of the morning to where the cricket sings;\nThere midnight's all a glimmer, and noon a purple glow,\nAnd evening full of the linnet's wings.\n\nI will arise and go now, for always night and day\nI hear lake water lapping with low sounds by the shore;\nWhile I stand on the roadway, or on the pavements grey,\nI hear it in the deep heart's core."},{title:"When You Are Old",author:"W. B. Yeats",year:1892,text:"When you are old and grey and full of sleep,\nAnd nodding by the fire, take down this book,\nAnd slowly read, and dream of the soft look\nYour eyes had once, and of their shadows deep;\n\nHow many loved your moments of glad grace,\nAnd loved your beauty with love false or true,\nBut one man loved the pilgrim soul in you,\nAnd loved the sorrows of your changing face;\n\nAnd bending down beside the glowing bars,\nMurmur, a little sadly, how Love fled\nAnd paced upon the mountains overhead\nAnd hid his face amid a crowd of stars."},{title:"Where the Mind Is Without Fear",author:"Rabindranath Tagore",year:1912,text:"Where the mind is without fear and the head is held high;\nWhere knowledge is free;\nWhere the world has not been broken up into fragments by narrow domestic walls;\nWhere words come out from the depth of truth;\nWhere tireless striving stretches its arms towards perfection;\nWhere the clear stream of reason has not lost its way into the dreary desert sand of dead habit;\nWhere the mind is led forward by thee into ever-widening thought and action\u2014\nInto that heaven of freedom, my Father, let my country awake."},{title:"Remember",author:"Christina Rossetti",year:1862,text:"Remember me when I am gone away,\nGone far away into the silent land;\nWhen you can no more hold me by the hand,\nNor I half turn to go yet turning stay.\nRemember me when no more day by day\nYou tell me of our future that you planned:\nOnly remember me; you understand\nIt will be late to counsel then or pray.\nYet if you should forget me for a while\nAnd afterwards remember, do not grieve:\nFor if the darkness and corruption leave\nA vestige of the thoughts that once I had,\nBetter by far you should forget and smile\nThan that you should remember and be sad."},{title:"Crossing the Bar",author:"Alfred, Lord Tennyson",year:1889,text:"Sunset and evening star,\nAnd one clear call for me!\nAnd may there be no moaning of the bar,\nWhen I put out to sea,\n\nBut such a tide as moving seems asleep,\nToo full for sound and foam,\nWhen that which drew from out the boundless deep\nTurns again home.\n\nTwilight and evening bell,\nAnd after that the dark!\nAnd may there be no sadness of farewell,\nWhen I embark;\n\nFor tho' from out our bourne of Time and Place\nThe flood may bear me far,\nI hope to see my Pilot face to face\nWhen I have crost the bar."},{title:"The New Colossus",author:"Emma Lazarus",year:1883,text:"Not like the brazen giant of Greek fame,\nWith conquering limbs astride from land to land;\nHere at our sea-washed, sunset gates shall stand\nA mighty woman with a torch, whose flame\nIs the imprisoned lightning, and her name\nMother of Exiles. From her beacon-hand\nGlows world-wide welcome; her mild eyes command\nThe air-bridged harbor that twin cities frame.\n\"Keep, ancient lands, your storied pomp!\" cries she\nWith silent lips. \"Give me your tired, your poor,\nYour huddled masses yearning to breathe free,\nThe wretched refuse of your teeming shore.\nSend these, the homeless, tempest-tost to me,\nI lift my lamp beside the golden door!\""},{title:"There Will Come Soft Rains",author:"Sara Teasdale",year:1918,text:"There will come soft rains and the smell of the ground,\nAnd swallows circling with their shimmering sound;\n\nAnd frogs in the pools singing at night,\nAnd wild plum trees in tremulous white;\n\nRobins will wear their feathery fire,\nWhistling their whims on a low fence-wire;\n\nAnd not one will know of the war, not one\nWill care at last when it is done.\n\nNot one would mind, neither bird nor tree,\nIf mankind perished utterly;\n\nAnd Spring herself, when she woke at dawn\nWould scarcely know that we were gone."},{title:"Death, be not proud",author:"John Donne",year:1633,text:"Death, be not proud, though some have called thee\nMighty and dreadful, for thou art not so;\nFor those whom thou think'st thou dost overthrow\nDie not, poor Death, nor yet canst thou kill me.\nFrom rest and sleep, which but thy pictures be,\nMuch pleasure; then from thee much more must flow,\nAnd soonest our best men with thee do go,\nRest of their bones, and soul's delivery.\nThou art slave to fate, chance, kings, and desperate men,\nAnd dost with poison, war, and sickness dwell,\nAnd poppy or charms can make us sleep as well\nAnd better than thy stroke; why swell'st thou then?\nOne short sleep past, we wake eternally\nAnd death shall be no more; Death, thou shalt die."},{title:"A Red, Red Rose",author:"Robert Burns",year:1794,text:"O my Luve is like a red, red rose\nThat's newly sprung in June;\nO my Luve is like the melody\nThat's sweetly played in tune.\n\nSo fair art thou, my bonnie lass,\nSo deep in luve am I;\nAnd I will luve thee still, my dear,\nTill a' the seas gang dry.\n\nTill a' the seas gang dry, my dear,\nAnd the rocks melt wi' the sun;\nI will love thee still, my dear,\nWhile the sands o' life shall run.\n\nAnd fare thee weel, my only luve!\nAnd fare thee weel awhile!\nAnd I will come again, my luve,\nThough it were ten thousand mile."},{title:"On Children",author:"Kahlil Gibran",year:1923,text:"Your children are not your children.\nThey are the sons and daughters of Life's longing for itself.\nThey come through you but not from you,\nAnd though they are with you yet they belong not to you.\n\nYou may give them your love but not your thoughts,\nFor they have their own thoughts.\nYou may house their bodies but not their souls,\nFor their souls dwell in the house of tomorrow, which you cannot visit, not even in your dreams.\nYou may strive to be like them, but seek not to make them like you.\nFor life goes not backward nor tarries with yesterday.\n\nYou are the bows from which your children as living arrows are sent forth.\nThe archer sees the mark upon the path of the infinite, and He bends you with His might that His arrows may go swift and far.\nLet your bending in the archer's hand be for gladness;\nFor even as He loves the flight that flies, so He loves also the bow that is stable."}]},902,[]);
__d(function(t,e,o,i,a,n,r){a.exports=[{topic:"philosophy",author:"Socrates",cite:"Plato, Apology",text:"The unexamined life is not worth living."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Ren\xe9 Descartes",cite:"Discourse on the Method",text:"I think, therefore I am."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Heraclitus",text:"No man ever steps in the same river twice."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Epictetus",cite:"Enchiridion",text:"Men are disturbed not by things, but by the opinions about things."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Seneca",cite:"Letters to Lucilius",text:"We suffer more often in imagination than in reality."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Seneca",cite:"Letters to Lucilius",text:"While we are postponing, life speeds by."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Friedrich Nietzsche",cite:"Twilight of the Idols",text:"He who has a why to live can bear almost any how."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Friedrich Nietzsche",cite:"Twilight of the Idols",text:"What does not kill me makes me stronger."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Jean-Paul Sartre",cite:"No Exit",text:"Hell is other people."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Ludwig Wittgenstein",cite:"Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus",text:"Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Inscription at Delphi",text:"Know thyself."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Horace",cite:"Odes",text:"Carpe diem. (Seize the day.)"},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Henry David Thoreau",cite:"Walden",text:"The mass of men lead lives of quiet desperation."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"Henry David Thoreau",cite:"Walden",text:"I went to the woods because I wished to live deliberately, to front only the essential facts of life."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"John Donne",cite:"Devotions upon Emergent Occasions",text:"No man is an island, entire of itself."},{topic:"psychology",author:"Viktor E. Frankl",cite:"Man's Search for Meaning",text:"Everything can be taken from a man but one thing: the last of the human freedoms \u2014 to choose one's attitude in any given set of circumstances."},{topic:"psychology",author:"James Baldwin",text:"Not everything that is faced can be changed, but nothing can be changed until it is faced."},{topic:"psychology",author:"Ernest Hemingway",cite:"A Farewell to Arms",text:"The world breaks everyone and afterward many are strong at the broken places."},{topic:"psychology",author:"Alexander Pope",cite:"An Essay on Criticism",text:"To err is human; to forgive, divine."},{topic:"psychology",author:"Alexander Pope",cite:"An Essay on Man",text:"Hope springs eternal in the human breast."},{topic:"science",author:"Isaac Newton",cite:"Letter to Robert Hooke, 1675",text:"If I have seen further it is by standing on the shoulders of Giants."},{topic:"science",author:"Francis Bacon",text:"Knowledge is power."},{topic:"science",author:"Albert Einstein",text:"Imagination is more important than knowledge."},{topic:"science",author:"Albert Einstein",text:"The important thing is not to stop questioning."},{topic:"science",author:"Carl Sagan",text:"Extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence."},{topic:"science",author:"Theodosius Dobzhansky",text:"Nothing in biology makes sense except in the light of evolution."},{topic:"science",author:"Richard Feynman",text:"The first principle is that you must not fool yourself \u2014 and you are the easiest person to fool."},{topic:"science",author:"Richard Feynman",text:"What I cannot create, I do not understand."},{topic:"science",author:"Arthur C. Clarke",text:"Any sufficiently advanced technology is indistinguishable from magic."},{topic:"science",author:"Neil Armstrong",cite:"Apollo 11, 1969",text:"That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind."},{topic:"business",author:"Alan Kay",text:"The best way to predict the future is to invent it."},{topic:"business",author:"Donald Knuth",text:"Premature optimization is the root of all evil."},{topic:"business",author:"Linus Torvalds",text:"Talk is cheap. Show me the code."},{topic:"business",author:"Steve Jobs",cite:"Stanford commencement, 2005",text:"Stay hungry. Stay foolish."},{topic:"career",author:"Steve Jobs",cite:"Stanford commencement, 2005",text:"The only way to do great work is to love what you do."},{topic:"career",author:"Theodore Roosevelt",text:"Far and away the best prize that life offers is the chance to work hard at work worth doing."},{topic:"career",author:"Theodore Roosevelt",cite:"Citizenship in a Republic, 1910",text:"It is not the critic who counts; not the man who points out how the strong man stumbles. The credit belongs to the man who is actually in the arena."},{topic:"career",author:"Thomas Edison",text:"Genius is one percent inspiration and ninety-nine percent perspiration."},{topic:"career",author:"Wayne Gretzky",text:"You miss 100% of the shots you don't take."},{topic:"money",author:"Warren Buffett",text:"Price is what you pay. Value is what you get."},{topic:"money",author:"Warren Buffett",text:"Be fearful when others are greedy and greedy when others are fearful."},{topic:"money",author:"Warren Buffett",text:"Someone's sitting in the shade today because someone planted a tree a long time ago."},{topic:"money",author:"Benjamin Franklin",cite:"Advice to a Young Tradesman",text:"Remember that time is money."},{topic:"money",author:"Benjamin Franklin",cite:"Poor Richard's Almanack",text:"Beware of little expenses; a small leak will sink a great ship."},{topic:"money",author:"P. T. Barnum",cite:"The Art of Money Getting",text:"Money is in some respects like fire; it is a very excellent servant but a terrible master."},{topic:"productivity",author:"Benjamin Franklin",cite:"Poor Richard's Almanack",text:"Lost time is never found again."},{topic:"productivity",author:"Benjamin Franklin",cite:"Poor Richard's Almanack",text:"Well done is better than well said."},{topic:"productivity",author:"Peter Drucker",cite:"The Effective Executive",text:"Until we can manage time, we can manage nothing else."},{topic:"productivity",author:"Peter Drucker",text:"Efficiency is doing things right; effectiveness is doing the right things."},{topic:"productivity",author:"Lao Tzu",cite:"Tao Te Ching",text:"The journey of a thousand miles begins with a single step."},{topic:"leadership",author:"Peter Drucker",text:"Management is doing things right; leadership is doing the right things."},{topic:"leadership",author:"Sun Tzu",cite:"The Art of War",text:"The supreme art of war is to subdue the enemy without fighting."},{topic:"leadership",author:"Abraham Lincoln",cite:"House Divided speech, 1858",text:"A house divided against itself cannot stand."},{topic:"leadership",author:"Franklin D. Roosevelt",cite:"First inaugural address, 1933",text:"The only thing we have to fear is fear itself."},{topic:"leadership",author:"John F. Kennedy",cite:"Inaugural address, 1961",text:"Ask not what your country can do for you \u2014 ask what you can do for your country."},{topic:"leadership",author:"Nelson Mandela",text:"Education is the most powerful weapon which you can use to change the world."},{topic:"history",author:"Julius Caesar",text:"Veni, vidi, vici. (I came, I saw, I conquered.)"},{topic:"history",author:"George Santayana",cite:"The Life of Reason",text:"Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it."},{topic:"history",author:"Winston Churchill",cite:"House of Commons, 1940",text:"I have nothing to offer but blood, toil, tears and sweat."},{topic:"history",author:"Winston Churchill",cite:"House of Commons, 1940",text:"Never in the field of human conflict was so much owed by so many to so few."},{topic:"history",author:"Abraham Lincoln",cite:"Gettysburg Address, 1863",text:"Government of the people, by the people, for the people, shall not perish from the earth."},{topic:"history",author:"Martin Luther King Jr.",cite:"Letter from Birmingham Jail",text:"Injustice anywhere is a threat to justice everywhere."},{topic:"history",author:"Martin Luther King Jr.",text:"The arc of the moral universe is long, but it bends toward justice."},{topic:"relationships",author:"Martin Luther King Jr.",cite:"Strength to Love",text:"Darkness cannot drive out darkness; only light can do that. Hate cannot drive out hate; only love can do that."},{topic:"relationships",author:"William Shakespeare",cite:"A Midsummer Night's Dream",text:"The course of true love never did run smooth."},{topic:"relationships",author:"William Shakespeare",cite:"All's Well That Ends Well",text:"Love all, trust a few, do wrong to none."},{topic:"relationships",author:"Emily Bront\xeb",cite:"Wuthering Heights",text:"Whatever our souls are made of, his and mine are the same."},{topic:"relationships",author:"Elie Wiesel",text:"The opposite of love is not hate, it's indifference."},{topic:"relationships",author:"Ernest Hemingway",text:"The best way to find out if you can trust somebody is to trust them."},{topic:"communication",author:"Edward Bulwer-Lytton",cite:"Richelieu",text:"The pen is mightier than the sword."},{topic:"communication",author:"Maya Angelou",cite:"I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings",text:"There is no greater agony than bearing an untold story inside you."},{topic:"communication",author:"Mark Twain",text:"The report of my death was an exaggeration."},{topic:"creativity",author:"Steve Jobs",cite:"Wired, 1996",text:"Creativity is just connecting things."},{topic:"creativity",author:"Chuck Close",text:"Inspiration is for amateurs \u2014 the rest of us just show up and get to work."},{topic:"creativity",author:"Maya Angelou",text:"You can't use up creativity. The more you use, the more you have."},{topic:"creativity",author:"Vincent van Gogh",text:"I dream my painting and I paint my dream."},{topic:"creativity",author:"William Shakespeare",cite:"As You Like It",text:"All the world's a stage, and all the men and women merely players."},{topic:"creativity",author:"J. R. R. Tolkien",cite:"The Fellowship of the Ring",text:"Not all those who wander are lost."},{topic:"mindfulness",author:"Jon Kabat-Zinn",cite:"Wherever You Go, There You Are",text:"You can't stop the waves, but you can learn to surf."},{topic:"mindfulness",author:"Anne Lamott",text:"Almost everything will work again if you unplug it for a few minutes, including you."},{topic:"mindfulness",author:"Ram Dass",text:"Be here now."},{topic:"mindfulness",author:"William Shakespeare",cite:"Hamlet",text:"This above all: to thine own self be true."},{topic:"health",author:"Juvenal",cite:"Satires",text:"Mens sana in corpore sano. (A healthy mind in a healthy body.)"},{topic:"health",author:"Benjamin Franklin",cite:"Poor Richard's Almanack",text:"Early to bed and early to rise, makes a man healthy, wealthy, and wise."},{topic:"health",author:"Muhammad Ali",text:"Float like a butterfly, sting like a bee."},{topic:"history",author:"Charles Dickens",cite:"A Tale of Two Cities",text:"It was the best of times, it was the worst of times."},{topic:"relationships",author:"Leo Tolstoy",cite:"Anna Karenina",text:"Happy families are all alike; every unhappy family is unhappy in its own way."},{topic:"relationships",author:"Jane Austen",cite:"Pride and Prejudice",text:"It is a truth universally acknowledged, that a single man in possession of a good fortune, must be in want of a wife."},{topic:"philosophy",author:"William Shakespeare",cite:"Hamlet",text:"To be, or not to be: that is the question."},{topic:"leadership",author:"Virgil",cite:"Aeneid",text:"Fortune favours the bold."},{topic:"psychology",author:"Robert Frost",cite:"A Servant to Servants",text:"The best way out is always through."}]},903,[]);
